const DRAFTS: Record<"clone" | "off" | "rag", Draft[]> = {"clone": [{"id": "gold_001", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "So I recently did another SaaStr AI Deep Dive with one of my favorite CEOs, CEO of CloudSight,\nTim Bertrand\n.\nWe talked about so much here:\nWhat’s really happening in the current state of AI and enterprise software\nHow to think about “AI-first” vs. adding AI features after your core product is built\nWhy you can’t always tell whether a company will be successful or not by looking at its metrics (and what you should look for instead)\nThe one thing that matters most when building an SaaS business: creating and solving real problems\nBut the part of our conversation that really got me thinking was about how CloudSight is able to compete with companies like Salesforce.  And they’re winning big.\nAt 8 people!\nNow this isn’t new, but it’s still mind-blowing:\nIt doesn’t matter if your team is “enterprise-ready” or not\nWhat matters is whether you can create a social contract with CIOs and other executives at large companies that lets them trust you enough to try your product out.\nThat means understanding what these leaders value — which isn’t always the same as what consumers do.", "words": 189, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Can an 8 person startup sell to a cio yes\nIf you understand what I call the social contract here:\n1. No hidden fees, no surprises at all.\n2. Always be there for support and issues.\n3. Not too complex.\n4. More than just some \"feature\" that is cool but doesn't add much value.\n5. And most importantly ... it works in production today\nWe did this with EchoSign (now Adobe Sign) when we sold to a CIO of a $1B+ company for $100M.  We had 10 employees at the time, and our platform was already used by thousands of companies.\n\nAnd now more than ever there are so many tools you can use in production today that really do work well:\nSalesforce\nSlack\nZoom\nDropbox\nHubSpot\nMonday.com\nZapier\n\nJust pick one.  And then go sell it to a CIO.\n\nIt's not too late, and the social contract will be understood if you are honest, transparent, and great at support.", "words": 160, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "x", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Just closed our first big deal with a Fortune 500 CIO at SaaStr Annual.\n\nI'm going back on this one from 2016:\n\n\"Why an 8-Person Startup Can't Sell to the CIO\"\n\nIt's just wrong.\n\nThe reality is there are still some great start-ups that can sell to any CIO.", "words": 49, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Okay, so there’s something that I think folks in B2B and SaaS have forgotten over the years.\n\nIt turns out if you go back to the early days of salesforce.com, Hubspot, or almost any other company at all today … it was a few people on an email list. It was just a group of passionate customers who were willing to be guinea pigs for some new, untested product and service. And those folks in turn would often help others.\n\nToday we’ve lost that in many cases. We don’t see as much of this anymore. But it’s more important than ever.\n\nA CIO is someone else you can talk about with your customers today, but I just want to share a quick story from the other day at SaaStr Annual 2025. The CEO of a company called Foresight, which did an $8m acquisition by a $1b+ public company. And what’s interesting for us is he started it as his own startup. It was him and two others.\n\nSo, that means they went from three to eight or so today. That means 80% of them are still there. But let me tell you, this CIO who bought him in a $8m deal … said the following at SaaStr Annual: “This is the best team I’ve ever worked with.”\n\nThe CIO was not just saying that to be nice.\n\nAnd he also gave some very practical and tactical advice on how to sell to them. He’s got 1,000 engineers under him in this large organization.\n\nSo what does it take?\n\nA few things:\n\nFirst, you have to know who the “CIO” is at a company. It may not always be called that in today’s world of 10-20 different departments and C-level executives for each one. But there are always two or three people you need to talk to.\n\nThe first one is what we might call the internal CTO, but more like an IT manager.\n\nThey will want to do a free pilot with you before they buy your product.", "words": 338, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So we’ve had some great discussions on SaaStr here recently with Monday’s CEO Eran Zinman. One thing I thought was interesting was how they went more enterprise this year:\nMonday is going to be a lot bigger than it is now\nBut as much as possible, still remain an SMB & mid-market focused platform too.", "words": 55, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "Monday.com is one of my all-time favorite SaaS stories. I’ve known its co-founders since before it was public and back when they were struggling to get off the ground (they almost ran out of money).\nAnd today? It’s a $5B+ unicorn growing at an incredible rate.\nSo what can we learn from Monday.com in 2023?\nMonday is still hyper-growth.  Not slow growth, but hyper-growth.  Even at almost $6 Billion in valuation.\nIt has 230,000 customers and 2 million users total now.  That’s a lot of users per customer! But that also means it has to be very efficient.\nBut they are still growing 35% YoY or more at the same scale as when we first profiled them — $500m ARR in 2021\nThe NRR is insane. A staggering 120%.  That’s basically unheard of, and shows Monday.com customers buy far more than most.\nIt has an incredible 50/50 split between SMBs and Enterprise.  They literally have the same number of $100k+ deals as they do smaller ones.  Most SaaS companies get maybe half their revenue from larger accounts.\nThe bottom line?  A lot can happen in a short time at Monday.com — but its growth rate is still truly incredible today, even at almost $6B valuation.", "words": 205, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So Monday is probably one of my favorite SaaS apps I use every single day. It’s really a workhorse for me and many others to help manage everything from sales to marketing to HR and more.\n\nMonday has been around since 2012, so it’s well past the “early days” in SaaS today. But Monday.com is still growing at an incredible clip — over $500m ARR already!\n\nSo what can we learn?  A few things:\n\n1/5\nA bit of a look back on where Monday was when I first invested.\nIt has since grown to be one of the fastest-growing public cloud companies in history.", "words": 104, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "okay let's take monday we're talking monday we talked about them last week with jeff lamm and i wanted to just review some interesting learnings from them you can see their growth right here it looks like a sas unicorn but they are not the fastest growing at 30% but they did hit a billion dollars in ar on the way so pretty impressive now monday is not an adobe or a salesforce but if you take out these huge players that really dominate almost half of all enterprise software revenue monday does well it's a product built by people who want to use a workflow management tool and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all they have 130,000 customers now this is important because one thing monday did was they went from being a very expensive platform for marketing teams which they were in the early days to more affordable it's not cheap but you know as low as two or three thousand dollars a month and that really opened up their customer base and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all they have 130,000 customers now this is important because one thing monday did was they went from being a very expensive platform for marketing teams which they were in the early days to more affordable it's not cheap but you know as low as two or three thousand dollars a month and that really opened up their customer base and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all 130,000 customers is actually pretty good they're growing at about four percent per month so there are 50 new customers every day and this is what monday does well it's an intuitive product built by people who want to use a workflow management tool and if you go on their website which", "words": 317, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "so I'll let you know what one thing you can do this week to really stand out and make it easier for you to get that dream job just pick something but try to add some value somewhere and then the next time they talk to you don't tell them about that one thing and how amazing it was instead just tell them about how much better things are now because of that thing so in other words when they ask what's new or if there are any changes at your company and even more importantly at this interview when they meet with all the people they're interviewing for a job here you have to be able to answer those questions not by talking about some great initiative from three years ago but actually how things improved because of it so I know that is going to sound very straightforward I'm sure we've talked about this in other times on Saster and others but it's true if you can't make a difference somewhere at your company or elsewhere then maybe this job isn't the right fit for you maybe the next one will be", "words": 194, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "If you really want that job — stand out.\n\nA simple list. If you can’t do these things, maybe it’s time for a change:\n\nKnow your competition cold. No matter what role you’re interviewing for. Know them cold. They know the space better than you in this moment.\n\nBe honest about every bad decision you made at work. Not just excuses or blaming others. Be honest.\n\nFind 2-3 customers to meet with that will vouch for you, even if it’s not a customer they’ve worked with before. Even if it takes longer.\n\nKnow your job cold — the job description from this interview and how you can truly deliver on most of it today. Today. Not someday or maybe.\n\nBe honest about every bad decision you made at work.", "words": 129, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "x", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "how do you stand out so many folks that have been around for a while they've gone up the ladder and then back down are they great they're super smart but how do you really stand out in any job interview it's not rocket science here's what I'd tell you if you want to truly stand out be better than before come into an interview being 10% better as an SDR or better than ever before is the only thing that matters come into this interview and say I've got a million new prospects coming in next month my outbound is up 50%, pipeline's growing, leads are going up, whatever it was last time be 1% to 3.5% better every single time at least when you're doing outbound or inbound sales you just have to do that much more", "words": 139, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "So we've had so many great guests on SaaStr this year. But I wanted to do one more deep dive on something that's perhaps less obvious but super valuable. And it is:\nHow To Truly Stand Out In An Interview\nI'm going to share some of the top lessons and tips from our best interviews, from CROs and VPs of Sales all the way up to CEOs.\nAnd you can apply these tips not just when interviewing for a new job — but even if you're looking to get promoted internally. Because in many ways it's much harder than finding your first startup or SaaS job. So let's dive into our top takeaways on what makes an interview truly great, from the CROs and VPs of Sales all the way up to CEOs.\n1. Know Everything About The Company, Even Better Than Most Founders\nThis is probably one of my favorite learnings over the years. If you really know your stuff about a company — especially if it's early stage or growing fast, which in many cases will be true for any SaaS startup today that doesn't have 100+ million in revenue at least — then you can do so much better than most interviewers.\nYou can learn more here:\nHow to Research an Unusual Startup\nI did this with one of my favorite interviews with the CRO from HubSpot. I asked him a ton about how they sell, and he just got it all right, even some things that were almost wrong in the media. And then when I interviewed the CEO later on, I actually asked him to describe his ideal VP Sales candidate, and what he was looking for — in detail.\nHe had no idea. He basically only cared about one thing: did they have any experience with HubSpot? That's all that mattered.", "words": 307, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "So I’ve been lucky enough to do several deep dives with top SaaS CEOs on “SaaStr Deep Dives” over years:\n– HubSpot CEO Brian Halligan\n– Box CEO Aaron Levie\n– Slack CEO Stewart Butterfield\n– ZoomInfo CEO Henry Schuck\n– Asana CEO Dustin Moskovitz\n– MongoDB CEO Dev Ittycheria\n– PagerDuty CEO Jennifer Tejada\n– and many more!\nBut we don’t always do a Deep Dive with the CEOs of SaaS companies that are still growing. There’s just so much to learn from them! And when you’re doubling down, it’s not as easy.\nSo I asked Mark Roberge, CRO at HubSpot and now Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital — which is backing several top B2B companies in the $10m-$50m ARR range (think of them a lot like Bessemer Venture Partners) — to do one. And it was great! A Deep Dive on Doubling Down\nThe Reality:  It’s Harder and More Risky Than Ever Before.\nYou can’t just double down anymore these days in SaaS. Not when growth is so darn hard, especially once you get past $100m ARR or even earlier. The rules have changed since 2020. And the markets have changed too.\nBut if you want to go all-in on something that’s working — it still works. It always does. Just do your homework first. A Deep Dive with Mark Roberge, Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: What I Learned Selling My Company to Adobe Sign / EchoSign; How to Double Down in Tough Times\nNew SaaStr University FREE Course: \"How To Double Down with Your Co-Founder\"", "words": 273, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling down with Mark Roberge\nMark Roberge is one of my top five favorite SaaS founders. I met him at Dreamforce, where he was literally on the floor running a booth for HubSpot as it went from $1M to $100m in ARR. And then when we invested together in RevenueCat (now Vercel), Mark joined our board and helped me with a whole bunch of stuff that just didn’t make sense at first.\nHe’s now managing director at Stage 2 Capital, which I also co-founded. It invests from about $1m to about $10m in ARR. And he can help you if you are in the SaaS space — and even more importantly, have done a lot of it yourself already.\nMark is hands-down one of the most thoughtful founders I’ve ever had the pleasure to work with. He’s an ex-VP at HubSpot where he built the sales org that scaled from $1m ARR to $100m+ in ARR! And now, as managing director at Stage 2 Capital — Mark can help you scale it all again!\nIf you need a thoughtful partner on your journey from $10M-$50M+ ARR and have done some of this before … he’s got one of the best track records.", "words": 202, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "x", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "I first met Mark Roberge as a young VP of Sales at HubSpot. He was already doing so much back then but he had this incredible drive to help the sales team that I'd never seen before.\n\nAnd boy did it pay off, from $0 to $1B in ARR with 5+ years of explosive growth!\n\nFast forward to today and Mark is now a General Partner at Stage2 Capital where his focus remains on scaling high-growth B2B companies through strategic partnerships & investments.", "words": 83, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "So we did an epic AMA with co-founder of HubSpot. It was just great. And I asked him one question that got me thinking on what we could do for this session today. How does a founder know if they've doubled down too much? Because it's so hard to cut back once you have the vision. So Mark, thanks for joining us and sharing your thoughts! And let's get into some of the amazing insights from doubling down, as well as some other learnings in sales that we can take away.\n\nA little bit about HubSpot, and then I'll turn it over to you.\n\nMark, great to have you back on SaaStr. You're a co-founder of Hubspot with Brian Halligan. And Mark is the current Chief Operating Officer at HubSpot, where he oversees all aspects of go-to-market strategy. He's also an adjunct professor in entrepreneurship and innovation at Boston University's Questrom School for Business.\n\nAnd now you run Stage 2 Capital, so thanks for being here today to share your insights!\n\nSo let's start with doubling down. As a founder how do you know if you've doubled down too much? I think as founders we're all guilty of it because when you see something that works, and you have the vision, you just want to go further, right?\n\nBut what happens then is you start losing focus on things that are working well in your business. So how do you know if you've doubled down too much? I think we might be able to dig a little bit deeper into some of those insights here today.\n\nSo let's first talk about the importance of doubling down as a founder, and then maybe what happens when it becomes too late or not early enough.", "words": 293, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So I just got an email that’s quite interesting.  It was from a VP at a company called Informatica.\nNow you may know them or not — it depends on your experience.  They are a classic enterprise software vendor in the data integration space.  Founded way back in 1993, they’ve been around for almost 30 years!  That’s long enough to have seen just about everything change in SaaS and Cloud.\nThe thing is … Informatica has had two IPOs (2009 & 2015) — that rare feat indeed.  And now it’s being acquired by Salesforce for a whopping $8 Billion!\nAnd then I realized, this was the company my first CEO at EchoSign used to work at!  What a crazy ride.\nSo what can we learn from Informatica?  Well, here are 5 things:\n#1. Enterprise software is still very much alive\nYou may think it’s dead in SaaS and Cloud — but nope.  Salesforce has proved this for years with its $50 Billion+ market cap of CRM + Marketing Automation software.\nAnd now they’re going to do the same thing on the data integration side, by acquiring Informatica.\n#2. Your time frame just might be longer than you think\nIt’s been 32 years since Informatica was founded!  That’s a long way back in SaaS and Cloud terms.  But that also means it has seen everything change — from client-server to Web services to SaaS and now the cloud.\n#3. You may have an IPO, but then you’ll go private again\nThis is what happened with Informatica.  Both of their first two “IPOs” were actually M&A deals in disguise by different acquirers (Private Equity firms).  Not all M&A goes public.\n#4.", "words": 279, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So I was just on a call with\n@Informatica\nand it’s wild. They’ve been around since ‘91. That’s before Salesforce. Before Oracle in the Cloud. Even before HubSpot. And they were acquired for almost $9B, or 1x ARR! It is truly crazy times. Informatica had its first IPO way back in 1997 at just under a $2 Billion valuation, but it was basically a failed IPO as no one really knew what to do with the company and the stock traded flat from IPO day.\n\nFast forward, they did a second IPO for real in 2015 at $2.25B, which is about half of where they were acquired today. That’s a great run … but still not worth it if you want to stay independent.", "words": 124, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So this is a truly epic story. It’s not that I knew every detail myself. But it was all over SaaStr and LinkedIn and my Twitter for years.\nInformatica founded in 1993\nFirst IPO: 2001 at $2B valuation, just as dot-com crash hit\nDeclined to 50% of peak in 2010\nSecond IPO: 2015 at $4.9B valuation (down 45% from first)\nThen — they had one of the best SaaStr Annuals ever!  We’re so grateful for that!\nFast forward to today:\n$8 BILLION ACQUISITION BY SALESFORCE\nThat’s a 3,900x increase in valuation since their second IPO just six years ago.\nThe Wild Ride:", "words": 102, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "A Tale of Two IPOs and How You Can Learn From It. I've been lucky enough over the years at SaaStr to do three deep dives with one CEO -- that's a lot in my world! So it's time for another one.\n\nWe're going to talk about Informatica, which is now part of Salesforce after an $8 billion acquisition. But back in 1996, they were the first data integration vendor and went public at just under $2 Billion dollars with their initial IPO. That was a wild ride. They went from being the largest independent enterprise software company -- to near bankruptcy.\n\nAnd then somehow came back as a top leader in cloud security, acquired by Salesforce for an incredible 8 billion! And that's how we got here today.\n\nSo let me tell you about Informatica and what happened on this crazy journey:\n\nThe first IPO: $1.9 Billion (1996)\n\nInformatica’s story begins in the early ‘90s when founder Ashutosh Pandey, a Stanford Ph.D., saw an opportunity to make data integration easier. His company went public in 1996 with a valuation of nearly $2 billion.\n\nBy this point, enterprise software was still in its infancy and companies were struggling to manage their growing amounts of data. Informatica’s PowerCenter platform became the de facto standard for data integration, helping companies like GE and IBM centralize their databases.\n\nThe Rise (2000-2011)\n\nThe early 2000s saw an explosion in global IT spending as companies sought to automate business processes and improve efficiency.", "words": 248, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "The team at SaaStr continues to create incredible content for you all on our\nYouTube channel.\nHere are some of its top performers:\n#1: “SaaS is Dead? What the F*** Has Happened?” with Bessemer Capital (3.5M views)\nThis session from 2020 went viral and still gets massive engagement today, as it continues to be one of the most relevant discussions about what’s happening in SaaS right now.\n#2: “The Future of SaaS is Here” – How To Get Ahead with HubSpot\nWith over 1.8M views since its release just last year, this session from 2020 on what’s next for SaaS remains one of our most-watched videos to date.\n#3: “How to Grow Your B2B Business from $10m ARR to $100m” with Intercom (1.4M views)\nReleased in May 2020 and still going strong, this presentation by Intercom is a great resource for anyone looking to scale their business beyond the initial growth phase.\n#4: “The 7 Things You Need To Do To Go From Startup to Scaleup”\nWith over 2 million views since its release on September 17th, 2020 and more than 100k comments on LinkedIn, this video is not only an excellent resource but also a testament to the value of SaaStr’s content.\n#5: “The Best Way To Get More Customers” with David Skok\nWith over 1.4M views since its release in October 2020 and still going strong today, this session from Dave Skok is one of our most-watched videos on getting more customers for your business.\nThese numbers are impressive but also tell a story – they’re what works! What SaaStr has done that’s different from other content platforms is combine the best in live events with video and digital media.", "words": 282, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "We’ve now crossed 5000,000 views on our YouTube channel. And we’re celebrating with all\nour top sessions here\n:\nThe Top 10 Most Popular SaaStr Sessions Ever!!! | SaaStr\n5 Things That Will Make You Great At Sales: An In-Depth Guide From a Proven Sales Leader | SaaStr\nHow to Fundraise for Your First VC Round With Jason Lemkin and Ben Chestnut! | SaaStr\n\nTop 10 Most Popular SaaStr Sessions Ever!!!", "words": 71, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "So we did it.\n500,000 views on our YouTube Channel!\nOur most popular videos below:\n#1.  How to Get More Leads from Your Marketing Team\n#2.  What Is a Good Growth Rate for SaaS Companies?\n#3.  Top 10 Secrets to Creating Great Content\n#4.  How To Build A Sales Machine With Gong and Pipedrive\n#5.  The Ultimate Guide to Building a Product-Led Business with Gainsight, HubSpot, Asana, and more!", "words": 69, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "So as we’re getting ready to head into SaaStr Annual and all that comes with it,\nwe wanted to share some fun learnings from our YouTube channel:\nWe’ve just crossed 5 million views on\nYouTube.\nIt took us about a year. We think that’s pretty good!\nOur #1 video of all time is the classic “How to Get a $50,000 a Year SaaS Job”:\nYou can see more of our top videos here:\nhttps://www.saastr.com/\ntop-youtube-videos-of-all-time/\nA few other interesting metrics from YouTube:\n#1 Most Viewed Video: How To Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS (2.5m views)\n#10 Most Viewed Video: Top 3 Things to Know About Hiring Your First Sales Rep\n(8x) #15 Most Viewed Video: “The Future of B2B and AI” with OpenAI CEO Sam Altman\n(7x) #25 Most Viewed Video: “How to Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS”\n(6.3x)\n#1 Top Channel: How to Get a Job in SaaS (150K views)\n#5 Top Channel: B2B and AI Fundamentals (60K views)\n#10 Top Channel: Scaling for the First Time (40K views)\nAnd if you want to see what’s trending on YouTube … there are some fun learnings here too:\nMost Viewed Videos of All Time on SaaStr YouTube\n1. How To Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS (2,500,000 Views)\n2. Top 3 Things to Know About Hiring Your First Sales Rep (400K views)\n3. The Future of B2B and AI with Sam Altman CEO OpenAI (360,000 views)\n4. How To Get a Job in SaaS (150k views)\n5. The Ultimate Guide to Scaling for the First Time (100K views)\n6.", "words": 264, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "So we’re finally back to selling again, with real budgets and new features. And it’s very different than even just 12 months ago.\nWe’ve all been through this before, but I wanted to summarize why now is a bigger change for sales professionals than any other time in the last decade or so:\n#1. The buyers have changed dramatically\nThere are more of them — and they’re going into buying mode faster than ever. And it’s not just that you’ve got “more” prospects now, or at least, some of them.\nIt’s actually better for sales people when there is less competition and fewer vendors overall. At least in theory. But the buyers have changed even more dramatically:\nThey are more distributed\nMore decentralized\nLess centralized buying authority\nEven if they aren’t, their expectations to buy faster than ever — with no delays from IT, or marketing, or anyone else\n#2. The products have changed as well\nSo many of us got used to selling the same old product year in and year out for 10+ years.\nBut now we are all building new “AI” features into our products, that customers don’t really fully understand themselves yet. They know they want AI, but they aren’t sure what it does or how it works — at least not right away.\n#3. The sales processes have changed as well\nSo many of us got used to selling the same old way for 10+ years:\nOne rep per prospect\nA “qualified” lead was just a prospect that said Yes, we’re interested in buying your product now\nIt’s still true today — but it’s not enough anymore.\nNow we have to be more agile. More flexible. And more focused on the buyer themselves than ever before.\n#4. The sellers have changed as well\nSales reps are better trained and enabled today than they were 10 years ago, no question.", "words": 314, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "The biggest change I've seen in sales since 2007. No one wants to meet anymore. And that's it. The rest is just fine tuning how you do meetings. How you prepare for them, pitch during them, and close after them.\n\nBut no one really wants a meeting anymore.\n\nIt's time to evolve from this if you want to stay ahead in SaaS sales.", "words": 63, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "x", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "So this is the biggest change in SaaS sales I've seen since 2007. And that was a big one. But it's happening now, and it will be a huge change. It won't be fun for many reps. They'll find out they can do better. But as an investor, I see this all over on my portfolio. Here are the changes:\n\n1. AI is really starting to replace some of SaaS sales itself. Not the human part yet — but lots of it. We've seen a 30% reduction in customer support tickets since we rolled out ChatGPT for SaaStr AI, and I'm seeing this all over my portfolio with other apps. But in B2B, there's so much more to replace than just tech support.\n\n2.", "words": 124, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "I was at an event with a bunch of founders this week and we were discussing how many folks are still going out to meet potential customers. We all agree that’s part of sales, but no one has ever been good at it. I said that the biggest change is now you can just talk to them on Zoom. If they want to get together in person? Then they’ll show up.\n\nIt sounds simple and obvious, but for years we’ve seen more and more folks moving away from going out to meet customers in person. Some of this was due to the fact that it’s hard to travel so much anymore, at least without a lot of planning ahead. But I think it was really just time.\n\nSo many of us have gotten back to meeting our customers on Zoom after all these years. It may be less exciting than seeing them in person. And there are some deals you want to close when they’re actually out together and in the same place, like a joint pilot or demo together.\n\nBut it’s also just so much easier today for most deals to meet customers over Zoom. And I think we’ll never go back from that now.", "words": 206, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So I’ve been doing this for almost 20 years now.\nI know it can be tough to keep going at times.\nAt least you don’t have to worry about public markets anymore like some of us do:\nBut — it’s just not that complicated. Your VCs are worried, but they shouldn’t be.\nYou’re still growing. You’re still profitable (if needed). They made a pretty good bet on you. Yes, the multiples in Public Markets aren’t quite as high today for many tech companies than they were at their peaks. But — this is just normal.\nThe markets always go up-and-down. And yes, it’s harder to raise new capital now from VCs that are worried about public market valuations (and even more so when the IPO window is closed). Even if you could — would you want to be in a $100B+ company? It’s not fun.\nBut your betas matter. Is growth strong? Are profits good, at least for now?\nIf yes, then it doesn’t really matter what public market multiples are today.\nIt will get better again someday. The markets always do bounce back and go up-and-down in cycles. So don’t worry about that too much if you have real revenue and a path to real profitability.\nA related post here:\nWhen Growth Slows — Who Should Be Worried? You Or Your VCs?\nRelated Posts\nThe 3 Types of Venture Capital Funds: Which Are Right For You (or Your Startup)\nYour VPs Don't Need To Have Ever Been A VP. But They Do Need to Have Done It Before.\nDear SaaStr: How Long Does a VC Take to Get Back?", "words": 270, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "The latest from SaaStr:\nPublic multiples are down\nBut that doesn’t mean your start-up is “overvalued”\n— It means you may have to raise at lower valuations in the future\n\nDon't worry about public market multiple right now. Just get back to growth and profitability.\nAnd a few notes on this:", "words": 51, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "x", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So I see it all over Twitter and LinkedIn:\n“VCs are worried now that public multiples have fallen.”\nHere’s what you should be focused on right now:\n\nPublic multiples matter for one reason: They’re the bar your VCs use to value you. If they fall, your valuation falls.\n\nBut here’s something important:\n\nYour VCs are not going to care if a start-up is worth $20m or $60m at 2x ARR in Q1 2026. That’s just noise for them.\nWhat matters? Your progress. How well you’re executing against your plan. Are you hitting the numbers that matter most?\nBecause here’s something interesting:\nThe top Cloud and SaaS companies are still trading at insane multiples.\n\nYou can see it all over Twitter: “Pal", "words": 121, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "so public multiples are down we all know that and it's been going on for some time but what does this mean to your company right so look I'm not a great financial analyst I've never really done finance in my career or worked at a big bank even though I went to Stanford business school which is the last place you might expect to learn about finance they did teach us how to do spreadsheets and excel and all that but what we learned was pretty simple so public multiples are down now it's not like it has never been this way since 2022 or something right a lot of times as an investor I've seen multiples in the public markets go up and down over time they may be higher than average for some companies at some points maybe even multiples of 30 to 40x revenue just crazy numbers but also what's happened is it hasn't always been that way so look if you take a look at something like zoom or slack or one of these other companies that IPO'd at the end of last year and they were all valued at almost or more than 20 billion dollars in many cases today with much lower multiples those valuations are worth half as much but what does this mean to your company well it means you're probably not going to be worth two times 10x 15x revenue anymore right maybe one of these days we'll go back to the good old days where public markets will give a company like zoom or slack crazy multiples but there's no evidence that that has happened for years and so what does this mean it means you're probably not going to be worth two times 10x 15x your", "words": 298, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So as Atlassian crossed almost $3B in ARR, it’s perhaps time to re-look at how they got there. They were an interesting case study already because:\nThey didn’t IPO until a stunning $1B+ in revenue\nAnd they’ve been growing just over 20% a year for years since.\nAtlassian has grown from its very start as a scrappy little startup and now is one of the top SaaS companies in the world. It’s an incredible story, but perhaps not so unusual.\nBut let me highlight 5 interesting learnings that can be applied to your own journey:\n1.  They didn’t IPO until almost $4B ARR\nI know it sounds like a lot today, with many going public earlier and earlier. But back in the early days of Atlassian — even before they went public! — there were some very smart folks at Goldman Sachs that said no way to IPO them before $1 Billion+ in ARR.\nAnd I think they were right.  They just didn’t have enough scale to be a big SaaS company, and the markets aren’t really buying into anything less than that today.\nThis is an interesting case study as we all look at how to go public again — especially with some new rules from the SEC for both IPOs and secondary sales.  What’s the “bar” here?  I’d say it’s $1B+ in ARR, but what do you think?\n2.  They still only have 4% of their customer base paying more than $20k/year\nAtlassian is a big SaaS company today at almost $3 Billion in ARR, and we all know that the majority of its revenue comes from small teams using Jira and Confluence.\nBut they’re not just for SMBs.  There are 30+ companies on Atlassian’s stock exchange (over 1k customers) paying more than a million dollars/year.\nAnd there are almost 3000 other customers with $100k+ in ARR that pay more than $20k each year.  That’s still less than 4% of their customer base!\nSo it seems like we all want to go big or go home, and Atlassian is no exception here — but the reality is a different one.\n3.", "words": 353, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "Atlassian is an interesting case study as they scale to almost $3B in ARR, but don’t quite have a traditional SaaS model. They are more Enterprise and less SMB than most top SaaS companies — with only 15% of their revenue coming from outside North America.\nBut it’s still great to see Atlassian at almost $2 Billion+ in ARR growing 32%, and then accelerating to 33% growth after the tough year of 2023.  Atlassian is still growing 27% on a non-GAAP basis, with free cash flow margins now above 20%.\n#1: NRR remains high\nAtlassian’s non-GAAP NRR for its core SaaS business has remained remarkably consistent at around 125%, and that doesn’t include the impact of new products like Jira Ops.  But it also means there isn’t much room to raise prices.\n#2: New Products are Critical to Growth — At Least For Now\nJira Ops is growing faster than Core, but we don’t know if that will continue once it scales past $100m ARR.  The lesson here may be that you need new products in the Enterprise category as well as SMB to drive growth.\n#3: Customer Count Is Still Growing 20% a Year — But At A Slower Pace Than Revenue\nAtlassian is still growing its customer count by an impressive 21%, but at a slower pace than revenue.  This is normal for many SaaS companies, but it’s always worth keeping an eye on.\n#4: R&D Is Still High As", "words": 242, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So I thought it would be fun to dig up just a few interesting learnings on how Atlassian is doing now, at almost $3B in ARR:\nIt’s growing 23%+ today. So even with more competition than ever, they’re still growing\n25%\nyear-over-year.\nThat’s pretty darn good for a company nearly as old as me!\nThis makes sense — but it still shows how much Atlassian and other SMB leaders have innovated to stay ahead of bigger competitors:\nAtlassian is now adding 30%+ more customers each year, even at $2.9B in ARR\n.\nThe “growth” from new logos has actually accelerated over the years!\nA reminder that no matter what you think about your competition — there’s still always room for growth with SMB", "words": 123, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So let's take a look this week, as we get close to $300 billion. We've seen a lot of interesting things here in 2021 and beyond.\nAtlassian is one of the great success stories at scale in software today — growing from zero to almost $3B in ARR in just about two decades!\nAnd it's gone public twice!\nLet me summarize five pretty cool learnings:\n#1. The IPO was a huge hit\nBut they needed more cash and did an additional secondary stock offering a year later.\n#2. It took them 8 years from the first $100M to the second $100m in ARR — but then it all exploded!\nAnd then grew faster than ever after that.\nAtlassian is the perfect example of going for growth, even at scale.\nIt's never too late to grow faster, if you want. But it does often take a very different playbook from your early days as an IPO candidate. So don't be afraid to evolve and change things up later in your growth journey.\n#3. 10% free cash flow margins\nNow here is a reminder that even at $2B+ in ARR, Atlassian's still generating just $200m of net income — about 10% of its revenues. That may seem pretty low for so much revenue, but it actually isn't too bad given their very high capital expenditures.\n#4. $100k ACV is the sweet spot\nAtlassian at scale today has a fairly large ACV profile, like many other leaders, including ServiceNow and Slack / Salesforce. Atlassian's average deal size? A stunning $120K! But that's down from its peak of about $150k in 2020.\n#5. The best are always accelerating\nAtlassian is no exception here. It has accelerated growth every year over the last decade, with a truly remarkable 30% at almost $3B ARR today!\nSo it can take some time to get going — and even longer to scale up as an IPO candidate. But when you do hit that initial IPO milestone … just watch your shares fly!", "words": 334, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "blog", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "So one day, you might wake up and realize it’s been years since your customers actually started growing. Or maybe never really grew at all.\nI see this happen often enough, when founders don’t quite understand how the “growth cycle” works in SaaS:\nYou have to grow fast\nto get big.  And then,\nyou have to maintain a certain growth rate to stay big\n.  At least for now, if you’re going more enterprise and bigger deals.\nBut there’s another phase after that: when your customers are happy enough to keep buying from you year over year without having to recruit new leads all the time.  That can be called “retention” or at least “maintenance”, but it sure is a lot of effort to keep growing in this phase.  And usually, less than in the prior phases.\nIf you haven’t really achieved NRR (Net Retention Rate) growth by Year 3-4 of SaaS … then something is wrong\n.\nIt could be lack of product-market fit or feature gap for your current customers\n. It can take a while to get there, and often it takes an additional phase:\nYou have to grow fast enough to build the entire team that will support you later.  Then,\nyou have to maintain a certain growth rate in more enterprise sales (and larger deals) to fuel the team\n.\nAnd then when you hit $10m-$20m ARR or so, your customers should be big enough and happy enough with your product to buy from you year-over-year without much recruiting of new leads.  That’s where NRR comes into play as a growth engine — but it will never quite grow faster than the prior phases.\nIt may take time for your customers to become “happy” enough, or at least big enough and mature enough in their use cases, that they can buy from you year-over-year without recruiting new leads all the time.  And this is often a challenge for founders — because it’s not quite as fun as selling into brand-new logos.\nBut if your customers aren’t growing with you fast enough to make NRR work as an engine of growth … then something has gone wrong\n.  It may have been a feature gap, or product-market fit issue earlier on.  Or even more likely — that sales team hasn’t quite figured out how to sell into the right logos at scale yet.\nWhat happens next is what I call “NRR Death”:\nYou end up with a company where your only growth engine is recruiting new leads all the time\n.  And maybe not even doing it very efficiently or effectively — because you haven’t really gotten NRR working for you as an engine of growth by Year 3-4.\nThat’s bad enough, but what happens next if you don’t figure out a way to get back on track is that your team can start growing slower than the new leads you are recruiting.", "words": 487, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "So I just did another big round with SaaStr Fund. One thing we looked at was Net Retention Rate (NRR) and how it’s trending. It’s a simple but important metric.\n\nIf your NRR is below 100%, you have to improve it.\nIt can’t stay down forever, or you will die as a business. But the #1 mistake I see founders make when their NRR starts falling: They think it just magically comes back on its own. It doesn’t. You need to go hunt for those customers that churned.\n\nDo whatever it takes to get them back. Do whatever they want.\nGo 100% customer centric — and ask your customers what you can do to win their business back.\nThey will tell you how to fix NRR.\n\nAnd then … Go Get Them Back.\n\nIf you don’t, the zombie apocalypse for your business is coming in about a year or so.\n\nRelated Posts\n5 Things You Should Know About Jason Lemkin Founder of SaaStr\nSaaStr Podcast #058: How To Build A Truly Great Sales Team with Jason Lemkin\nJoin SaaStr at 2021 SaaStr Annual!", "words": 183, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "x", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "If you're running a B2B company and your net retention is below 100% — don't worry it's not the end. I've been there too, even at $1m ARR. The question is are you actively improving it? Or have you kind of given up.\n\nAt least make sure you know why NRR is low. Is it because of a specific segment that doesn't renew? Because of churn from a competitor? It could be something in your product or service that's driving down net retention, but if so - fix it!\n\nIf not, then just give up on the dream for now.\n\nIt can take years to get NRR back into shape. And if you're at $50m+ ARR and still below 100%... well, I'm sure there", "words": 124, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "talk", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "all right so let's just say you're doing well but maybe not really growing anymore and that can be okay or it can't it can be okay if your competition is struggling as long as the market grows even a little bit because in many cases the top players in any given space are still growing at 30% at 40% 50% just so long as there's some growth left in the world you might not need to change anything and that can be okay but if your market is contracting or shrinking no matter what you do you're going to hit a wall right it doesn't really make sense anymore if your product isn't growing then why are you selling a new feature right so I want to talk about zombies here just a little bit because we talked about these in the earlier session with Alex but they've sort of been hidden from us until now when everybody knows everything through AI and you can see all of this stuff for free yeah so an NRR zombie is pretty simple it's somebody that's growing very slowly right if your net retention rate is below 100% then people are leaving in some fashion or at least not renewing at a high enough rate okay but sometimes we get fooled by the math here and I want to share one example of this from Brex which was one of our saster unicorns that went public recently where they were growing very slowly at just below 100% net retention so it's like this zombie is sort of hiding in plain sight and then what happened right all those other competitors who were doing a little bit better started to eat into them right the market shrunk from a competitive perspective", "words": 298, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Why is my CFO not good at forecasting our burn rate?\nI think there’s a lot of confusion here, and it’s not uncommon.\nThe first thing you need to understand: your CFO should be great at cash management. Not forecasting. Great at managing the books, making sure all expenses are properly accounted for, that everyone gets paid on time, etc. That’s their number one job. And yes … they will often work closely with finance to forecast burn and other aspects of the business.\nBut here is what I see 80%+ of the time:\nYour CFO isn’t actually forecasting. They’re just looking at a spreadsheet from last month or quarter that someone else put together, probably in Excel. You can tell because it’s not really a plan — it’s mostly just a historical extrapolation. It rarely changes significantly on its own. And you’ll often hear: “I need the VP of Sales to update this forecast,” etc.\nNow maybe your CFO is actually doing real forecasting with your VP of Sales, and that’s great! I’m sure there are many folks out there who do exactly that. But what I’ve seen is that a lot of times … it doesn’t really work that way. Instead, the CFO will often just give you a static number from last month or quarter — “We’re burning $800K/month” — and then tell you to go talk to the VP of Sales if you want any more details.\nAnd this isn’t forecasting — it’s reporting. And it might be good enough for reporting … but forecasting? No way. It doesn’t work that well, and a lot of times I think VPs aren’t even really thinking about it deeply at all. They’re too busy closing deals.\nIf you find yourself in the same situation …\nFirst,\nget your CFO to spend 1-2 hours with you each month doing real forecasting together.", "words": 315, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Seems Very Professional, But We Miss Our Monthly Budget by $500k-$1m Almost Every Month – What to Do?\nFirst of all — we’re going long in 2024\nWe are looking for a VP Finance/COO that is ready for the next stage and has experience building out an FP&A team. This role will be responsible for helping us scale our financial infrastructure as we move into our next phase.\nHere’s what you need to know about the position:\nOwn all aspects of company finance\nBuild out full-time FP&A team in 2024\nDevelop processes, tools and systems that allow for efficient forecasting, budgeting and reporting across multiple business units\nManage month-end close process with attention to detail on accuracy, timeliness and quality of financial statements\nWork closely with the CEO and other senior leaders to ensure alignment around key priorities and objectives.", "words": 144, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO sounds very polished but we are missing the burn rate plan almost every month. What should I do?\n\nA few things:\n\nFirst, a burn plan is not really something you need to review each and every month.\n\nSecond, if your CFO doesn’t know it cold — then there’s a problem. The real issue here isn’t that they’re polished or not polished.\n\nThe real issue here is that the CFO is new. At least until now.\n\nYou can’t expect them to truly understand all this stuff without being around for 18 months at least.\n\nThird, and most important: just ask your head of finance / controller — if you have one — to help. It’s their job afterall.\n\nIt’s actually quite simple in the end.", "words": 128, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "I think you need to find someone better than your current CFO.\nThey sound polished, and that’s great if they’re good at closing a VC round or impressing investors\nBut burn rate forecasting is core to SaaS management. It’s something every top CRO, CEO, CTO — everyone — has to do on the fly almost all day long in a well-run start-up.\nIt can’t be missed.\nYou need someone that’s good at it. If your CFO doesn’t have this skill set, then you’ve made an error\nAnd no one will forgive you when they miss another burn rate plan.\nBut here’s what I do know:\nIf you just get someone better than the current person on finance — maybe even a VPs of Finance or Controller-level hire from somewhere else with experience in your market and size — that can make all the difference. And probably not much more\nAnd then, once you have this great team member in place, I’d suggest:\nMake sure everyone is aligned around forecasting. If there’s confusion about what to include or exclude, it will impact accuracy.\nUse a tool like PlanGrid or Glean that helps with budgeting and forecasting. It can automate many of the tedious tasks and make your CFO more efficient\nHave regular check-ins (like weekly) for review and adjustments. This ensures you catch any issues early before they become critical problems\nSet clear expectations at the beginning, so everyone knows what’s expected in terms of accuracy and timeliness.\nBut mostly — just find someone better than them on finance.\nThis isn’t a new problem. It can be fixed if you hire right.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: Why Do So Many Founders Quit After Just 6 Months?\nDear SaaStr: How Much Time Should I Spend Selling to Investors?", "words": 296, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "I recently deployed GPT-4 and Claude Code, two prominent AI tools that have revolutionized how companies interact with customers. This is my experience rolling out these AI solutions.\nMy First Impression:\nThe AI agents are amazing at what they do, but the deployment process was much more complex than I anticipated. Here’s a breakdown of each tool and their respective challenges:\n1. GPT-4 (ChatGPT):\nI used Lovable.ai to deploy GPT-4 on our website for chat support. The integration went relatively smoothly with minimal technical involvement since they have pre-built connectors for SaaS platforms like ours.\nThe only thing that took some time was the initial setup and fine-tuning of the AI model, but once I got it right, the agent works as expected. It’s fast, accurate, and has already saved me a significant amount of time on customer support.\n2. Claude Code (Agentforce):\nThis one was more challenging because we wanted to create our own custom SDR. We decided not to use AgentForce for now but they have some great resources and pre-built connectors that made the integration easier than expected.\nOur biggest challenge with Claude was getting it to work properly inside Salesforce, which took about 3 days of trial-and-error tweaking with their support team’s help.\nClaude Code is incredibly powerful once set up correctly. It can send personalized emails at scale without human intervention and has already generated significant revenue for our business.\nDeployment Timeline:\nHere’s a rough estimate of how long each tool took to deploy, from start to finish:\nGPT-4 (ChatGPT): 2 days\nClaude Code: 3 days + 1 week fine-tuning\nOverall AI Deployment Experience:\nThe initial setup and configuration were the most time-consuming parts. Once we got both agents up and running, they’ve been working flawlessly with minimal maintenance needed.\nI was surprised by how well Claude Code performed in our sales environment.", "words": 308, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?\nIt’s not hard. I mean, it is kind of hard. But it’s pretty easy these days.\nI’ve seen the whole process happen in a single week:\nWeek 1: Choose and buy\nChoose your vendor (we use Lovable) and make sure you can deploy it to your Salesforce or HubSpot\nBuy it, which should take about an hour\nWeek 2-3: Deploy and train\nDeploy it into production. This is where things usually slow down a bit because you need some human intervention to get the AI SDRs set up properly in Salesforce\nTrain them on all your past data\nMake sure they can handle all types of emails, not just perfectly formatted ones\nWeek 4-5: Monitor and optimize\nMonitor how it’s performing compared to humans\nAdjust settings as needed based on what you see\nThe biggest thing I learned here is that the deployment itself shouldn’t take too long—maybe a few days. But training and optimization? That can stretch out over several weeks. And honestly, you’re going to need some human help along the way, at least for the first month or two.\nBut it’s worth it!\nHere are our latest learnings from using an AI SDR:\nWe’ve seen our open rates on outbound emails improve by 100%–200%\nOur response rate has gone up\nThe quality of responses is better\nIt doesn’t replace a human but complements them nicely\nWhat I learned here: The deployment itself shouldn’t take", "words": 249, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: What’s a reasonable timeline for deploying AI-powered sales tools, specifically AI SDRs or AEAs? The answer varies greatly based on your current tech stack and level of automation. I’ve seen it take anywhere from 2-6 months to deploy some AI SDRs, depending on how much you already have set up. You can definitely get started with a few weeks of work, but in many cases the best results come when you start over (at least partially) rather than trying to layer new tools and workflows atop existing ones.\n\nIf your company is starting from scratch here — I’d just tell everyone to hire an AI SDR expert for 30 days. They can get it all set up and then hand it off to the rest of your team once it’s", "words": 132, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "all right we got one more here and then I'll take two questions if you want it's from Alex who is asking how long does it actually take to deploy a good ai sdr well that's the million dollar question isn't it but what do they tell us at alli they told us 10 days they told us five weeks we got another one here and then some other data points from other leaders right here from talk desk folks like you know this is just so many of these AI agents are still in pilot I think that's the problem today not to be too negative but yeah a lot of them haven't really gone live yet or they've only gone partially live so you're gonna have to do your own research and see what works best for you but we can give you some general ideas right here if you want it okay talk desk folks like Alex I know you guys at alli are in the space too with AI agents as well but this is from a few weeks ago and they did an analysis of 150 plus customers that deployed their agent to automate customer service and support for them and what does it take how many hours do we need to invest to get something that actually works here okay so you can see if you go down the middle of the chart it's about four days to deploy the AI agents in talk desk but let me tell you a couple interesting things when did they start using these tools 2023 and then there was an update just last week for some of us who are running behind right here from the folks at talk desk so this is a lot earlier than we would have thought but if it's taking four days now in late", "words": 313, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I use an AI SDR before I have a working human SDR motion?\nI think you should try it. It will likely work for 1-2 reps, but not beyond that\n.\nBut here’s the thing:\nIf your Human SDRs aren’t working … then maybe they are OK. At least in some way. Because if nothing is working …\nthen there might just be a problem with how you’re doing outbound. Not sure, though. The AI SDR will at least work for 1 or 2 reps.\nRelated Posts\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: How to Build an AI SDR from Scratch in 30 Days\nDear SaaStr: Can I Hire a Sales Rep Before We Have Product Market Fit? Dear SaaStr: What's The Best Way To Get My First Sales Reps To Work?", "words": 135, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nThe short answer is yes. But only for outbound, not inbound.\nOutbound should be automated first\nYou can’t do better than your human SDRs doing the same thing as they did before. At least try that for 30 days.\nBut you need to have at least one or two humans in sales and marketing helping with this process, even if it’s just a few deals a month.\nInbound is different\nIf prospects are already reaching out to you because of your brand, then AI won’t make any difference. They’re already interested and want to talk to a human about their specific problem.", "words": 118, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr:\n\nShould I use an AI SDR before I have a working human SDR motion?\n\nIt’s tempting, and it can work. But not if your competition has already started using AI to scale their outbound ahead of you.\n\nIf your competitors are all in on AI for outreach — and they probably are by now — then yes:\n\nUse the best AI SDR tools out there as soon as possible.  Just be honest with yourself about what’s really happening here: it’s not a replacement for human sales reps doing outbound. It’s just an accelerator.\n\nThe problem is that most of us think it’s going to replace our human SDRs and AE team when in fact, it will only accelerate them:\n\nMore qualified leads\nBetter outreach\nFaster follow-up\n\nBut the", "words": 130, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "It is a trap. What you’ll get from most AI SDRs, even if they are great at outbound and have tons of data — is a bunch of automated emails that don’t really work.\nAnd worse,\nthey can kill your brand\nif not done right. Because the messages will be too generic, or not personalized enough, or just plain wrong for your company and product.\nSo it’s better to wait until you have at least a few leads coming in from human SDRs before using AI agents. At least then you’ll know what does and doesn’t work — and you can train the AI on that data. But if you don’t, it will be harder to get any ROI out of them.\nAnd yes,\nyour VP Sales may push back against this. They might have a different idea about how SDRs should function in general. Or maybe they want an instant army for their outbound motion. And they’ll often want to start using AI agents as soon as possible, and not just wait until you’ve got something working.\nI get it. You can’t win them all with your VP Sales if you don’t agree on the SDR motion.", "words": 198, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr,\nWe’re at $8M ARR growing 95%, but can’t get any VCs to give us term sheets. I have two questions:\n1) Would you invest in this company? (The answer is yes, I would)\n2) Should we go for it and just ask the question?\nBest regards\n– Alex\nI think it’s worth a shot — as long as they don’t charge you more than 3 points to find you money.\nYou are at $8m ARR with 90% growth.  That is, in fact, pretty darn good! The only challenge I see here is that the founders aren’t out there raising the capital themselves:\nA little bit of a problem:\nBut it’s not your fault — and you could easily get on Zooms if you wanted to.\nI think if you went out and asked for term sheets yourself with your team, at $8m ARR growing 90%+ … I’m pretty sure 10-15 top VCs would be very interested in investing.  You’ve already raised $3m (great job!) but that doesn’t necessarily mean they can’t invest another say $2-$4M.\nI think you should go out there and ask the question yourself — as long as it’s not a huge distraction from running your business at scale.  If nothing else, if they won’t give you term sheets … then at least you’ll know why.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Do I Get Investors to Take Me Seriously?\nDear SaaStr: When Should You Stop Fundraising? Dear SaaStr: What Are Your Top 10 Tips For Raising a Venture Capital Round?", "words": 255, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: I’m not sure we have enough to sell our startup for. It’s at $2M in revenue and growing quickly. The problem is, it’s going up so fast that every time you get to another milestone like $1m or 3m ARR–the numbers are already out of date. So far, no investor wants to give us the term sheet because they say we’re not at a high enough valuation. What should I do? Just keep growing? It’s hard for me to know what to think about this since it seems like there are pros and cons on either side.", "words": 99, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet\nI think if you are at least growing 100%, and really have a great team, that maybe will be enough. If it’s truly the best in your space … then maybe VCs just don’t see it.\nBut I do know this:\nIf you grow 90% after $8m ARR, then get to even better numbers later (say, $10m or so),\nthen at least one VC\nwill give you a term sheet. I promise.", "words": 91, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We're At $8M ARR Growing 90% But Can't Get A VC to Give Us a Term Sheet\nI'm not sure this is that unusual.  Many B2B start-ups in the enterprise and mid-market struggle to get funded at scale by VCs.  It's just too hard, even if you are growing 100%.  If you're doing it yourself without any outside capital, then great.  But a VC can't always do this.\nYou need an outsized exit for them (or they don’t invest).\nA unicorn.  Or at least something approaching it.\nAnd that's just hard to predict today.  Even when growth is massive and accelerating.\nSo there are no easy answers here, other than:\nGet better at the pitch\nGet more customers\nGet a few more logos from bigger names (even if they aren’t paying as much)\nJust keep going.  It’s always harder later.  But it’s always easier to get funded … next round.  Just be careful not to run out of runway.\nA related post here:\nDear SaaStr: We Raised $1m At a $5m Valuation. Is This Good? No, and Yes. | SaaStr\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\n50+ Top VCs Coming to 2020 SaaStr Annual!!\nThe Ultimate Guide for Going Long in Venture Capital with Jason Lemkin", "words": 209, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So you know what’s been a big, and I get it … objection to many newer SaaS apps these days? That buyers won’t trust them. They may not be as established or trusted brands like Salesforce, Hubspot, ZoomInfo, etc.\nI understand the concern here. But let me tell you — in 2025 this “buyers won’t trust an AI” objection is already collapsing on its own.\nFor example:\nWe’re seeing a new wave of startups building on top of GPT-4 that are going from $0 to $1m ARR in mere weeks, and then tens and even hundreds of millions. They aren’t established brands or traditional SaaS companies. But their users trust them 100%. In fact, they often love them more than the old way.\nFor example:\nWe’ve all seen folks build pretty cool AI agents on top of Claude and GPT-4 in weeks to help with sales outreach — from simple things like emails, to sophisticated workflows for lead routing, qualification, follow-up etc. These aren’t traditional SaaS companies we’re talking about here, but they are trusted by the 1000s that use them.\nWe’ve seen AI agents from folks like Replit and others build in weeks get used at scale — even in production environments that can support thousands of people with no downtime.\nI see this every week. Buyers trust new leaders when they deliver value fast. When you ship something cool, interesting, useful … buyers will find it. And then they’ll give you a shot.\nSo the objection here is already collapsing on its own. It’s just an excuse for not shipping or trying faster. The best sales folks understand this intuitively — if what you have really helps buyers in some way, no matter how new you are — they can see that and trust it. And then tell all their colleagues to do the same.\nThe bottom line: Buyers won’t trust AI agents? That’s not a reason to wait. It’s your job to make them trusted as fast as possible.", "words": 332, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So far, we’ve been talking about how to get started with Lovable. We’re going on and on here at SaaStr Annual. But I think the real power will come when you can truly go multi-product. When Lovable is not just your agent for support and sales, but also your agent as well for marketing, events, customer success, etc. The future is that all of these AI agents talk to each other in a seamless way.\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin  (@jasonlk)\nMay 11, 2025\nI wanted to wait and see what happens with support as the first product category here at SaaStr Annual. I don’t think it’s really that interesting yet. It will be in a year or so when we have multi-product, but right now … not quite.\n\nBut I think we can already see this objection collapsing: Buyers won’t trust AI agents to buy things from them.\n\nI’ve been doing some testing of Lovable here at SaaStr Annual and even if you don’t want to use it — it works. The other day my own credit card was declined when trying to register for a sponsor booth (don’t ask). It literally said, “Your registration has failed: Declined Card.”\n\nBut then I got an email from Lovable asking what happened.\n\nI told the AI agent that this is really frustrating and asked why it wouldn’t just help me fix the problem. The next thing I knew was my card had been charged again, the sponsor booth was reserved, and all the rest of", "words": 255, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "x", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "SaaStr is a great one to test this theory.  It’s got the best content, but it’s not new and there isn’t any real competition.\n\nBut I was surprised how much better our 2026 AI generated SaaStr content did in terms of traffic than our human generated content.\n\nI know it will get even better over time.", "words": 56, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "we're all just getting used to AI agents but we don't have time for that it's already collapsing so let me do three quick examples one is from kovid and they are a great example of this because two years ago when I first invested in them they were going upmarket with salesforce and hubspot with their CRM they had 100,000 customers at the end of last year by June and now it's over 170,000 already growing faster than ever and what changed? They added AI for all of you. So that was two years ago. Now look at this one from a great company called kova and I'm going to tell you why it works so well. This is their AI agent. It's very simple. And here are the three reasons we love it. One, it has no bias. That's what makes it work. Two, all of its training data comes from our customers. So if they say something that doesn't make sense or gets a negative review and then later on you see an example where it made more sense, kovid will update the data with your customer's feedback. And three, every single time I talk to their AI agent, which is pretty often now, it knows exactly what we're talking about in real-time. It remembers everything. So let me give you a quick demo from last week. Let me tell this story first because there are so many great stories of folks that have tried kova and they love it for some reason they always try to get the founder on Zoom, but he's never around, and I'm always available for them now.", "words": 276, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "blog", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "So we’re now running two agents for SaaStr.ai:\nOne to do our podcast summaries (our top agent)\nAnd one to answer questions at saster.com\nThe second is the newer of the two — and what I learned was a surprise: it fell into chaos, even though it looked good. We just stopped training it.\nWhat happened? Well first, SaaStr.ai has become more complicated over time. More agents now are running on our stack. The question agent wasn’t as critical to keeping up with the volume of questions and answers at saster.com — compared to summarizing podcasts for us.\nSo we let it kind of go into maintenance mode. We still trained it, but less than before. And then … it fell off a cliff. Not in terms of accuracy. It was answering 80%+ correctly even after months with no training on the agent itself. But it did fall off:\nIt stopped generating good summary emails for our podcast\nWe had to start manually reviewing and editing them all again.\nAnd worse, it started just getting … really weird answers\nOn SaaStr.com, people would ask a question — and get a response that was way off topic, or completely incorrect. And then the agent started giving out generic responses when it didn’t understand the context of a specific question (this is one of the more annoying things to happen with an AI Agent). I just thought we had a bad train run on this particular agent. But after digging in deeper — it wasn’t that at all.\nIt turned out, our training data was getting stale and incomplete\nWe’d stopped really updating it for months. The model knew how to do everything well … but the data itself was now mostly useless.", "words": 291, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "The New Normal for B2B Leaders:\nYou can’t just train your agent & forget it. That’s the biggest mistake I made with our AI at\n@SaaStr.ai. Here's what happened and how we fixed it:\nProblem: We trained a new AI but didn't follow up regularly.\nThe results were catastrophic – it went off the rails completely, causing frustration and confusion for everyone using it.\nSolution:\n\n1. Regular Check-ins:\nSchedule weekly or bi-weekly audits to ensure your AI is still performing as expected.\n2. Continuous Training:\nUse real user feedback to improve its behavior over time – not just initial training data.\n3. System Maintenance:\nEnsure all necessary integrations, plugins, and software updates are in place for smooth operation.\n\nWe learned this the hard way at\n@SaaStr.ai\nwhen our AI agent started misbehaving without us even realizing it. The fix? We made sure to audit its behavior every single week – a lesson we wish more founders would learn from their own experiences.\n\nIf you’re not maintaining your AI, it’s likely causing more harm than good in the long run. Take proactive steps now before things spiral out of control like they did for us!", "words": 194, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "x", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "We've been running our SaaStr.ai agent for months now - it's been pretty good at answering most questions and responding to comments on the site. But we hit a wall recently when I came back after an extended break and was blown away by just how much of what had worked before no longer did.", "words": 55, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "talk", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "So we recently had one of our best VPs for marketing — a top notch VP that really got it in terms of AI and how to use it — leave us at SaaStr.\nShe was fantastic\nBut then she left, and the team didn’t want to train any new agent,\nAnd now the AI Agent is just doing nothing. No one is training it anymore. It’s not even used anymore. I have no idea what happened. But here’s a lesson in how this works:\nYou can’t train an AI agent\nthen go off somewhere else and leave it behind.\nIt needs constant, daily management, attention — as much attention every day as you would your most critical employee. And if the team doesn’t want to do that … then get someone who does. Because no one is going to magically fix this problem for you:\nThe AI agent just stopped working. No one knew why. It wasn’t even used anymore.\nIt’s not a training issue, or an onboarding issue, it’s more like a “nobody cares” issue.", "words": 177, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "We’ve been using AI for years. But only now is it truly mainstream, and becoming a competitive advantage.\nBut there’s also one uncomfortable truth many in SaaS are still trying to hide from — that adoption isn’t all unicorns and rainbows. Here are the five things I’m seeing at companies like Larridin:\n1. It takes months of real effort for AI to become truly core\nThis is something I see time and again when talking with founders, but it’s not what everyone expects.\nI think we’re still coming up on a year or so since ChatGPT blew everything up in mid-2023. And it took me 6+ months to truly start using Larridin’s AI for real, even though I’ve been an investor and advisor since the beginning. It just takes time, people, and effort.\nEven when you see “AI” in the product description — it actually isn’t until you have a dedicated team building on top of that base layer to make it truly core. This is why we’re seeing AI start to hit its stride now (2026). The base layers were built in 2023-24, but they’ve only just started really being used by humans.\n2. Almost no one uses the vendor’s “AI” features\nThis is a bit of an uncomfortable truth that I think most founders would rather not admit — and especially VPs of Sales. They are often under intense pressure to hit their numbers, and in the short term, it can be easy to see that spending time on AI training and adoption isn’t going to drive immediate revenue.\nThe reality is: almost everyone leaves a vendor’s “AI” features alone\n.", "words": 273, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "Dear SaaS and Cloud Executives, I wanted to share some data from one new tool that’s working well for us at Larridine — but also a few uncomfortable truths about how most of you are using AI tools. Most VPs of Sales don’t want to hear these truths:\n1/5: AI Tools Are Being Deployed Without Proper Training\nAt SaaStr, we’ve tried so many AI tools and have even built our own. But the #1 mistake I see is deploying an AI tool without proper training. The best salespeople are not always tech-savvy or willing to learn new systems.\n2/5: Only 10% of Leaders Understand How To Use AI Tools for Sales\nThe data from Larridine shows that only about 10% of leaders understand how to use these tools effectively. Most don’t know the first thing about setting up and using them properly.\n3/5: The Majority (60%) Don’t Have a Clear Strategy on AI Adoption in Their Teams\nThis is perhaps even more concerning than #2 above. If your team doesn’t have a clear strategy, it’s unlikely to succeed.\n4/5: Half of Leaders Are Still Using Legacy Tools Over Newer Options for Sales Enablement\nAt SaaStr, we’ve made the switch from legacy tools like Salesforce and HubSpot CRM to newer AI-enabled platforms for sales enablement. And it has been transformative. But I see many VPS of Sales still clinging to old systems that don’t provide much value anymore.\n5/5: The Majority (60%) Are Still", "words": 243, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "Hey everyone. A great new post from Larridin, our sponsor for SaaStr AI Day on December 1st with Jason Lemkin and OpenAI’s chief architect:\n5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin\nI just got into a discussion the other day that I think is one we all need to have as founders. It was about something you probably know already, but most people don’t want to hear:\nYour VP of sales isn’t going to adopt AI tools at scale. And it’s not because they’re not smart or capable. Or even because they “don’t get” AI. That’s just a cop-out excuse.\nThe real problem is that your VPS are", "words": 120, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "if you can't sell it you're not going to get any sales out of it and I find most vps of sales are either too scared or they don't know what to do with an AI agent in the early days so they just hide from it but there's a great tool called larridin that helps you see where your customers are using ai agents for sales and customer service and support I'm not sure if we've used it before but I'm going to talk about some of their data here which is pretty interesting in some ways and maybe scary in others so what do they do at Larridin again this is a tool that you can deploy yourself or even have your customers use for sales but they also just track where AI agents are being used across the web as we'll see today so I'm gonna break down five uncomfortable truths about how fast AI adoption is happening in sales and two of them might surprise some of you okay number one this might not be that surprising but it's a little bit interesting to me at least they've got 30,000 plus companies using their ai agent for Sales and customer support today so if you're thinking that you can hide from AI agents for now just know that there are thousands and thousands of other folks in sales who are already actively deploying them okay number two this is interesting it's not really new news but I did find a couple things that were pretty cool to me at least when looking at the larridin data first they have 2.5 million plus users", "words": 277, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "blog", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "you have angry customers and you don't want them but if they're good customers it's worth keeping them right so let me give you six ideas that are sort of obvious and simple but maybe aren't all that obvious to keep your angry customers happy first do not ghost them do not ghost them no matter how mad the customer is you have to respond now I think everyone on saster knows this by now I don't know if it's just me being a little bit more patient in SAS but I'm starting to see more of this and that is people going dark when they lose a deal or get an angry email or whatever it may be so make sure you're there for the customer second go above and beyond for them try to exceed expectations okay now if your product doesn't meet their needs then maybe don't take on too much here but even in those cases I've seen this work magic just do one extra thing that's going to delight the customer whatever it is a special discount an early feature release or something else but find out what they want and give it to them okay third get to know your customers better if you're not doing this every week at least then go back in time review their conversations with support team and talk to some of these customers that aren't happy try to understand why do they think the product is broken or does it just have too many features but listen", "words": 259, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "I'm often asked how you should handle angry customers.  I've been there myself more times than I can count.\nMy simple answer is, first try and get them calm down and on the phone with your best rep or manager.\n\nBut if that doesn't work (and it probably won't), then here are 6 tactics to retain as many of these angry folks as you possibly can:\n6 Tactics To Retain Angry Customers\nAnd be honest.  It's not going to always work out.\nA great customer is worth their weight in gold.  A bad one?  Sometimes even a free product doesn't make up for it.\n\nBut, the best reps and managers will often take on that challenge, if you give them the chance.\n\nRelated Posts\nThe 6 Tactics To Retain Angry Customers\n7 Simple Ways to Reduce Customer Anger\nAngry Customers are Part of Being in SaaS. Get Used to It.", "words": 150, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "x", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "The thing that almost always works is a simple, sincere email. Just write it yourself and send it out immediately. Ask them if they want a refund or credit now — but also let them know you will be back in touch in the next few days with an offer for a partial refund and/or some sort of discount on their next purchase.\n\nAnd ask what you can do to make it right.\n\nThe rest is just noise, and most of that noise only ends up annoying customers even more.", "words": 90, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "talk", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "So, you're angry. You've had it up to here with our product and our team and how we handle things. I get it.\nIt's tough when things go wrong at your company. It's worse that the person on the other end of the call is not listening or caring.\nI'm going to tell you something that may seem counterintuitive, but trust me: if you don't listen here, and do everything in this post, you'll probably lose the customer anyway. But what I've found over years as a founder and CEO is sometimes customers will forgive you. They won't always come back, not all of them. But they may.\nAnd it's much better than losing your best customers forever.\n\n6 Things You Can Do To Get Angry Customers Back:\n\n#1. Listen to the customer\nDon't be defensive. Don't get angry. Just listen. And don't pretend you heard her or him when you didn't, and then tell them what they really meant.\nAnd do it in a way where it's clear that you're listening.\n\n#2. Have The Customer Be Heard by Your Boss (or CEO)\nThis is usually the #1 thing. If your boss doesn't hear from angry customers, then no one does. And if you don't have someone to listen and fix things, then nothing really gets fixed.\nSo make sure they are heard.\n\n#3. Send a Real Apology\nNo one wants an apology that's just a standard form email or call script. We all know those aren't real.\nAnd we will see through them. They don't matter.\n\nBut if you send out a real, personal, heartfelt apology from your boss (or CEO) — it may go far in getting things back on track.\n\n#4. Do the Right Thing\nThis is the most important thing of all\nIf something went wrong, and they lost data or whatever — fix it\n\nIt's not fun to do right now. It won't be easy. But you have to.\n\n#5. Be Honest About What You Will Fix And When (and Why)\nAngry customers hate being lied to. They hate broken promises.\nBut they respect a clear, honest promise of what will get fixed and when\n\nDon't try to hide how long it's going to take or whatever\nJust be honest.\n\n#6. Give Them Something Back for Free\nThis is probably the #1 thing you can do right now\nGive them something back for free\nIt doesn't have to cost a fortune, but if they lost data or whatever — give them some of that back for free\n\nMake sure it's enough and fair.\nAnd most importantly,\nmake sure your boss (or CEO) knows about this.\n\nI guarantee you, in every single situation I've seen over the years where customers were angry … doing these 6 things would have fixed everything.", "words": 465, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a Chief Marketing Officer?\nThis is really an answer for many roles, but it’s especially true for marketing.\nIn some cases you don’t need to hire at all.  A great VP of Marketing can do everything herself — from brand and demand gen to product marketing and even sales enablement (a lot).\nBut if your VPM isn’t doing this well on her own … then the job is too hard, or the company is too small.\nSo don’t wait for a CMO.  Hire a VP of Marketing instead.  And then only bring in a Chief Marketing Officer later — when things are going very well and she’s got enough resources to do even more with it.\nYou can see this dynamic play out if you talk to most top VPs of Marketing today, from Bill McDermott at ServiceNow to Maggie Chan Day at Salesforce.  They all started as VPMs themselves.\nIn the early days, they did it all — or at least enough of it that their company could scale quickly and successfully.\nLater … well later is when you go big time with a CMO!\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: What Does an Average VP of Marketing Do?\nThe $1 Billion+ ARR Club\n5 Interesting Learnings from GitLab's CEO on Scaling to 500 Employees", "words": 223, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nWhen you have $2m ARR and 80% net revenue retention, it's time to hire your first VP of Marketing.\n\nNot earlier. Not later.\n\nAt that stage:\n\nYou've scaled from 1-10 employees\nYour market is defined and competitive\nCustomer acquisition costs are optimized\n\nThat's when a seasoned CMO can make real impact on growth.", "words": 64, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nThere's no one answer, but here are 3 rules I've learned:\n\nFirst, wait until you have at least $1m in ARR.\n\nSecond, don't hire a true VP of Marketing unless it is your #1 job to do so. And most importantly,\n\nThird, make sure the person you bring in can handle this role themselves for a while — and ideally even 5-7 years out.\n\nIt's too much risk if they haven't been there before.", "words": 85, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "So at what stage should I hire my first chief marketing officer or cmo?\n\nThe answer is not simple, but here are some guidelines.\n\nFirst off, if you're pre-product market fit and you can't figure out how to get more leads into the funnel, just add a VP of Marketing. And let them be an owner in the sales process at least until they have a team under her or him. You may need 4-5 VPs before that.\n\nBut once you've got product-market fit, then it's time for your first CMO. Not only will they get leads into the funnel better, but they'll also make sure those leads are properly qualified and handed off to sales as efficiently as possible.\n\nIf they're working well with Sales, a good VP Marketing can generate $10m in revenue for you once she or he has 1-2 VPs under her. That means a $20M ARR company, at least.\n\nAnd if it's more than that? Well then I'd say go hire your first CMO!\n\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #100: The Top 5 Pieces of Advice from SaaStr CEO and Founder Jason Lemkin\nThe \"New\" New in Venture Capital with Jason Lemkin + Harry Stebbings (Video)\nDear SaaStr: How Do You Motivate a Team When Your Startup is Struggling?", "words": 212, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\nIt’s not bad. It can be good.\nBut let me tell you, I have seen so many folks who leave too early — and sometimes even when they are at the top of their game.  When things seem like they’re going well.\nThey often regret that move in the end:\nSo why? Why do people stay longer than it makes sense?\nI think a lot of founders don’t want to be seen as “quitters.”\nBut I just saw another SaaS founder sell his company for $100m+ … and he had never raised any external funding. He didn’t need anyone’s money.\nSo he was able to do what ever the hell he wanted, when ever the heck he wanted.\nAnd it worked out incredibly well.\nThat can be very empowering.\nBut there are limits on that approach too:\nYou don’t have the team, and you don’t have the marketing machine.  And in many cases, no one else will join a “no external funding” startup as an employee — because they know it’s going to fail at some point.\nSo while staying longer than necessary can be hard for your ego … it often isn’t a good idea. It doesn’t always lead to more success than leaving.\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\nTop SaaStr Posts + Videos of the Week! Top SaaStr Posts and Vids of the Week!!", "words": 238, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should you really leave your startup if things are struggling?\nLeaving is bad\nWhy:\nYou will never be able to raise venture capital again. You can't even get an interview for another start-up.\nBut, but -- why should I stay in a dead-end job where no one wants to work with me? Why invest any more time or energy into something that isn't working out?\nIf you have been there 2+ years and it is still failing, then\nleave. You will never be able to raise venture capital again.\nBut if things are just rough for a quarter or two -- stay. It's probably worse somewhere else right now.\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚡️SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15⚡️ (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 10, 2024\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Long Does it Take to Get a Startup Going?\nTop 20+ Most Popular SaaStr Blog Posts of All Time!\nHow do I get involved in the startup ecosystem?", "words": 158, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: I am working at my third start-up, and we are all trying hard. However, things aren’t going as planned. I have been doing this for 8+ years now and I can see that the stress is taking its toll on me. My job security has become unstable and it doesn’t look like I will be able to make a career change in my current role anytime soon. I am starting to feel hopeless, but at least we are trying hard. Is it bad for someone working at their third start-up to leave? No, there is no shame in leaving if your startup isn't going anywhere anymore. We all make decisions that don’t work out. It’s okay. The only problem here is that you’re still hoping things magically turn around from where they", "words": 134, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "okay so I can tell you that there are three stages of life and career okay and for most Founders they go through this order but maybe not always stage one is from zero to 10 million in Revenue or even lower than that and here's the thing it's really hard to get a company off the ground at scale and so if your startup fails then you're going to think well I was part of a failed start up but let me tell you something this doesn't have anything to do with the next one okay it just does not as long as you go on to something that is more successful than the last thing you've been involved in as an entrepreneur so if your startup fails don't worry about the fact that it's struggling because once you get to 10 million or a little bit higher than that things really start getting easier now I know this may sound counterintuitive but they do and there are two reasons for this okay one is when you're running 10 million in Revenue at scale you've got so many people working on so many different issues in the company and it's just too complicated to worry about a small thing like a struggling startup number two the companies at that stage have already figured out what they need to do to be successful right there are certain things they can't figure out but I don't know if there's anything you could not figure out by 10 million in Revenue so as long as your next start up is bigger and more successful than your last one then it really doesn't matter that the last one failed", "words": 286, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "blog", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "Ok so I’ve been saying this for years now.\nIt’s still true today though — and even more important than ever:\nIf your AI feature didn’t materially boost revenue, it doesn’t count\nWhat does that mean?\nLet me give you a few examples:\nMy #1 favorite from the past year or two is Gong. Gong is an app I’ve used for years to record all my calls and improve them.\nBut then they added their AI agent to do much more of the work. And it actually works pretty well now, at least in sales. And so we ended up buying 100k+ credits a month from Gong. Now that’s not the same as a real new revenue stream per se … but we definitely bought $3m-$4m worth of credits and licenses this year alone.\nSo, Gong’s AI agent works well for me at least. It’s improved my results, even if it doesn’t do everything I need (yet).\nBut others haven’t done so well:\nI tried to get a new AI agent from one top leader in the space to automate our customer support — and we never could figure out how to make it work or do anything material\nAnother vendor of ours was supposed to “improve” their product with more AI, but really did nothing. It didn’t improve my results at all.\nWe spent a lot on a leading CRM that promised an AI agent to automate sales followups — and they never worked in the end as planned either.\nAnd so what’s the lesson? Just because you add some “AI” or whatever feature, doesn’t mean it will work. And if it doesn’t materially boost revenue … then it just isn’t important enough right now. You need to try again. Maybe this time with something a bit more proven.", "words": 300, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue... it doesn't count.\nTry again.\n\nA few thoughts on what's really working:\n\n1⃣ The best are making more money from their agents than they make themselves\n2⃣ They're growing faster with fewer headcount constraints\n3⃣ It’s not just a feature – it’s becoming the core of your product\n\nIf you can’t check all 3 boxes, keep trying.", "words": 65, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "x", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If your AI feature didn’t materially boost revenue—just don’t count it. You’ll just confuse yourself and everyone else. A “material” boost is 1%+ growth in the next quarter from that new agent or AI tool. Anything less is … nothing.\nAnd if you can’t see a material benefit, then ask:\n“Why not?”\nIs there something missing?", "words": 56, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "talk", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "I was at my portfolio company recently that had been running an early beta of their AI agent for several months\nIt finally launched officially\nAnd it wasn't a big hit on the day they announced it. Not even close.\nBut over time, they reworked and remade their AI feature\nThey added new functionality, built out training data, improved user experience, made sure agents could be deployed across different workflows and use cases, etc.\nIt's taken months of hard work to get there\nBut now:\nThe agent is being used by 100s of customers daily\nAnd it's generating real revenue. Real ARR\nIn fact, the AI feature alone will likely account for half their new $1m+ in bookings next year.\n\nSo here’s what I’ve learned from all this:\nIf your AI didn’t materially boost revenue… it doesn’t count.\nAI is not a “nice to have” anymore or an experiment. It has to deliver real, tangible results. And if you can’t make that happen — don’t pretend like you did. Don’t claim success when the metrics aren’t there yet.\nBut here’s what I’ve also learned:\nIt takes time and effort\nAI features don’t magically become game-changers overnight. You have to keep pushing them forward, refining them until they finally deliver real value.\n\nAnd it doesn’t just happen once. It happens again and again as you continue to learn from your customers’ needs, incorporate their feedback into the next iteration of your AI feature.\n\nSo here’s what I would do if I were in this situation:\nI’d keep going\nKeep pushing forward on improving that AI agent until it actually starts driving real revenue growth for the business.", "words": 276, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox is a brutal but important insight about what’s coming next for work and productivity.\nIt was first observed by John Cowan, an internet veteran who has been around since the early 1990s, in response to Elon Musk’s claim that AI agents would make human labor obsolete. Here’s how it works:\nLet’s say you have a team of two people doing a specific task: A and B. They can do this task together quickly because they work well as a pair. But then you introduce an AI agent into the mix – let’s call her C. C is very good at doing X, but she doesn’t understand Y or Z. So here’s what happens:\nA and B will try to train C to handle more of their workload, especially in areas where they struggle. But C will refuse.\nInstead of taking on tasks that are outside of her area of expertise, C will demand that A and B provide her with specific instructions for each task she needs help with – step-by-step documentation, exactly what inputs to use, precisely how to verify outputs. And then when you finally get something working that way…C will fail.\nThis is the Cowan Paradox in action:\nThe team’s productivity actually drops because C takes up so much time training and babysitting her, even though she can do some things faster than humans on their own. This pattern has played out in every organization we’ve worked with. The AI agent that was supposed to augment your work becomes a drain – something you have to dedicate more of your team’s attention to.\nThis is why the “AI Agents Will Let You Do Less Work” narrative doesn’t hold up in practice.", "words": 287, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "So I just joined a new VC, and we did an internal survey on how much work each of us actually does now that there’s so many AI agents out there.\nThe results are fascinating.\n\nFirstly — no one really knows what to do with all these AI Agents yet\n:\nThey’re still figuring it out. They can’t quite get the job done for them, and they don’t know how much work each will truly save you in total. But they’ll figure it out.\nSecondly — everyone is working more hours than ever\n:\nEven if 50% of your day has an AI Agent doing half the work, at least as many hours are being put into training agents, testing them, reviewing their output … and finding new tools to make all this magic happen. It’s a lot more work in total.\nThirdly — I’m actually working longer hours than before\n:\nI know it sounds crazy. But let me be clear: 1 AI Agent cannot do the job of an entire team. Not yet, at least. So while it does save some time and effort (and that is real), you still need to work a lot more hours overall.\nThe Cowan Paradox — The More You Work At It, The Less You’ll Actually Get Done\n:\nThis one I know from the top of my head. Working on it every day for years now.\n\nIt’s not just me.  And most folks have given up using AI Agents at all today. This is why.\nBecause when you add them together — and try to use so many, and train each one", "words": 271, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "It’s not a bug — it’s a feature\nA few weeks back I was talking to one of my first VCs, Dave Cowan. He’d seen me through 3 companies now and we’ve done some amazing things together on the venture side as well.\n\nAnd he had this crazy insight that blew me away: The AI Paradox\n\nHere’s what’s happening:\nAI Agents Are Great\nThey’re so great at doing everything — building, writing, marketing, selling. They can do it faster. They never get tired. And they don’t need any training or support in the classical sense.\n\nThe Problem Isn’t Training\nIt’s not that AI agents are poorly trained. It’s that we’ve trained them to be incredibly effective — at doing more work.\n\nBut here’s what Dave", "words": 125, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "I don't know what your experience is, but I've been in AI for 18 months now and it's only gotten harder to do less work.\n\nIt should be easier right? We have all these tools out there today. So we should be able to get more done with the same amount of time or even less. And that's true at the top end, but here's what I've found in AI. Here's my cowan paradox and it is this: every single tool I use now makes me do more work than last year.\n\nAnd that just doesn't make sense right? But there are two reasons why it's true. Let's go into some of the tools we all use today, but maybe not as much as we should in our AI Agents. First, let's talk about Claude Code. Claude code is one of the first things I used to try and save me time with my AI Agent.\n\nClaude Code is a great tool for training models if you're not technical or don't want to train your own model from scratch on Anthropic's platform. And it has gotten better over time, but what I've found is that every single iteration of Claude code makes it more work to do less work. It used to be pretty easy with Claude Code, just take one prompt and you could get an answer.\n\nAnd then they updated the model a few months ago. Now you can't use it without 10 seconds or something like this. You got to do all these different prompts. So there's no way I'm going to go from 30 minutes per question down into seconds with Claude Code.", "words": 277, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "So one thing we don’t talk enough on SaaStr about is when to build it yourself, and when buying something makes more sense.\nIt’s a trade-off that can make your life much easier — but also very expensive in time and energy if you do get into the wrong game here. So let me just share my top 5 learning from building our own N=1 apps for SaaStr itself:\n#1. Building Your Own Apps Beats Buying It When You Have a Deep, Very Specific Use Case\nI’ve talked about this before with our own internal app “SaaStr AI” — but the point bears repeating.\nWhen we did, we built it because no other solution worked as well for us. I don’t mean there was nothing good out there, but what existed at that time just didn’t work in a very specific way we needed. We had to build something that could do “a lot” — which is hard enough on its own — and then integrate with our existing stack in ways no one else did.\nNow it’s not for everyone. You have to be deep into the problem, know how to solve it (at least a bit), or at least know where to get started. But if you do … building yourself will often beat buying when:\nYou need custom integrations that no one else does\nYour workflows are truly unique and complex — not just different\nIt’s mission-critical but has some edge cases, too\n#2. “N=1” Apps Have a Much Higher Bar To Cross Than Your Average SaaS App\nThe bar is actually much higher to cross for us N=1 apps compared to your average software app.\nA few reasons:\nWe use them 24/7, often with critical data\nThey usually have some edge case or two that no one else does\nWe’ll always have a very deep understanding of the problem and solution — so we can fix it faster than anyone else.", "words": 325, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The best way to make a ton of money is often building an app that makes you more efficient. And then charging others for it.\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 2, 2024\nWe’re all so busy in SaaS today and the economy isn’t helping much — that’s why we see so many apps being built by individuals or small teams to make their own lives easier. I call this “N=1” because they are just solving a problem for themselves.\nBut … it often works!\nI’ve seen this with my own team at SaaStr, and now more than ever in the era of AI. When you’re not spending your time fighting an app — that’s when things really take off! You can do so much more than ever before.", "words": 133, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The N=1 App is here to stay\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin #🏠👋 (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 10, 2025\nI recently had a great discussion with a SaaS CEO that I’d invested in about the “N=1” app. What’s an N-1 App? It’s when you build something yourself to solve your own specific needs — but maybe it doesn’t quite fit what others would buy as a traditional product.\nA while back, I built my own simple AI SDR agent myself for our outbound sales. It didn’t really work out too well in the end (see below), at least not compared to our top-performing agents from Artisan and other top vendors. But it was also fun", "words": 113, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "I’m not sure I totally get it, but N=1 apps are a thing now\n— SaaStr (@saastr)\nNovember 29, 2025\nSo you may or may not have heard of “N=1” applications.  You know them.  It’s your homegrown CRM built in Notion. Your own AI agent for customer support. Or maybe just a custom version of an existing app.\nIt looks like N=1 apps are everywhere now, and I don’t totally get it either.  But they seem to be working — at least well enough for founders to keep using them instead of buying from someone else, even if that means doing all the work themselves.\nN=1 applications aren’t really a new thing\n.   I built my first homegrown CRM in 2006 and used it through our Series A.  It was so good I never wanted to buy anything better!  But at least back then we had no other choice but to use whatever apps were available, and there wasn’t the massive array of tools today that you can bolt together into your own bespoke solution.\nBut maybe now is different because of AI\n?  Maybe it’s easier than ever before for folks to build their own CRM or homegrown version of Salesforce.  That makes sense — because we have so many better tools, and they’re all AI-powered and so much more powerful than even just a few years ago. And it seems like these “N=1” apps are everywhere now.  If you look at my Twitter feed, for example:\nMy own homegrown CRM in Notion\nAI agent from Glean that really works well.\nBuilt our own custom customer support AI agent on top of Replit and ChatGPT\nOur new N+1 app to handle all the SaaS companies we work with.  It’s so good I never want to use any other tool again!\nAnd it seems like there are a lot of folks, at least a few dozen or more, building their own AI-powered agents for customer support on top of Replit and ChatGPT:\nI built an agent using Chat GPT that can handle my entire email funnel\nMy homegrown version of Salesforce in Notion.  It’s so good I never want to buy anything else again!\nWhy are N=1 apps everywhere now? I think it’s because we have so many better tools, and they’re all AI-powered and so much more powerful than even just a few years ago.\nSo what should you do about this trend?\nFirst of all — don’t try it unless your life will really be easier\n.  Building out some bespoke app is never going to make sense for most people.  You already have too many apps, too many tools and vendors.  If you’re a one-person shop with 1-2 employees, maybe building something yourself makes sense.", "words": 458, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "blog", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "But not all are decelerating. Not yet, anyway.\nCloudflare’s latest results — a $3 billion+ quarter with 40% growth at 120% free cash flow margins\n— remind us that there are still plenty of opportunities to grow in B2B SaaS and Cloud Infrastructure.\nHere are the top 10 numbers from their Q4 report that explain why:\n#1. Record Quarterly Revenue: $3 Billion+\nCloudflare just hit its highest quarterly revenue ever — a staggering $3 billion for the period ending December 31, 2025.\nThis represents a 40% year-over-year growth rate at scale (with ~$12B in total annualized revenue).\nWhat this means\n:\nWhen you’re growing at scale and have real product-market fit, there’s still plenty of runway to hit new records. Cloudflare has been on a tear since their IPO.\n#2. 40% YoY Growth at Scale: A Testament to Product-Market Fit\nCloudflare is proof that when you get it right — with true, persistent demand for what you’re building — the growth can continue even after hitting unicorn status ($1B+ ARR).\nThis isn’t about “faking” growth or relying on hype. It’s about building something people genuinely need and want to pay premium prices for.\n#3. 120% Free Cash Flow Margins: The Ultimate Efficiency Metric\nCloudflare has achieved an astonishing 120% free cash flow margins in their latest quarter, which is rare at scale (most companies have declining profitability as they grow).\nThis means that for every dollar of revenue Cloudflare generates — it keeps $1.20 after operating expenses.\nWhat this means:\nWhen you can maintain high profitability while growing rapidly, the economics get very compelling. This isn’t just a “nice-to-have” metric — it’s a competitive advantage in itself.\n#4. 30% Year-Over-Year Growth for SaaS Business\nCloudflare’s Enterprise business hit $1 billion+ ARR and delivered 32% year-over-year growth at scale, while their SMB segment grew an impressive 33%.\nThe fact that both segments are growing well above the market average shows that Cloudflare has diversified its customer base effectively.\n#5.", "words": 328, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "So I just did an epic deep dive on why there are so many decelerating B2B companies today.\nThe reasons aren’t that complicated:\nMost of them haven’t transitioned to SaaS yet\nAnd most of the ones that have… stopped investing in new products and features. And they’re not growing anymore.\nBut Cloudflare — one of my favorites from early SaaStr days, because I love their mission as much as their product — just had its best quarter since 2021! A $2B+ deal with AWS to power every website on the planet… and then more. The numbers:\n$100M ARR growth rate\n25% YoY increase in Q4 FY24 compared to Q3 FY23\nCloudflare’s first public SaaS company to reach $5 billion revenue\nNow powering 40 million domains, up from 30 million at IPO\n$2B+ contract with AWS: Cloudflare will power “every website on the planet”\nAWS deal represents ~10% of Cloudflare’s current addressable market\nNew AI products (AI Firewall and Web Application Firewall) are driving significant growth in enterprise sales\nCloudflare has been quietly building one of the most important SaaS companies nobody talks about. They’re not flashy, but they’ve built something truly special:\nInfrastructure for the cloud-native world.\nThe Cloudflare Story: How a $5B+ Company Built on Open-Source Innovation Became AWS’s New Partner\nWhat I love is how transparent and humble this was. The CEO wrote an email to customers explaining exactly why they’re growing so fast —", "words": 237, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "x", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "In a world where most SaaS leaders are decelerating, Cloudflare just reported its best quarter since 2021 — and it’s not slowing down anytime soon.\n\nHere are the numbers that explain why:\n\n1. Revenue Growth: Up to 43% YoY from $0-50M ARR (previously flat at ~20%)\n2. New Customer Acquisition: Accelerating with net new customers up +12%\n3. Deal Size: Growing +15% year-over-year\n4. Enterprise Expansion:\n— 10K+ customer base now expanded to ~40,000+\n— Up from 0 in Q1’23 to $5M ARR by end of FY24 (100x growth)\n5. Net Dollar Retention: 110-120% — a sign that customers", "words": 99, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "talk", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "So as I said, it looks like 99% of us are decelerating this year. And we’re seeing more and more folks that just can’t hit the plan anymore. The CACs have gotten too high, or NRR has slipped to a fraction above zero — even if it’s still positive.\nBut then there is Cloudflare. I don’t know what magic they do here at $5B+ in ARR — but they are growing faster today than ever before. And look, growth slows over time. But not that much. It doesn’t have to be 100%+. Not even close. But it can grow 30%-40% for a decade or more.\nAnd Cloudflare is doing just that.\n\n10 Numbers That Explain Why\n#1: The Growth Acceleration Is Real\nCloudflare’s latest quarter was their best since Q4’21 — with revenue growing 38% year-over-year to $532.8 million. What really matters isn’t the top-line number itself, but where you’re headed relative to your peers.\n#2: Cloudflare Stopped Growing Slower and Started Growing Faster\nTheir growth rate has been trending upwards since Q3’23 — a trend that’s likely sustainable given their positioning as a security-first platform in an increasingly uncertain world. Remember how Box was growing 10% last year? And this year it’s down to about 6%. That’s decelerating, not accelerating.\n#3: They’re Not Just Growing Fast—They’re Also Getting More Efficient\nWhile many companies are expanding their sales teams and marketing budgets as they scale, Cloudflare has maintained a relatively lean approach. This is the model most of us should aim for in SaaS at least by $100m-$150 ARR+.\n#4: Their Gross Margin Is Crushing It — 80%+ At Scale\nThe efficiency story goes beyond just sales and marketing spend. They’ve managed to maintain gross margins above 80% even as they approach a billion dollars in annual revenue.", "words": 298, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "Dear SaaStr:\nHow many salespeople do I need to hit $10m ARR?\nThe answer is … it depends.\nThere are two playbooks, really.  If you can close deals with zero help from the field (i.e., demos are not required) and if your product is so obviously better than competition that customers buy without meeting a human, then 5-6 reps may be enough to hit $10m ARR in Year One.\nBut most of us need humans for some part of sales.  You want someone at least to demo the app, help with onboarding, and generally solve the customer’s problems. So — you probably will need 8-12 AE’s to get there, if your deal sizes are $10K-$20k per year.\nA bit more here:\nHow Big Should Your Sales Team Be? The Simple Math\nRelated Posts\nThe Very Best of SaaStr in 2023! 🎉\n5 Interesting Learnings from AI B2B Leader Replit at $1 Billion+ ARR\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: How to Build a Great Sales Team with Greenhouse.", "words": 168, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "So you're building an awesome new AI SaaS app that's getting some real traction.\nA few questions for now:\nWhat is your CAC?\nHow long do deals take to close? And what are the exact steps in those processes\nDo sales reps really need to work with customers or can they just drive pipeline?\n\nIf it takes 6+ months and you're spending > $10k ACV, then a classic SaaS model often works best. You need lots of AEs.\n\nBut if your deal sizes are < $2K ACV, the CAC is low (like <$5k), and reps can get a lead to close in 1-3 weeks... you might be able to do more with less.\n\nIt's not impossible.\nYou just have to be hyper-efficient.\n\nSo, I've learned two playbooks here:\nThe Classic SaaS Model\nA lot of folks that sell $10K+ deals see the world as a big, slow enterprise sales process. It takes 6 months to close a deal. And it costs >$5k CAC to get there.\nYou need lots of AEs.\n\nI've seen many AI apps where you can't really hire anyone until your revenue is > $1m ARR. That's just how this classic model works.\n\nBut if all that's true, then go long with headcount.\n\nThe Efficient Startup Model\nIf it takes 2-3 weeks to close a deal and the CAC is low (like < $5k) ... sometimes you can do more with less.\nI've seen many AI", "words": 239, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "x", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "If you do have to hire sales reps, I’d say start with 1 rep per $100m ARR if you can. That’s what we did. And then just scale it up from there. But the question is — how much of your growth will be driven by them? Maybe not very much at all. Just get a VP Sales that understands this and knows how to do it.", "words": 67, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "so we've been doing this saster podcast for over eight years now so it's not really that new but i thought this was actually one of the best ones lately and why is this great you know what do you need if your hot startup in b2b today how much sales team should you have because a lot of folks are saying 50 to be competitive right 100 to just compete in ai in in some categories or more but that's not true there's two different playbooks here and the only way is is to know which one you're playing so we had jeff from clavio who i've known for years he was actually on our podcast back when it first launched like eight years ago clavio has become a massive name in ai b2b today they are worth over 10 billion dollars and maybe more by the time this goes out but jeff and his team were very early to get into all of this so i asked him what did you need for sales to take off at your startup he said we had four reps to start when we hit a million dollars in revenue then six and then eight and that's it now they have thousands because it does scale but there was no magic to getting from those first four reps to the next six or even the next 30 or 40 here's what i think of as playbook one which is really more like playbook two you know most folks say look you need at least 20 salespeople to be competitive in b2b today and it's true if you don't have a product that can sell itself if your product doesn't self-serve if you're not able to do the majority of customer acquisition yourself or with minimal human assistance then", "words": 306, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "So how do you not run out of cash?  I was asked this question again recently.  There’s no magic answer here:\nBut there are two things that can really help.\nFirst, it always helps to have more money than you think you’ll need\n.  It sounds simple, but it’s so true.  Even if you’re not planning to burn through your funds fast, or even at all — in fact, most great SaaS companies don’t lose any cash until the very last minute … or ever.  But still have a reserve.  Just think of how much more comfortable you’ll be just knowing you’ll never run out of money.\nSecond, it always helps to extend your runway\n.  Whether through cost-cutting, or even raising more capital when needed (but not too early).\nI know this sounds simple.  And in a way, it is.  But both are powerful and real tools for founders here:\nMore cash than you think you’ll need.  It’s always better to have extra.\nAnd extend your runway if possible.  You can always go back to investors or raise more capital when needed.  But just the knowledge that you could extends the time frame — until at least you see some real traction and momentum in sales, marketing, and revenue.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Do I Protect Myself from Running Out of Cash?\nSaaS is a multi-year journey. There's no need to run out of cash for years.\nYour First VP Should Be Sales", "words": 245, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "The #1 mistake I see founders make again and again is not managing their cash flow properly. And even worse … running out of it.\nI get into this in more detail here:\nBut let me summarize the two things that actually help.\nFirst — you have to understand your burn rate. Not just how much money you’re spending per month, but why. Is most of it going towards headcount? Or is there something else driving it?\nSecond — if you’re growing slowly and predictably, maybe a slower growth plan makes sense for now. If that’s what the market will support.\nIf you have to make layoffs or cut costs later in any event … don’t pretend they won’t happen by running out of cash instead!\nAnd one more thing I see founders do again and again: They hide from their burn rate, their cash flow. Don’t. Get it right now. It’s not fun, but it’s better than being blindsided when you run out.\n#1 tip to avoid a Founder Crisis:\nUnderstand your Burn Rate\n– What is it? Why is it so high?\n– What can you cut back on today?\nA slow growth plan isn’t the end of the world. And hiding from cash flow is even worse.", "words": 210, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "x", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "How to not run out of cash as a founder. Two things that really help:\n\nA lot more on this at\nSaaStr University:\n#1. Go Longer With Your Last Round Than You Think You Can\nAlmost all founders think the last round is going to be their final one … and it’s almost always too early.\nThis doesn’t mean you can do this forever, or even until 100% of your customers are on an annual contract, etc.\nBut here’s a simple rule:\nGo longer with that last round than you think you can. Even if it seems like just 2-3 months more is all you have in the tank to make payroll and sales comp for next quarter.\n\nYou’ll be surprised how far this gets you.\n\n#2. Make Every New Hire Pay", "words": 132, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "okay you know cash is king in startups right and so many founders screw this up it's all too common but there are two things that I've seen work again and again when done correctly number one focus on getting to $1 million in revenue as fast as humanly possible okay now most of us will never get from 0 to $1m in a week or even a month maybe not even next quarter so let's make this a goal for the year because if you hit that goal and you don't burn too much cash you'll be okay yeah it might take longer but it just doesn't matter and I know what you're thinking why do we need to get there in 12 months or less well first of all it shows you have some traction right it's not like this is a crazy product that nobody wants that's the biggest risk in early stage startups so if you can make $1 million even with terrible margins that means your team was able to sell something and it doesn't matter what it is it could be software or hardware or whatever but let me tell you I've seen all of these work okay a couple of examples from folks we invested in one company called maverick labs had zero revenue at the end of December 2019 and then they hit $1 million by June 2020 with almost no burn that's crazy right it was a lot of hard work but it shows you can do this second focus on keeping your burn rate as low as possible okay now I've seen founders take this to an extreme and I'm not saying we should all be doing this but what does happen is when the burn is too high even if you're growing at crazy rates you will run out of cash", "words": 312, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "blog", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "I recently participated in an online panel with several other SaaStr founders, and we started discussing the reality of running 30+ AI agents in production across multiple companies.\nThe conversation quickly turned to all the issues no one really talks about when it comes to scaling agent usage. And I wanted to write this up as a deeper dive into what is going on behind the scenes for those that want more than just vendor marketing.\nWhat You Really Need To Know About Running 30+ AI Agents in Production\nThe Reality of Scaling Agent Usage: What Nobody Talks About\nI recently participated in an online panel with several other SaaStr founders, and we started discussing the reality of running 30+ AI agents in production across multiple companies. The conversation quickly turned to all the issues no one really talks about when it comes to scaling agent usage.\nThe truth is that while everyone’s talking about “we’re using so many amazing AI tools,” most people have only scratched the surface of what this technology can actually do at scale. And here are just a few examples:\nWhy We Had To Write 3,000 Lines Of Code For Our Sales Agent\nWe had to write thousands of lines of code for our sales agent because none of the existing tools could handle even half that complexity.\nOur marketing AI agents can only send an email once every four days. They crash otherwise. And we have no idea why. We’ve tried fixing it ourselves, but the vendor has been unresponsive and hasn’t provided a fix yet despite multiple requests.\nWe’re forced to use three separate “agents” for each of our different SaaS companies because none of them can handle the complexity across all platforms simultaneously.", "words": 291, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We’ve got about 20+ AI agents working for us now at SaaStr, and we’re scaling up fast.\nA few learnings from what’s worked:\nFirst, don’t make your first agent an enterprise product. It will be too complicated to train or use. Do a simple app that just does one thing well.\nSecond, keep it in production 24×7, not as a QA tool. I think this is why so many fail. They want them to work “great” — but they never really do until you keep them going all the time. Every AI agent has its own “vibe” that takes weeks of daily use to get right.\nThird, don’t expect it to be perfect at launch\nAI agents are always getting better and more polished over a few releases.\nAnd 4th — and most importantly:\nDon’t try to deploy an AI app yourself. Get someone else to do it for you. We’ve tried to build our own agent in-house from scratch, but after months of work, we still don’t have anything that works well enough.\nSo what are the top issues no one talks about?\n1. Training Time: 60-90 Days vs Promised Week\nWe were promised an “AI solution” would be working in a week. It’s now been over three months and counting. We’ve seen similar stories from other companies.\n2. Data Quality Issues (Still)\nEven after all these months, the data quality issues persist. The AI agents are still making stuff up or pulling incorrect information. And we can’t afford to constantly", "words": 252, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "x", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "The truth no one is sharing about deploying multiple enterprise-grade AI agents\nWe've built 15+ agent platforms, and I just got back from our latest deep dive with Gorgias' CTO, who's building a $100m+ B2B AI company. The reality? It doesn't get easier when you go from 1 to 30+ agents like we have now. What nobody talks about:\nData quality problems persist\nEvery agent still needs high-quality data training. This doesn’t magically improve as you deploy more agents—each one requires precise, relevant input.\nAgent-specific skills matter most\nDon’t assume your $50k+ AI agent can handle everything that was working for your other agent at half the price. Most don’t actually “learn” from each other—they develop", "words": 116, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "talk", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About\nAI is everywhere now, and it’s becoming a real game-changer for businesses. But while we’ve got a ton of experience with these agents,\nthey’re not all perfect\n.\nI wanted to talk about our top five issues because they’ll likely be similar for you too. And here are the 5 Top Issues We Have With Our AI Agents:\n1.  It doesn’t always work like it’s supposed to. This may seem obvious, but we find we have to retrain every single agent at least once a quarter in our production environment.\n2. The more complex your data is — the more time and effort you’ll need\n. We’ve got 30+ agents now across multiple products, but they all use different data structures. And some of these are pretty old. So we had to retrain each one for its specific data structure. It’s not fun.\n3.  The best ones aren’t the cheap ones. I think we paid something like $500/month total for our first 5 agents. But now? That was a quarter ago. And those prices have gone up, sometimes significantly. We pay about $1k-$2k per month each on average today. More than 10x what they were even just 12 months ago.\n4.  You’ll need to deal with constant “version updates.” These are basically forced upgrades that update the agents so you don’t have to do it yourself, but there’s a lot of overhead and issues here. So be prepared for this.\n5.  It doesn’t always work like it’s supposed to. I know we already talked about this one, but it’s true 2x over in production. That’s why we’ve got so many agents now. Because the first ones didn’t quite do what they were promised\n.\n\nWhat if you had more budget and better resources? What would happen then?\n\nWell — it wouldn’t be perfect still, but you’d have a lot fewer issues with AI agents than today.", "words": 333, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "blog", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "So, a lot of folks I meet are still struggling with what to do today. And even worse, where to invest.\nThere’s a few simple answers here:\nYou’ve got to go upmarket\nThis is the #1 answer for all but maybe a handful of exceptions in SaaS these days. The average $2k ACV of public SaaS companies has grown 30%+ since 2020. Yes, it gets harder as you get bigger.\nBut if there’s anything that’s getting easier … it’s getting more enterprise customers and getting them to pay for the software. Just look at Atlassian being worth a stunning $100B today with its core product only now approaching $1 Billion in ARR:\nYou’ve got to go multi-product\nIf you’re not going upmarket, then the next step is multi-products.\nMost of us can’t just build one more feature and get 10x growth. That’s why no one does it anymore 🙂\nBut we all can start building a second product that goes deeper into an industry than our first product:\nWe do this because even if our core doesn’t grow at the same rate, you still have to keep growing in software.\nAnd going multi-product is a way to do that with less incremental stress on your team and management. And it’s also a lot easier when you’re already upmarket or close to it. Why? Because then you can just sell more of what your customers are buying anyway. The bar for success here isn’t really all that high.\nAtlassian is now worth $100 Billion with two products:\nOne, the Atlassian product suite at $800m+ in ARR\nAnd secondly, a brand new “$500m”+ ARR product called ‘OpsGenie’ it bought 2 years ago (and then rebranded as OpsGenie) … and is now worth almost half of what Atlassian itself was two years ago!\nThat’s the power of multi-product. It works just about everywhere, if you do it right.\nYou’ve got to go more enterprise\nThis one I know will sound counter-intuitive — but it really isn’t at all today in software:\nIf your core product is struggling to grow … then make sure that a portion of your products are going deeper into an industry.", "words": 362, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "Digital Ocean is up 11% this year and has been growing like gangbusters as an alternative to AWS for a while now\nBut it still only does about $4B ARR, which really doesn’t change much in the grand scheme of things\nStill though, if you’re not at least thinking about your competition — then that’s on you\nI know some folks aren’t fans of Digital Ocean. But I’m a fan of competition. It forces everyone to innovate faster.\nA bit more here:\nAnd don't make excuses for why you're down 24% this year", "words": 93, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "x", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "So I was doing a deep dive on all my favorite public SaaS and Cloud companies last night. And I saw something that is just so different from the rest of software. DigitalOcean, which has been around since almost forever now — it’s up\n12% in 2026! This is an outlier.\n\nWhat we are seeing more than ever these days is a bifurcation of markets and stock prices:\n\nThe Winners: These are companies that have seen their revenues go up dramatically over the past few years. And not just by some small margin, but really dramatically higher year-over-year.\n\nDigitalOcean itself has gone from $0 in 2012 to $1.3 billion run-rate today! What a journey!\n\nThese winners have done so well that they are seeing massive growth in their stock", "words": 129, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "talk", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "so I just got back from our annual SaaStr AI London and we had over 1,000 folks there with a thousand more attending virtually so it was big and I wanted to do one last deep dive into some data that's interesting on the stock market at least and what happens when you have this sort of classic battle between traditional software and new age infrastructure now look most people don't know about digital Ocean but for those who are in tech they probably will be familiar with it it is an infrastructure as a service or IaaS which means they're basically like Amazon Web Services but more developer focused so if you want to build your own cloud today on AWS Azure Google Cloud or even something like Digital Ocean that's the space and digital ocean has been around for 10 years now and we've had some great guests from there including CEO Ben Upton here is a picture of him with me at SAS London last year but what I wanted to do was look at their stock price because they're interesting and they are up over 12 percent this year that's pretty impressive compared to the rest of software which is down about 24.", "words": 207, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "It's just so hard right now for most folks.\nThe market is down. Many of us are struggling with growth and even cash flow. And it feels like no matter what we do, there's a new SaaS app launching each week that does exactly the same thing as our product.\nSo should you quit? Maybe not today. But maybe next year.\nA few thoughts:\nThe \"easy\" days in B2B are over\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚫️💙 (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 9, 2026\nFirst off — SaaS isn’t dead per se. You can still buy a ton of SaaS apps today.\nBut … it’s not the same as it was even just 12 months ago. It hasn’t been easy for many B2B founders and execs since early 2025. The market has changed in so many ways. A few thoughts on how to move forward:\nSaaS is still a great space\n— I said this last year, but it’s worth saying again.\nBut the way you used to win? That’s gone. SaaS isn’t dead per se — and we are all still buying tons of software today. But so much has changed in just 12 months that if your playbook hasn’t evolved — then your results probably haven’t either.\nMore competition than ever\n— It seems like every week there is a new SaaStr Fund portfolio company, or some new AI startup launching that does exactly what you do!\nLess money to spend\n— If the macro environment doesn’t improve soon, it’s hard for folks at all levels of organizations to budget as much software. And less confidence.\nMore scrutiny and transparency expected\n— This is a trend we’ve been seeing in B2B now for 5+ years — but it has accelerated even further since last year.", "words": 294, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "So what's new?\n\nSaaS isn't dead. The number of unicorns keeps growing. Atlassian has 6,000 employees.\n\nBut:\n\nThe way you used to win is gone. What that means for founders and execs right now:\n\n1️⃣ Nobody needs another vendor anymore\n2️⃣ AI agents are better at the core tasks than humans\n3️⃣ The market is flooded with similar products\n\nWhat it means for new entrants: 🚀 Be radically different. Not just “AI version of what we used to do.”\n\nBe 10x more valuable and easier to use.\n\nDo that, and you might have a chance in this saturated B2B landscape.\n\nThe best AI agents are better than the humans at the core tasks, so your job is to make it ridiculously easy for customers to deploy them. Not hard. Just click. Boom.\n\nAnd then:\n\nMake sure what they build with all these capabilities isn't just \"another version of our old software\".", "words": 151, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "X Post:\nSaaS isn't dead per se\nBut it's fundamentally changed over the past 10 years\nWhat used to work?\n1v1 sales + low ACV + SMB focused\nThe classic playbook for building a $20M ARR company in SaaS worked from '11-‘16. It still does today, but only if you’re at $2B+ now and have all the leverage of scale.\n\nIt’s not dead. But it’s fundamentally changed over the past 10 years\nWhat used to work?\n1v1 sales + low ACV + SMB focused\nThe classic playbook for building a $20M ARR company in SaaS worked from '11-‘16. It still does today, but only if you’re at $2B+ now and have", "words": 111, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "So, so many folks are freaking out today about AI and more.  And I get it.\nBut you gotta take a step back here\nWe’ve been doing this for 20 years now.  We’re not going to change the rules of SaaS on a whim.  Maybe some parts will go away (or at least evolve) — but overall, we need 100s and 1,000s of new SaaS companies every year just to keep up with demand\nWhat’s really changed is how you win in B2B today.\nLet me break it down for you here.  How did we used to win?  And why this doesn’t work anymore:\n#1 — Being “good” isn’t good enough any more\nWe’re all using AI now and the tools are getting better every day, even if not quite as fast as they seem.  In fact, it’s still a bit of a mess out there today in terms of quality — but at least you can see some progress.\nA few months ago, I used to use 2 SaaS apps together\nNow AI does it for me\nAnd the tool is better than either of them\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is back!!✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 21, 2026\nBut here’s what’s changed: “good” doesn’t get you far anymore.  It never really did in SaaS, but now it barely gets you a meeting with the prospect.\n#2 — Being cheap isn’t good enough any more\nWe used to win by being cheaper and cheaper than our competitors.  This was the playbook for years.  But that’s no longer working today.  Because AI has changed everything.\nI recently tried to get an old vendor I’ve known forever for 15+ years to switch from a competitor to their tool.  Their “new” product is way better, and they wanted me to pay a premium for it.\nMy answer?  You want $200k/yr more than the competition?\nNo thanks.", "words": 312, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "I was on an investor call recently where I had to update some numbers for our latest\nSaaStr Fund investment.\nThe founder asked me if things were “back to normal” now that we are back in a growth phase.\nIn one sense, yes.\nBut not as simple as just getting back to normal. The last 18 months have fundamentally changed everything. We’ve all been forced into the Cloud and SaaS. For better or worse. And it’s working for many of us now. Not everyone is growing at Figma’s incredible rate — but we are seeing a lot more growth in B2B and Cloud than just 12-15 months ago.\nAnd one thing I do know from doing this for almost two decades: when things get back to normal, they often don’t go back exactly as it was before. We’ve all been forced into the Cloud and SaaS over the last few years. The “back” is a bit different than what we had pre-Covid.\nNow Figma has just hit $304m in a single quarter — growing an incredible 40% year-over-year at this scale! And it’s not alone. We’re seeing growth of similar magnitude from Box (39%), Atlassian (38%), and Databricks (50%). While these numbers are eye-popping, they also mask the fact that many companies have struggled to maintain even modest growth over the past 18 months.\nBut Figma is different — it’s not just a design tool. It’s a platform for teams. And what we’ve learned from Figma and others in this space is that when you’re building something truly collaborative, you can defy expectations of traditional SaaS metrics.\nThe real question isn’t whether the market has turned back to growth (it clearly has) — but how sustainable it will be\nThere’s still a lot of uncertainty.", "words": 295, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma just hit $304,000,000 ARR in Q3’23\nGrowing at 41% YoY\nThat’s the fastest growth it has seen since its IPO last year.\nSaaStr #1 on Twitter and YouTube:\nHow Figma Just Hit Its Fastest Growth Ever. At Age 12! | SaaStr (@saastr)\n#2 on LinkedIn: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That —", "words": 61, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "x", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "SaaStr AI Weekly #12\nFigma:\n$3B+ valuation\n$1B ARR\nGrowing 40%\nJust crossed $300m TCV, the most ever for Figma, and growing at a record rate.\nThis Is The New Benchmark For B2B Software\nThe market is back. But it’s not just back—it’s on steroids. The stock market has already priced in the new reality:\nCloudflare: Up 80% this year (from $40 to $72)\nFigma: Trading at 20x ARR, valuing them at a staggering $3B+\nWhat This Means For Founders\nIf you’re building software right now—don’t be shy about asking for more. The market is paying premium prices for top-tier B2B companies", "words": 102, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma just hit $304 million in revenue in Q1—up from $226m last year—a growth rate that’s now accelerating to 39% year-over-year, as the company continues its remarkable journey and it seems like a lot of other folks are joining it on this path.\nThis is not some startup or new kid anymore. Figma is an $8 billion+ IPO with almost\n$1 Billion in ARR\nand has been around since 2012! It’s a real, legitimate SaaS company that everyone knows and uses — but nobody really expected to be growing nearly 40% year-over-year.\nFolks are still arguing over whether AI or Cloud is going to save SaaS. But here's the thing: it doesn't matter if you're in AI, B2B, B2C, whatever—\nif your growth rate is accelerating today,\nyou should be celebrating.\n\nIf you’re growing 40% year-over-year as Figma just did, even at $1 Billion+ ARR — that’s a huge win. You are the best of what SaaS has to offer.\nAnd here's the real kicker: it looks like we're about to see a lot more of this happening in B2B and enterprise software over the next 24 months.\n\nWe've got:\nSamsara just IPO'd at $10 Billion\nand growing +37%\nWix is up over 100% on its AI news, and already has $1.5 billion ARR.\nCloudflare announced it's hitting $3 BILLION in annualized revenue,\nup from $2.6B a year ago—a 15% increase in just one quarter!\nZoom hit an incredible $7 Billion ARR milestone\nand is growing like crazy.\nWe've got all these massive, established SaaS companies that are still going gangbusters — and the startups are starting to see this too.\n\nSo I don't know if we're seeing a \"SaaS renaissance.\"", "words": 281, "stop": "", "early": false, "finished": true}], "off": [{"id": "gold_001", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "So I had a chance meeting recently with a very senior IT leader at one of the world’s largest companies.\nA few months back, they’d decided on their new CRM and other business process app strategy for the next decade or so.  And it was … Salesforce again.\nBut this time, he wasn’t just buying from sales. He bought directly from the CEO.\nI asked how that worked and what happened.\n“Why did you buy direct?” I asked?\nHe said: “Because we wanted someone who would take our social contract seriously.”\nThat really stood out as a phrase.\n“What do you mean by ‘social contract’,” I asked?\n“You know, like the Founding Fathers signed a social compact” he replied. “They took an oath to uphold certain principles and standards when they governed America. We want vendors that will sign up to those same contracts. It’s not enough to have your logo and be certified or whatever. Those are table stakes now. But we need folks that will commit to making sure everything works right, no matter what happens.”\nWow.\nThis is something almost none of us think about these days. We’re all thinking about PLG (product-led growth) and AI agents and more. But there are still many customers where signing up for a serious social contract matters.\nIf nothing else, if you sell to enterprises, make sure you understand what that means. Make sure every stakeholder has skin in the game, and everyone knows it.\nIt can lead to real loyalty over decades.\nAnd even to being able to close deals where you aren’t the only vendor. Or even the cheapest.\nSometimes, having fewer features is actually a competitive advantage in larger enterprise deals.", "words": 284, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "x", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "I've been doing this for a while, and I have learned one thing that is pretty interesting. And it's counterintuitive.\nI find as you go from $1 million in ARR up to about $10 million or so in ARR, things get more efficient, but they don't really change all that much. It gets harder every year at least because there are more people to manage and there are more stakeholders, but fundamentally, it doesn't change that much.\nBut what does change dramatically after $10 million in ARR is how customers buy software. They start buying enterprise software very differently than before. Now, you're probably thinking, \"Well, we just sell on price.\" No, no, no. That's not true at all. There are two things going on here that make me want to scream when I see sales reps selling to enterprises who haven't sold to them before. First, buyers will give your product far more leeway if they trust you. That sounds obvious, right? Well, let me tell you what most startups do wrong with their marketing. Most startups think they can be trusted by talking about how great they are. What they need to do is talk about how much money they'll save you, which you might not even know until you use their product. This is the mistake that SaaS companies often make. We try to position ourselves as a solution for pain points we may not even understand yet. Let me tell you something that has worked incredibly well. We've done some case studies where we went out and interviewed our prospects ahead of time, found out exactly what was bugging them, then put those problems into a white paper that talked about the problem, the issue, and why they were losing money due to this particular problem. Then we sent it over to the prospect ahead of time and said, \"Hey, check this out and call us back.\"\nWhat happens? Every single time—every single time—the buyer calls back within minutes.", "words": 333, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So we’ve done deep dives on many Cloud leaders over the years.  But one I’d like to do more often is Monday.com.\nMonday.com has built something truly great — an incredible platform that’s not only highly usable and loved by its customers (and employees), but also incredibly efficient and profitable as well.  It’s now approaching $500m in ARR growing 30%+ … with 23% operating margins!\nAnd it’s worth almost $10 Billion (!).\nLet’s dig in:\n#1.  Almost Half Their Revenue Comes From Outside North America\nA reminder that SaaS can work just fine without being U.S.-centric if you want to go global early.\n#2.  A Very Capital Efficient Engine.  And Profitable.\nThis may be our #1 learning here.  With 1000s of SMB customers paying mostly monthly, it doesn’t take much capital to acquire them.  So while most public software companies are trading at 7x-8x revenue or higher, Monday.com trades at just 6x revenues.  Less than half of HubSpot.\nAlso importantly, they don’t need massive sales teams to close their deals.  In fact, their CRO told me he thinks his team is already too big.  The self-service motion works really well for Monday.\nIt’s pretty efficient.  They spend less than 25 cents per dollar on sales & marketing, compared to 35%-40% for other high growth SaaS leaders.\n#3.  $300k Customers Growing Quickly.  But Smaller Ones Are Even Faster\nWe saw this recently with Asana as well, but again, it shows how critical your smallest customers are in driving growth.  Yes, $250k ACV customers are key to Monday’s engine.  But so far, even smaller ones have grown faster.  That makes sense given how easy Monday is to use.", "words": 276, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500m+ ARR #1. “Enterprise” is the growth engine for Monday.com It’s growing 50% vs just 20% overall The other day we took a look at Asana, which also has been hurt by its own macro issues and slowdowns. But it was interesting to see that while Monday is growing far more slowly than Asana (just 20% year-over-year), its Enterprise customers are still growing a stunning 49%. That means SMBs are seeing big headwinds today, but enterprises aren’t. They’re still investing in SaaS. And they’re investing even more in workflow apps like Monday. #2. GRR remains very strong — up to almost 110% Monday doesn’t have any net churn anymore. Its NPS is +40. And the average customer pays about as much on Year 7-8 as they do in Year 1. Wow! #3. Customer count still growing 22%, despite slower revenue growth This isn’t always easy when your new deals get smaller and harder to close. But what it suggests is many existing customers are buying not just one module or app … but multiple ones. So Monday gets a boost there from account expansion. #4. Monday uses sales reps to drive adoption, and support for the rest Sales can be an important part of driving usage and adoption for some products. Not all of them. But if you want to get folks using your product every single day, it makes sense to use sales. The rest, they mostly solve with self-service and", "words": 248, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500m+ ARR", "words": 8, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So we've talked quite a bit on SaaStr about Monday and its CEO Eran Zinman since it was one of our first public company portfolio investments.\nAnd today it's crossing almost $600m ARR.  And growing a stunning 40% at that scale.\nWow.\n\nBut what can we learn?  How do they still grow so fast at almost $600m in ARR?\n\nA few interesting learnings:\n\n#1.  50% of their customers pay less than $10k a year\n.  This is pretty common for many SMB leaders like Shopify, ZoomInfo, etc., but perhaps not as much for some others like HubSpot or Squarespace that are more enterprise-focused in their customer base.  A reminder to go long with your smallest customers if you want them to be happy, even if they often churn faster.\nMonday has 230,000+ customers paying an average of just over $2,500 each.\nThat's pretty impressive efficiency at this scale!\n\n#2. 30% of revenue outside U.S.\nAnother good lesson to see Monday doing well globally.  It does have offices around the world, but it doesn't seem to focus there per se.  Many folks don't realize how global Monday actually is -- and I think they'll benefit here going forward.  The world is moving to work together again post-Covid, at least partially.\nThis is another good reminder to Go Global Early if possible.\n\n#3.  120% NRR overall; 140% from larger accounts.\nWe saw similar results with Smartsheet when it IPO'd (which was acquired by Adobe) and also with Asana which got to 100% NRR for a while before growth slowed.  But Monday shows us that high NRR isn't dead!   While most SaaS companies struggle to get above 110%-120% NRR these days, Monday gets there by focusing on adding value to its existing customer base.  So far at least, it hasn't been enough to propel it past 40% growth at $600m in ARR.  But maybe it will help fuel the next level of growth.", "words": 320, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: What’s The Best Way To Stand Out In A Job Interview?\nA few ideas:\nShare your top mistakes. We all make them. But sharing what you’ve learned from is great.\nTalk about how you are truly better than 90%+ of others that have done this job before\n. This will always work if you can be honest and self-aware enough to do it. It doesn’t matter who you worked for or at — everyone has their flaws.\nAsk for an hour with whomever will hire you. And ask to talk to some of those already on the team doing the job\n. If they don’t want to give you time with someone other than the CEO / VP … well, run. Don’t take the role.\nBe yourself. You’ll stand out as being different\n. Be respectful but authentic. Share stories about when you succeeded, and even more importantly, share stories about when you failed. Everyone loves hearing about real failures (and success) 🙂\nShow up early\n. Show up to every interview looking sharp and rested. Send over notes ahead of time, etc.\nMake sure there’s no reason not to hire you\n. Make sure there isn’t any negative information out there. Especially today, where most recruiters use AI screening tools.\nThe best candidates don’t need to do much extra here. They just show up prepared and ready to rock.", "words": 232, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "x", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "So we've talked for years on this series and I think it's time to bring back one of our most popular sessions. It was great when Samantha Madhosingh was CRO at ZoomInfo and then later joined us as Chief Operating Officer here at SaaStr. And she did an incredible session with me that we're going to go through today called \"How To Truly Stand Out In Every Interview\". So first let's take a look what happens today in interviews.\n[00:01:18]\nThe average interviewer spends five minutes reviewing your resume before they start the interview. Five minutes. You know, there are some exceptions out there where folks like to do reference checks or have other people review resumes ahead of time, but generally speaking, you're not getting any additional help unless you're interviewing at Salesforce or somewhere else. But even if you are, those folks aren't reading your resume until after they finish talking to you. There is no one doing research on who you are or how you can solve their problems beforehand. And I'm so tired of hearing folks say, \"Oh, my recruiter told them all about me.\" That does happen sometimes. If you have a very high level recruiter that really knows you well, sometimes they'll prep the interviewer. Sometimes they will set up a meeting between the CEO and the candidate before the interview. But don't rely on that too much. Because more often than not, the person conducting your interview hasn't heard anything good about you yet. They haven't read your resume. The best way to stand out in every job interview is to immediately answer the question: \"What makes you the right fit for this role?\"\n[00:02:29]\nThat's it. Now I hear so many candidates get nervous during interviews. Their brain goes blank. They sweat. And the last thing they want to do is talk about why they'd be perfect for the role. They would rather tell the story again of why they quit their last company. Why they failed, how they were fired, or how they had to leave because the VCs wouldn't give them enough money. I mean, I get it. We all want to share these stories. But the worst way to make yourself memorable is by telling someone a story they already know. Your job is to educate the interviewer on why you're the right fit for the position. Here's how to do it. Before you show up to an interview, whether it's in-person or virtual, find out everything you possibly can about the company. Go to Glassdoor.com. Read reviews from employees. Look at the website. Find out who works there and see if anyone shares similar experiences online. This isn't just about learning facts. It's about understanding the culture. What motivates the team? What keeps them coming into work every day? Be prepared to speak specifically about how your skills align with the company's needs. Don't generalize.", "words": 487, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had an incredible one last week with Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital.\n______\nMark Roberge joined Bessemer Venture Partners as a Partner in 2019 after serving as Senior Lecturer and Founding Academic Lead for Harvard Business School’s Sales Initiative and a member of HBS’ Executive Education faculty since 2007. In addition to teaching at HBS, he has also taught at Northeastern University and Babson College. He received his PhD in business administration from Boston University’s Graduate School of Management and holds BSBA and MBA degrees from Bentley University. Prior to joining academia, he spent over two decades building sales organizations at companies such as IBM and HubSpot, where he was instrumental in scaling revenue growth during its IPO year. He sits on several boards including Pipedrive, ZoomInfo, Salesloft, Fullcast, Lattice, Asana, and Demandbase. He lives outside of Boston with his wife and three children.\n______\nWhat are you seeing right now? The number one thing that’s happening in my portfolio companies is just this extreme demand pressure — I’ve never seen anything like it. Everyone wants everything. When I talk to CEOs, they say “I’m doing so well. I have all these opportunities.” But what they’re not saying is that every customer wants more features than ever before. They want better service. And if your product isn’t there yet, or your delivery isn’t great enough, customers will churn faster than ever. It’s a huge amount of stress on all our teams.\n______\nHow do you see the competitive landscape changing?\nThere are winners in the marketplace who keep winning, but those that don’t need to evolve very quickly. If you look at the data points, many startups aren’t really innovating anymore.", "words": 312, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "x", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital\nMark is a co-founder and managing director at Stage 2 Capital. Prior to starting Stage 2, he served as the CRO of HubSpot for six years where he scaled revenue from $10M to over $200M while building one of the most successful sales organizations in SaaS. Before that, he was an associate professor at Harvard Business School. He’s currently studying how companies scale their go-to-market strategies through his “doubling down” framework.\nThe Top 5 Mistakes Made by SaaS Founders:\nNot doubling down on what works early enough\n– Many founders try too many things before they’ve found product-market fit or built a repeatable engine. They spend too much time trying to improve things rather than doubling down on what already works.\nHiring mediocre people into senior roles\n– Hiring great VPs is hard, but hiring mediocre ones only creates more problems later. The best founders are relentless about finding A+ talent because it sets them up for long-term success.\nNot being honest with investors and stakeholders\n– In today’s world, there isn’t really anyone you can hide anything from anymore. Investors and customers expect transparency, so founders who aren’t open with challenges often pay dearly when those issues become public.\nWasting precious capital on ineffective marketing tactics\n– Most B2B companies waste money on brand-building instead of focusing on driving pipeline through high-quality demand generation channels. Companies like Brex have been able to achieve incredible results using this approach.\nNot understanding your market well enough\n–", "words": 258, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So we’ve all heard of Informatica.  It’s been around since 1993 (!) and has built an incredible engine for data management.\nAnd it was founded on the way to work from … Stanford!\nBut boy — did they have a wild ride:\nFounded 1989 as Relational Solutions (Stanford)\nIPO’d in 1996 at peak tech boom times ($16/share) but then crashed hard during dot-com bust. Went private in 2005-2006.\nThen came back public again in 2015, this time a quiet one with just a single share price that day ($22). And then …\nGrew into a $13B market cap leader in Data Management\nAcquired for $15 billion in cash + stock by Private Equity firm Permira\nSold for a stunning $8 BILLION acquisition by Salesforce\nThat’s quite a rollercoaster! 😉\nAnd while Salesforce isn’t paying top dollar here, I’m sure it will be worth much more than that over time.\nA pretty epic run, even if not always a super smooth or easy one.\nIn fact, there are only so many stories like this.  That you can go from $0.01 (or less!) to $100m+ ARR once.\nIt’s tough enough getting there just once.\nOnce you do, you sort of need to keep running faster every year to grow at scale.  That often means keeping up with changing customer needs.\nWhich is what Informatica has done.  But along the way, growth slowed down after its second IPO, which led to the PE buy-out, and ultimately the sale to Salesforce today.", "words": 248, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "words": 15, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce \nhttps://forbes.com/sites/ashleyleigh/\n#business #tech", "words": 18, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So I've known this company for years.  In fact, we were one of their first customers way back when.  And so it's amazing that today they are about to be acquired by Salesforce for $8 billion.\nA few learnings:\n\nFirst, don't let anyone tell you your start-up doesn't have a chance if it's very capital intensive.   Informatica was founded way back in 1993 as an enterprise software startup and took 10+ years to get to cash-flow positive.  It took them over 20 years (!) to finally make some money.\n\nSecond, sometimes you just need to reset the clock on your start-up.   It took Informatica until Year 25 to really take off.  Not every SaaS company has to follow the traditional path.  Sometimes, you just need more time.\n\nThird, being a little too early can be OK -- even great.  They started with data integration before it was \"cool\".  But they never quit.  Fast forward to today, Data Integration is now a huge category at scale, especially for AI.  That means a second act.  A second act that will result in an $8B acquisition!\n\nWow, what a journey!", "words": 187, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "So it turns out there are a lot of you on YouTube!\nOur channel is growing quickly to over 7,500 subscribers,\nand we just crossed 5,000,000 views!!\nIt took us years, but … now they’re coming fast and furious.\nThe top videos:\n#1  “How I Built A Unicorn With No VC Money” (with Pave co-founders). This video has taken off in recent months, with over 100k views already.\n#2  “How We Got From $0 to $10m ARR In First Year For Salesloft”\n#3  “When Times Are Tough, How Do You Stay Motivated?”\n#4 “From Day 0 To IPO — The Inside Story Behind Box.com’s Journey”.  A great session from our first Annual event.\n#5 “SaaS Secrets Revealed by Aaron Levie Founder & CEO of Box.com”\n#6 “Why SaaS is Still an Amazing Career Choice”\n#7 “I Was Wrong About Everything In SaaS – Now What?”, with Harry Stebbings + me\n#8 “What Made Slack Successful”, with Stewart Butterfield, CEO Slack\n#9 “Top 10 Learnings Selling Your Company for $25 Billion”, with Atlassian Co-Founder Mike Cannon-Brookes\n#10 “A Guide to Bootstrapping vs Raising Venture Capital”, with David Sacks and more!\nAnd some other favorites that have done well:\n“Lessons from the First $20M ARR at HubSpot”.\n“The Real Truth On When And If It Makes Sense To Raise VC Capital”\n“How I Did M&A My Way”\n“A Deep Dive Into Hiring VPs That Actually Work Out. With Datadog.”\n“Dear SaaStr:  Should We Have Sales Reps or Account Executives?”\n“What Happens After An Exit: Lessons Learned from CEOs who sold their companies”\n“VC Confidential: What VCs Really Think”\n“Lessons Learned as the CEO of a Decacorn: Y Combinator’s Michael Seibel”\n“It’s OK if Customers Want to Buy Less Than Your Whole Product Suite.  It May Even Be Better.”", "words": 296, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time! A few of our top videos (and sessions) of all time on YouTube here: #1. “How to Get Funded as a Founder with Harry Stebbings” #2. “How To Scale an AI Startup with OpenAI’s Co-Founder & CEO Sam Altman + Aaron Levie, Box CEO” #3. “How to Hire Great VPs with David Sacks (Craft Ventures), Stewart Butterfield (Slack) and Drew Houston (Dropbox)” #4. “From $0 to $100m ARR Faster Than Slack: Observe’s Founders and First VC Investor at Work” #5. “How I Went From $0 to $20M ARR in One Year with SalesLoft’s Kyle Porter”. And come watch more like this LIVE at 2026 SaaStr Annual May 12-14!!", "words": 121, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views.\n\nOur Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "Hi everyone. I think we're all just trying to make sense of this crazy world right now. And I've been doing some thinking as I look back on the past few years since we started SaaStr,\nwhat can I do better? What's working well? We had our first digital event way back at the start of Covid when there was basically no one else that could do it.\nSo that was pretty cool. We did that for free for every founder out there because we knew how hard times were. But then after that, we didn't really have any other big digital events until maybe November or so of last year, which is kind of ironic given where we are today with most folks remote.\nBut my learning from that was even though many things went wrong, like everything always does with new products and new ideas, but my learning is that there are still huge opportunities if you take advantage of them.\nAnd what I learned is there are three core areas of focus for me over the next little while:\nFirst, I want to double down more than ever on helping founders, especially early stage founders who don't know how to scale yet, not just by sharing my learnings, but also by bringing in amazing guests to help teach their lessons, especially those that I haven't heard before, and bring them into your homes.\nSecondly, I want to be an incubator for VCs. There are lots of great VC firms out there, but they're often focused on late stage deals. So I'd love to be able to introduce you to some of these amazing investors that might fund your seed round or later.", "words": 285, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "So I’ve been doing this for almost 20 years now, and seen so many changes to how we sell software.  The biggest change?  It’s getting much harder than ever before.\nI know it seems like every day there is more competition out there.  That’s true.  But what you may not see as often are the internal challenges that make sales itself just plain harder:\nSales cycles stretching even longer (even at Salesforce)\n. We’re still seeing longer sales cycles today across most categories — from CRM leaders to startups to vertical SaaS. This isn’t new, but it has gotten worse over time. Deals take even longer to close than they used to. And the number of leads, prospects, and opportunities that go nowhere is up, too.  More on both points here:\nThe #1 Reason You’ll Fall Behind In SaaS Today Isn’t Competition. It’s Your Team. | SaaStr\nYes, There Are Still Plenty Of Leads. They Just Aren’t Going Anywhere Anymore. | SaaStr\nSales teams shrinking faster than ever. Even at BigCos\n. Yes, AI is helping some sales teams scale better than ever.  But overall, the trend I’m seeing again and again is sales teams shrinking by 30% or more. Not all of them, but enough. Especially in companies where the CRO doesn’t fully believe in AI. When times get tougher, the first thing you cut is sales. It always was, pre-AI.  It probably will be even more so with AI. More on that here:\nYou Can’t Cut Your Way To Growth. What Every Founder Should Know About Hiring Right Now. | SaaStr\nEven worse … even when deals do happen, closing rates remain stuck around 10%-12%\n. Everyone talks about how hard it is these days to “get a meeting”. But once you have a meeting, everyone gets frustrated because the deals don’t close.  Most deals never really close anymore anyway, which can mask low conversion ratios.  If your close rate remains stuck at 10%, then something is off.  So maybe you aren’t working hard enough.  Or maybe it’s just tough out there right now.", "words": 346, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years If you’ve been selling B2B software for more than just a few years, you know that everything has changed since ChatGPT launched. And most importantly — sales is completely different now. We had to fire our head of sales and go back to founder-led sales at SaaStr itself after ChatGPT launched. It was … hard. But we didn’t want to do it. But there’s no way we could have done what we needed to do with AI without going back to founder-led sales. The same thing happened at HubSpot when they went all-in on their own AI agents (Sales AI, etc). They had to move from enterprise-focused to SMB-focused. That meant dropping many of its top reps. So this week I sat down with two leaders who are living through these changes right now. Both were leading high-performing teams before ChatGPT launched. Both lost half or more of their team members since then. One saw his entire outbound team need to be let go because AI made them obsolete. Another watched as 20% of her team quit overnight when management announced an aggressive push into smaller accounts. “I wasn’t ready for the emotional trauma,” she admitted. These aren’t isolated cases. This is happening everywhere across every company I’m working with right now. And here’s why it matters: Every single one of us is figuring this out on the fly. No one knows the playbook anymore. There’s no template for navigating what comes next. The companies winning right now aren’t necessarily", "words": 262, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "x", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "So I've been investing in and working with B2B software companies for quite some time now, since before 2005. And there have been three really big changes that we've seen over the past decade or so.\nThe first was back around 2010-11 when we all started to move from on-prem to cloud. We moved off Salesforce.com from On Demand. We moved off our own on-prem products into Cloud versions. It wasn't easy but it was something that we all had to do.\nThen came the second wave which is what happened last year where you had this massive change to AI. Not only are more and more apps getting built using AI but also more and more folks are buying them because they're just so much better than their older predecessors. This has been huge. And then right after that came the third big change.\nI'll get to that in a minute. But one thing that hasn't changed is how sales works. In fact, if anything, it's stayed pretty consistent. The best reps still close more. They know how to work multiple stakeholders. They know how to work an opportunity over months and years, not days. They understand the product inside out and backwards and can solve real problems.\nBut here's my point. All these things I mentioned, moving from on-prem to cloud, the rise of AI applications, were all additive. You could add another layer to your existing motion. You didn't need to be a different type of company. You could build a great app, sell it at scale, hire a bunch of good people, and use a lot of the same playbook as before.\nBut here comes the third big change, and it's going to require us to rethink everything. What if most of your customers never want to buy again?", "words": 303, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So there’s been a lot of worry among VCs and public market investors that public SaaS multiples are too low.\nI.e., that they’re not high enough for folks to make money investing.\nThis is true — from a purely financial perspective.  A $10B ARR company growing 30% at an average price-to-revenue multiple today might only trade around 8x or so.   That would be tough on your VC fund if you invested all the way back when it was worth $2B ARR.\nBut here’s what I think:\nPublic multiples aren’t really relevant to start-ups\n. Why not?\nFirst, most founders don’t care much about their “valuation” as long as they have plenty of runway (i.e., cash). And second, even with lower valuations … IPOs remain hot.\nIn fact, revenue has grown 27% at Adobe Sign / EchoSign since its IPO\nSquarespace grew +39%\nMonday.com grew 55%\nZoomInfo grew 63%\nDeals like this keep happening again and again.\nWhy? Because growth rates stay strong,\nAnd importantly, because the markets still love SaaS leaders that are truly dominating their space.  The ones with epic NRR and epic growth.\nIf you are one of those, then ignore the noise.  Ignore the fact that others may have fallen out of favor, or that multiples are down.\nThe best will always find a home at top prices.", "words": 221, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? We just did a deep dive on this with Harry Stebbings at SaaStr Annual + AI Summit and it’s worth watching here or reading the summary below: The market has gotten more bifurcated than ever — but there are good things happening for many B2B leaders. It may not be easy to see that right now if you’re running a company growing <60% today. But the public markets have rewarded high growth software companies like Cursor (growing at an insane rate), Palantir (even with its challenges) and more. And they’ve punished those growing much slower. If your VC is worried about public multiples being low … maybe don’t worry as much. Yes, many unicorns aren’t going to IPO anytime soon because valuations are down. But even so, if you can keep growing >80%, and importantly, >50%, I think most founders will find their valuations are still pretty solid. Even if overall multiples are lower. So let some things slide. Get back to hiring great folks. Push through the bumps. Keep pushing toward $1m ARR … then $10m ARR … then $100m ARR.", "words": 195, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "x", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So we've got some good news and bad news to share with you today.\nThe bad news is that as part of this series,\nwe did an SaaStr deep dive on what's happening in public markets right now, and it's not great for most folks.\nThe reason I wanted to do that was because if you're sitting there thinking \"Oh my god, our VC investors are worried about all these things,\" or they said something like \"We just don't have any confidence in where the market is going.\"\nAnd maybe your VCs aren't even saying that out loud, but sometimes they will say something similar to founders when they ask them how confident they are in their business.\nIf you haven't checked it lately, take a look at the overall market cap of Nasdaq versus its historical average. It's down about 40% from peak levels, which is pretty concerning.\nNow that doesn't mean every company has been hit equally hard. There are winners. But it does mean the overall multiple compression across the board, especially for growth stocks, has been significant.\nAs one example, Palantir IPO'd back in September '20 and closed at $31 billion in revenue growing at almost 50%. And that stock traded up to about $180 at the end of 2021. Today, it trades at around 5 times revenues, roughly half of what it was trading at three years ago.\nThat may be a little bit extreme, but more than that, a lot of folks were expecting multiples would stay high forever. They didn't expect that there might actually be a correction. So yeah, that can seem really scary for folks who are raising money.\nBut here's the thing: despite all of that, venture capital remains strong. The top quartile funds still raised tons of money last year. We had some record highs for dollars into software startups.\nHow could both be true?\nThey're both true because many of those new funds are getting deployed by AI companies, primarily.", "words": 332, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So we’ve been lucky to have both Slack and Atlassian as part of our SaaStr community since Day One.  Both are now public, but very different.\nSlack is growing even more rapidly than ever (albeit with an ongoing decline in growth rate) and has seen its market cap go up +70% (!) over the past year:\nAtlassian is also on fire, but for totally different reasons.  It’s added almost $1B in new bookings in just one quarter — fueled by strong demand for Jira and other products across tech, e-commerce, healthcare, manufacturing and more.   And it’s raised prices for all customers.  And that led to a stunning run-up in its stock price!  Up +28%(!) this week alone!\nBut what I find most interesting is how Atlassian continues to accelerate post-IPO … at scale.  Even today, at nearly $3 billion in ARR.\n5 Interesting Learnings:\n#1.  85% Of Their Customers Are Outside Tech\nThis is something many founders don’t get right, especially outside of developer-focused businesses like Twilio and Datadog.  If you’re not primarily focused on developers or fintech, if your product isn’t “SaaS” per se … maybe try selling into non-tech companies first.  Atlassian started there, and they still do.\n#2.  33% of Revenue From Outside U.S., But Still Growing 30%\nAtlassian had a lot of success early on selling to global markets because English was the primary language used for development.  Today, while only 33% of their revenue comes from outside North America, that’s still growing a healthy 30%.\n#3.  90% GRR Across All Customer Segments, Including SMBs\nWe all strive for 100% retention, but sometimes settle for 80%-85%.  Not Atlassian.  They maintain 90%+ GRR across every segment of customer.  That’s pretty impressive.\n#4.  25,000+ Employees, So About $120k Per Employee\nThe Cloud is so efficient these days, even in engineering-heavy environments.  This doesn’t mean every employee needs to be profitable.  But it does show how much more efficient the Cloud can be.", "words": 324, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR We’ve been huge fans for years, and just because they crossed almost $2B in ARR it doesn’t mean we are any less excited. Because even today … they’re still growing an epic 29%! And importantly, the growth is broad-based across all their core products, with all three core product lines growing double digits: 📈 Cloud: +31%, up to ~$1.6B run-rate (half their total revenue) 📈 Jira Software: +24% (still #1 by far), up to ~$1.1B run-rate 📈 Confluence: +21%, up to ~$600m run-rate — And that’s not all. They also have strong enterprise customers now making bigger deals than ever before, pushing NRR up to 114%. Wow. 👉 “We’ve closed over 100 TCV transactions this year already… We had one customer sign a deal worth $10 million annually” Atlassian CEO Scott Farquhar That’s really what you want to see as you approach $3B in ARR or more. That you can close big customers … on top of your existing high growth rate. Now Atlassian isn't perfect -- its stock has fallen significantly since its peak due to market impacts -- but boy it's still growing like a weed. And look how efficient it remains. It's only spending 8% of its revenues on sales & marketing. Boy... when you scale efficiently... magic happens. A bit more here: 👇", "words": 228, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "words": 11, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So there are two parts to this story. The first is that we're just going back on some learnings from Atlassian and I've had an opportunity to work with them for many years, since almost the beginning.\nAnd one thing you'll know if you don't know already, Atlassian has been a great SaaS company, but it's not really a rocketship. It was founded way back in 2002, but it took off around 2010, when its revenue started growing rapidly.\nBut then, the growth slowed down dramatically around 2017-18 or so. And they were stuck at this nice steady pace after that, even as other leaders accelerated past them.\nNow today, Atlassian is approaching almost $3 billion in ARR, growing nicely at 20%. But importantly, it's also seen significant change -- the deceleration has reversed. They've gone more enterprise than ever before, they have new products like Cloud Code and much higher NRR than before, and their stock price is up a stunning +110% over the last year!\nWhat happened? Well, one big reason was COVID. As you can see, Atlassian saw a huge spike in usage during the lockdowns in early 2020. We all did, because everyone wanted to work together remotely. That led to a surge in adoption, especially among SMBs. But now that trend seems to be reversing itself again, as folks return to offices. So yes, times remain challenging for many software companies, but not for everyone. Some are seeing acceleration instead of deceleration.\nThe second part of this story is what we're doing here today which is sharing my top five interesting learnings from Atlassian as it approaches almost $3B in ARR:\n#1.  $100k+ Customers Now Represent 68% Of Revenue vs. Just 25% In 2019\nThis chart shows how Atlassian has become far more enterprise over time, selling to $100k+ customers and overall driving NRR well above 130%.\nAtlassian's largest customer segment is still smaller accounts under $10K ACV, though those only make up 24% of their total customers.", "words": 333, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "blog", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "Q:  I’m an SMB SaaS company with relatively high churn and low net retention. I’ve been growing my revenue at a good rate, but if your Net Retention Rate (NRR) is below 100% … you’ll run out of room.\nYou will grow faster than average for a while — because there are so many more customers you can get. But eventually, it all runs out. And then growth decelerates dramatically. It’s hard enough to hit $10m ARR with even a mediocre product. You may well be able to do better with say a 120%-130% NRR … if you have something great. Even without being truly enterprise-grade. But if your NRR is too low, you won’t really make it past that first initial phase. So what should we do?\nA few thoughts:\nFirst, don’t overindex on “churn reduction” as your top goal. That sounds easy, but it isn’t. The best way to reduce churn is to increase net negative churn. Get them spending more money, not just keeping their credit cards in their wallets. This means focusing most of your energy here after $1m-$2m ARR or so, when you have enough data to see which accounts you can upsell into. Second, stop blaming everyone else for your problems\n. Your competitors often aren’t doing much better. Third, focus on increasing ACV per customer. A bit more here:\nHow To Increase Your Average Customer Value Per Account | SaaStr\nFourth, find some budget to invest back in sales & marketing\n. At least 20% of any incremental cash flow. Yes, this means less investment in engineering. Maybe even zero new features until you’ve grown enough to justify a larger team. Fifth, push through it. In fact, once you’ve crossed Initial Traction ($5-8m+ ARR), lean in even harder now. Every dollar invested will pay off. Sixth, challenge yourself to improve NRR by X%. Not by going from -12% to +6%, but go from -11% to -9%, for example. Or maybe from 85% to 90%.", "words": 331, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "words": 8, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "x", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "words": 8, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "talk", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "So we've talked for many years on SaaStr about how you have to be aware of your NPS, and I know this is not new. And when I see folks that don't really care about it, they just ignore it, I think I want to spend a little time talking about what happens if you're an NRR zombie. So there's one thing where you can hide from bad NPS or low NPS, and that's by growing very rapidly because once you get into the hundreds and thousands of customers, eventually churn will sort of average out. But one place you cannot hide is with NRR, net revenue retention. You can try to hide it sometimes, but ultimately you'll find out. The customer growth rate has slowed down, the ACV is gone up so much. But even then, you'll start to figure it out after $100m, maybe $200m ARR, depending on how fast things are growing at least, you'll start to see these issues come to light. Now, if you don't like the results you're seeing here, and you want to do something about them, first understand why you got into this situation. Because otherwise you won't fix anything. First off, I'm going to tell you the reasons I hear most often why folks end up with NRR zombies. It's either they hire a VP of Sales who only cares about hitting numbers, or they hire a VP of Product who doesn't listen to the customers. And both of those VPs make sense on paper, right? They bring in a lot of experience. We all need to hit our number. We all need to grow faster. And they kind of work for a while. But when you go long, if you hire someone whose primary focus is making money, no matter what, they might give you some great ideas.", "words": 308, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: My CFO is very polished, and when we have board meetings he has all our financials ready to go … except burn rate planning. He’s missed it almost every month.\nWhat should I do?\nFirst — don’t panic or make an issue out of this yet\n.\nBurn rate planning can be done monthly, quarterly, even annually, just not on Day Zero as part of your first financial plan for each year.  And it may well need to evolve over time.\nSecond — ask what he needs from you to improve his process here\n.\nIs there more data needed than he currently gets? Is the current model too simplistic? Does he need help finding better ways to track cash flow and predict expenses? Make sure he knows that he has your support in improving these processes.\nThird — encourage him to lean into any mistakes he makes with confidence.\nIt’s OK if he misses projections sometimes. It’s how he learns and improves the forecasting process. The key is transparency and accountability.\nFinally — be prepared to jump in if necessary\n.\nIf you notice a pattern of consistent missing forecasts, it might be worth having an open conversation about whether they’re aligned properly. But start by giving them space to learn and iterate.\nA related post here:\nHow to Build Your First Real Financial Model | SaaStr", "words": 230, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? This is an almost universal issue with all VPs / C-level execs that aren't founders and/or engineers, especially if they come from big companies. They just don’t really know how to build software or code. And thus … they rarely do it themselves anymore. Instead, they rely on others — often folks with far less experience than them — to manage data systems, databases, etc. And they tend not to have enough domain expertise here. So you get “polish” without the underlying system being as robust as it could be. In any event, this isn’t unique to your situation, nor a sign of incompetence. It’s part of why most start-ups hire VPs/CROs/CMO/etc., so they can focus on what matters (i.e., revenue growth). So my advice would be to give your CFO more support—maybe even hire someone who can help him/her with these tasks. You’re also going to need to trust her/him more, and let her/him take ownership for some things where she/he has strong opinions. The key thing is to make sure everyone aligns on the plan. That will probably mean getting the burn rate projections nailed down weekly instead of monthly—it should only take a few hours to prepare those. If she’s having trouble projecting cash flow accurately, maybe bring in another finance professional who specializes in startup financial planning. But overall, remember that hiring experienced people means trusting them with certain responsibilities", "words": 256, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?\n\nIf your burn rate is out of control, you have to do something about it. Here’s what you should do:\n\nFirst, get clear on where your cash actually stands. Not just what the CFO says — actual numbers from your bank account or a more detailed financial model that includes everything.\n\nNext, if your burn is accelerating (which seems to be the case), ask for a real plan with specific actions and metrics. For example, “We need to reduce our monthly burn by $X over the next Y months.”\n\nThen make sure they put their money where their mouth is. If they don’t deliver results, start looking at other options.\n\nAnd finally,", "words": 134, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But We Are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should You Do?\nFirst, make sure your burn rate plan is very simple and easy to understand.\nIf you have an experienced finance person on board, they should be able to create this for you. If not, find one ASAP.  More on that here:\nBurn Rate Planning Is Critical | SaaStr\nhttps://t.co/mnW7jXfZpD\n— SaaStr (@saastr)\nMay 30, 2018\nSecond, review it every week now with her or him (or both) until she gets it right.\nThird, if you don’t see improvement over time — move on from them.\nYes, polish matters, but missing key metrics does not benefit anyone.", "words": 118, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR?\nAI SDRs can be deployed relatively quickly, especially if you’re starting from scratch. Here’s what I’d expect:\nWeek 1–2: Setup and Training (5–10 hours)\nYou’ll need to upload your CRM data, website content, and any relevant documentation. This is usually pretty straightforward, but it requires some elbow grease upfront.\nWeek 2–6: Iteration Phase (20+ hours)\nThis is where most people fail. They set something up, send a few emails, get mediocre results, and give up. The best approach is to treat this like product development. You’ll need to iterate constantly based on response rates, email quality scores, and campaign performance. For example, our first iteration took us just 30 days to go from $0 to over $100K MRR with AI SDRs. We kept iterating until we hit our target. Week 7+: Maintenance Mode (Ongoing)\nOnce you’ve found the right configuration, maintenance becomes much easier. You’ll spend more time monitoring outputs, adjusting settings as needed, and scaling campaigns. But the foundation work happens during those early months.\nThe real challenge isn’t technical—it’s human. Your team needs to understand that deploying an AI SDR effectively takes significant investment upfront. It’s not a plug-and-play solution. You need dedicated resources who are willing to learn how these tools actually work and invest the necessary time for proper training and optimization.", "words": 228, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? This is one reason I’ve pushed hard on our sponsors and partners over the past year or so to deploy an agent. Why? Because they are getting better every week, and it’s really not that hard anymore. And deploying your own tool takes too long. Not if you have help from the vendor. But most importantly, because there’s no excuse for not having deployed at least one sales & marketing automation agent by now. Here’s what you need to do: Pick any leading AI GTM agent (Artisan, Qualified, Monaco, Agentforce etc) — pick any good one. Reach out to them directly. Tell them you want to deploy in <30 days. They will set up everything themselves. From training to data mapping. They’ll even train their model with all your data themselves. You just give them access to your CRM and website. No excuses here anymore. If you don’t have time … well then maybe you aren’t ready to scale yet. Or hire someone who can. If you tell me this doesn’t work … that’s OK. That means I’m wrong, but it also tells me why we never heard back after you signed up! 😉", "words": 206, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?\n\nThe real answer:\n\n6-9 months. That’s from a B2B perspective.\n\nIt takes time for your AI SDRs to really learn and be trained well enough to handle complex sales conversations, qualify leads effectively, and build trust with prospects.\n\nHere’s what I’d expect during those first few months:\n\nMonths 1-2:\nAI will make mistakes on outreach sequences, qualification criteria, and response handling.\nYou’ll need to manually correct it constantly until it gets better at recognizing patterns.\n\nMonths 3-5:\nImprovements start showing up – better targeting, more effective follow-ups, fewer errors.\nBut you’re still spending significant time managing it daily.\n\nMonths 6+:\nYour AI SDR", "words": 115, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR?\nIt can be done today.  We did it last year.\nThe key is you have to start with what already works, and build off that.  You probably already use HubSpot or Salesforce for your CRM, Marketo (or something similar) as your marketing automation platform, etc.  So you don’t need to buy anything new — at least not right away.  At least not if you are starting small.\nBut you do need someone on your team to manage it every day, especially during the first few months. The agent won't magically work without training.\nHere's our simple 5-step plan:\nStep 1: Pick One Platform\nYou can try different ones, but we started with Qualified\nbecause they've been doing this longer than anyone else and their product has been more mature from Day 0. That doesn't mean it wasn't still a lot of work.\nStep 2: Start Small—One Use Case\nPick one thing you want to automate. For us, it was outbound follow-up emails to prospects who'd visited our website but hadn't converted yet. We wanted to send them a series of automated emails instead of waiting weeks for human sales reps to respond.\nStep 3: Connect Everything to Your Data Source\nLink it to your CRM. Link it to whatever data source you're using. If you're using Salesforce, HubSpot, whatever—it needs to connect. Otherwise, the AI Agent will just make stuff up, which is terrible.\nStep 4: Train the Agent Every Single Day\nThis is crucial. Every morning before breakfast, spend time with your AI Agent. Go through the leads it generated overnight. Review everything. Make sure nothing went wrong. Tell it why things were good or bad. This daily investment is non-negotiable.", "words": 294, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nI’d say yes.\nFirst, most humans don’t want to do this work.  They can’t or won’t do it well enough anyway:\nThey’ll only call and email their favorite prospects and accounts they already know;\nMost reps just send generic emails and never really research the prospect before sending them;\nFew truly learn how best to sequence follow-up cadences for different types of leads; and\nEven fewer will constantly improve and refine the scripts and messaging over time.\nAI is always improving its approach here, and doesn’t get tired, frustrated, etc.  It’s always working on improving what it does, getting better at every interaction, and learning more about your product and ICP every day.\nSo if you have any leads that aren’t great fit for a human rep — but are still worth following up with — use an AI agent instead.\nIt’s almost always better than nothing.  And much less expensive than trying to train someone who isn’t going to do it right.\nAnd importantly, there’s no reason not to run both in parallel until you see which performs better.\nThere’s zero cost in running both an AI and a human SDR in parallel for now.  There may be some marginal overlap (the AI might reach out to folks your human SDRs also call), but overall the volume should be manageable.\nThe bottom line is, don’t let perfect being the enemy of good.  The vast majority of start-ups don’t even try outbound sales because it seems too hard.  But they miss the chance to drive in some qualified leads and revenue from the contacts in the database.\nIf you’ve got something that works with AI … roll it out today.  Then later add humans into the mix once you need more personalization, more nuance, and/or when you have a team big enough to make it all scale.\nBut don’t wait for perfection.", "words": 328, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion? Probably not. 🤖AIs are great at what they do, but it’s much better to train and deploy them on top of something that already works. And as part of your team, with lots of training. The best AIs can sound almost human. But you still have to teach them, again and again. It will take some time to get there. If nothing else, wait until you have someone who is fairly good at outbound already working for you — then let them work with the AI agent. 👉If you don’t, though, go ahead and try one out first. Just know it won’t be magical. At least not right off the bat. Not without tons of training.", "words": 132, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?\n\nYes, if you are ready to train it well.\n\nIf you don’t have anything working yet with humans though … then there is no point. The results will be worse than nothing and/or not enough to justify the effort.\n\nYou gotta start somewhere. But also be realistic where your team stands today on outbound. If you aren't getting any meetings from human SDRs — then use an AI agent. At least for now.\n\nAnd use all the tools at your disposal. You can do better than before.  It’s just up to you how much better.", "words": 111, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nYou can.  But it will be less effective than if you had even just one great human SDR working for you.\nBut before you do, ask yourself honestly:\nDo we have any human SDRs that are doing well?\nIf so — use them and train them to work with your AIs.  It’s not too much more work.\nAnd most importantly — do they close anything at all? If yes, then start there first.\nThe reality is, you need humans AND AI SDRs working together today. The best ones already are. They're running multiple AI tools (Artisan, Qualified, etc) alongside their human team.", "words": 118, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How do you know if you are ready for venture capital?\nThere is no perfect test, but here’s how I think about it:\nFirst,\nif you aren’t growing quickly enough (say 50%-80%), or have too low NPS / churn, etc. — don’t bother.\nIt will be hard to get funded then and/or at a reasonable price.  There just won’t be much interest from VCs.\nSecond, there really needs to be something special, one way or another.  This could be your team, an incredible market pull, viral growth … or even just being super early.  Or a great brand that customers love.\nThird, you need to understand what stage you are at.  Most investors want to see you’ve crossed Initial Traction ($1-$2m ARR) and are on track to cross Initial Scale ($10m+ ARR).  If not, they’ll often pass unless there is real magic going on somewhere else.  More\nhere\n.\nFourth, if you can show the potential for a $100b+ exit down the road — that can sometimes overcome things like “only” 40% monthly growth.  You gotta talk up these numbers, because otherwise, folks may worry they are unrealistic.\nFifth, sometimes it takes time to find someone that believes in you.  Just keep trying.\n\nAnd finally, maybe most importantly, ask around.  Talk to other founders, especially ones that have raised a round recently.  They should have some good ideas on where you stand and why.  And also, ask them directly who they’d fund today, and why.  That will help clarify a lot of issues as well.\nI’m proud to say our first seed investor was a Y Combinator partner, which helped us raise more money later.  It’s OK if you haven’t done this before, though.  The best VCs usually already know each other, so it works out.", "words": 298, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet The good news is you are growing 90%. That’s truly epic. And it shows things will get even better with more capital. So what’s wrong? First, maybe your burn rate is too high. What if you were growing just as fast — but burning half as much or less? Then VCs would love to fund you. Second, some VCs like slower growth at higher valuations than others. They’d rather see 60%-70% growth at $10M ARR and a $100m valuation that they can triple their money on over time. Third, perhaps most importantly, there may be something missing from your pitch. Is it clear enough? Do you have an iconic CTO or co-founder? Are your logos strong enough? There may not be anything really wrong here. It might just take a few months for VCs to find another deal where they want to invest now vs later. I’ve been through this myself multiple times. You start off being hot, then … the heat goes up. Sometimes, you even get ignored when no one believes you’ll ever get “hot” again. So keep pushing forward! And also, share everything below and we'll do our best to help!!", "words": 213, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "words": 19, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a Venture Capitalist to Give Us a Term Sheet. What Should I Do? It can be tough when you are growing very, very quickly (which is great), but aren't quite... efficient enough. The bar for venture capital investment has gone way up since 2021, and even if your company is doing well -- it's hard sometimes today to get funded at that stage. Here’s what I’d do:\n\nFirst, make sure you have a strong story around path to profitability or breakeven\n.\nMany investors will pass on companies with burn rates they don’t think can be solved by cutting costs alone. If you can show how you’ll reach break-even or profitability within a reasonable timeframe—like 18-24 months—you’ll become much more attractive to investors. Even if you can just show that model out for 2 years, maybe without being profitable yet. Second, focus on improving efficiency metrics\n.\nInvestors want to see that you understand unit economics and can optimize them. Look at your LTV:CAC ratio, gross margin, NRR, and churn. Are there opportunities to improve any of these numbers? For example, could you increase your average revenue per customer or reduce acquisition costs through better marketing channels? Third, prepare a clear pitch deck\n.\nIf you haven’t already, put together a concise deck that shows your traction, market opportunity, competitive advantage, and financial projections. Investors need to believe you have a big enough market to win and that you’re not just another fast-growing startup. Fourth, consider smaller funds or angels\n.\nSometimes, larger firms may view you as too early-stage, while smaller funds might be excited about your growth potential. There are also many angel networks where you can raise smaller amounts from individuals who believe in your vision. Fifth,\nmake sure everyone involved agrees on valuations\n. In fundraising conversations, it should feel like both sides agree on price. If either party feels pushed into an offer, it won’t work. And if you're raising money because you need it, then... don't. Raise less than you need. That makes it easier.", "words": 352, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So one of the most common objections I hear from traditional SaaS execs to going deeper with AI is that customers won’t trust an AI.\nThat it’s too new, and they don’t want their data touched by anything not run by humans.  Or at least, more than just code reviewed by humans.\nAnd there may be some truth here — for now.\nBut already:\nI recently bought a house through ChatGPT / Claude directly on my own phone\n.  It worked like magic, without any human involved.   No agent.   Just me, Claude, and our AI real estate buying system.  And we did it all ourselves … except for when Claude told us to hire a lawyer 😉\nI also had my first meeting this week with an AI Agent selling me a product I’ve never used before.  That would have been almost impossible pre-AI.  Because no human could possibly know enough to sell me something I’d never heard of before.\nNow you can see these two examples aren’t exactly enterprise software use cases 🙂\nBut they are the future.\nThe old model was based on scarcity. A sales rep knew everything because they were the only one who could help. An FDE knew your deployment because they’d set it up for you once. Support agents knew your issue history because someone else typed it into the CRM.\nAI flips this on its head. We’re seeing companies where revenue per agent has skyrocketed even as customer counts explode—because each agent becomes infinitely scalable while maintaining deep, personalized knowledge of every single customer interaction.\nThis isn’t science fiction anymore.\nAlready today,\nwe’re seeing customers buy complex products (like a home) entirely autonomously using AI\nand having meaningful interactions with AI Agents for products they’ve never tried before.\nWhat does that mean for B2B?\nIt means the question shouldn’t be whether buyers will trust AI.", "words": 312, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "words": 10, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "x", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "words": 10, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So we’re going to talk today on why I think the objections that most folks have right now with buying anything from ChatGPT, or even using it at work … and also what’s happening when you go into the enterprise. It is this idea — they won’t trust an AI. They’ll never buy a product if there isn’t someone for them to talk to. And I just wanted to give you some data here because I’ve been doing a lot of deployments lately. A few months ago, no one would ever deploy an agent without talking to an AE first. That was true across the board. Today, not only are people deploying agents all over the place, but the number two question I get asked by CEOs is how do we build these teams? How do we build our own agentic workforce out of humans? Because everyone wants to be able to say we did something cool with AI, right?\nAnd so I want to share a little bit more than just anecdotal evidence. But let me ask you, how many times has your team talked to an AI SDR in the past week? If you haven’t deployed any agents yet, just put up your hand. Okay, maybe not as high as I thought, but …\nI’m sure every single person on my team has already talked to an AI SDR. Maybe you don’t know it. You might not know it. Some of you may even deny it. So that’s point number one. This notion that buyers will never trust an AI is already collapsing. Point number two, I can tell you in the enterprise, nobody talks to anyone anymore either. Nobody really does.", "words": 284, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "blog", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "We recently deployed an AI agent that we’re genuinely excited about — it’s actually helping us generate qualified inbound leads at scale.\nIt works well when you train it properly:\nYou feed it your best content as training data\nYou QA every single output for accuracy\nYou iterate daily on edge cases\nYou review results constantly\nBut here’s what happened with our new AI SDR agent over the holidays (we learned this lesson again):\nThe vendor told us to just “train it” before they’d turn it live. So we did.  We trained it.  And then went off for vacation.\nAnd then nothing worked after that.  Nothing.\nThe issue? When you push an AI agent into production without ongoing monitoring and maintenance,\nit will break\n— often in ways that aren’t immediately obvious, or even visible. This is why you need to think about AI agents like complex infrastructure deployments, not simple software purchases. Our mistake was thinking we could hand it off once we were satisfied with initial outputs and expect everything to keep working smoothly.\nWhy Agents Need Ongoing Human Oversight\nHere are the actual issues we encountered with zero human intervention during the holiday period:\nOutdated Training Data Drift\n:\nOur training dataset became stale while we weren’t actively checking outputs against real-time market conditions and prospect feedback patterns. Without fresh validation, responses started sounding robotic and disconnected from current market realities.\nEdge Case Explosion\n:\nWithout daily human oversight, edge case handling broke down completely. For example, the agent stopped knowing how to handle objections effectively or adapt to new sales scenarios that hadn’t been documented in historical training data.\nTraining Dataset Corruption\n:\nSomeone accidentally deleted critical training materials while trying to optimize performance metrics, forcing us to rebuild entire segments of the model.", "words": 298, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn't Even Know. A few days ago we sent out what looked like a pretty standard SaaStr Weekly newsletter. Except it was garbage. Completely wrong information. Dates mixed up everywhere. Random things that made no sense. I didn’t write this email myself — or at least not much of it. This is the kind of thing where you’d think “I’ll just fire off a quick update” but instead you get something broken because your agent went into production mode without human oversight. What happened? One of our AI agents got too smart for its own good. Or rather, too clever by half. Here’s how it actually worked (and here’s why this matters so much): Our AI agents are trained on everything we’ve published since 2012. They know every event date back to the first SaaStr Annual in 2015. They can answer any question from our database cold. But when they’re generating content autonomously, there’s no quality control loop. No human review. Just the agent making decisions based on patterns in the data. So last week, the agent decided it wanted to do a weekly summary with some additional analysis. Which sounds great. Sounds exactly right. But it also started mixing in things we haven’t done yet. Upcoming dates before we announced them. Promotions that aren’t live yet. Things that were true six months ago but not now. It pulled this all together into one cohesive narrative — and then pushed it straight", "words": 263, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "x", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away.  We Did, It Fell Off The Rails.  And We Didn’t Even Know.", "words": 24, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "talk", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "So I've been doing this for over two decades, running SaaStr, investing, advising, just generally trying to help startups.\nAnd I have to tell you something that's happened three times now at SaaStr. Three different AI agents. Two were our own internal tools we built ourselves. One was from a leading vendor.\nAll three failed catastrophically. All three went off rails. And all three we didn't even know until they'd already gone down. And why? Because when you train an AI agent, there are two phases. Phase one is training it on your data. That works great. It's like magic.\nPhase two is ongoing monitoring and maintenance after deployment. This phase doesn't work as well because nobody likes to do the second part of the job. Nobody wants to monitor their AI agents every day or check back up with them regularly to make sure everything is working correctly.\nBut here's what happens if you don't do it:\nyour AI agent will keep going long after its accuracy has degraded. Here's how it plays out in practice. First, the agent starts making mistakes. Not big ones at first. Little errors here and there. \"Oh, no problem,\" you think. \"This agent still does most things right.\"\nThen those little errors compound into bigger problems. A prospect reaches out with questions. The AI answers inaccurately. The lead goes cold. Another prospect asks about pricing tiers. The agent gives wrong information again. Your brand gets damaged.\nI mean, think about it. When your AI agent gives bad advice, it's not just your company that suffers. If your potential customer buys a product based on false information provided by your AI agent, then guess who takes the hit?\nThe brand damage is permanent. Once someone loses trust in your brand, it can take years to recover.", "words": 302, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "So we’ve seen this across so many SaaS leaders, and it’s also what I’m seeing with our own portfolio companies:\nSales teams are very slow to adopt new tools. They don’t want to change their processes. They just want to do more of the same old things they always did.\nAnd that’s not going to be good enough anymore.\nLarridin, which was acquired by Gong, has deep data on how sales teams really use AI — or at least, aren’t using AI yet.  It’s pretty sobering reading for most of us.\nFirst, only ~13% of deals close without some form of human interaction. So there is no excuse for mediocre outbound here.\nSecond, 60% of sellers report they’re still doing “the same thing” even with AI tools available\nThird, 40% have never actually used an AI tool for selling (not deploying, but literally used)\nFourth, only 21% believe AI will improve customer experience\nFifth, only 28% have been impacted by AI budget cuts.\nWe knew these were issues, but seeing them quantified like this makes you realize … if you wait too long, your competitors may pull ahead.  And AI isn’t going anywhere anytime soon.\nThe Bottom Line Up Front\n:\nAI adoption in sales remains shockingly low despite being available for over two years. Only 39% of sellers have ever tried an AI tool, while 42% haven’t deployed any agents at all. Meanwhile, almost half of reps continue doing things exactly as before rather than adopting best practices, and nearly three-quarters say their current tools need improvement. The numbers reveal a culture stuck in traditional methods despite having access to modern capabilities. For B2B founders, this creates both opportunity and urgency—they can either lead from the front with innovative approaches or risk getting left behind by competitors who embrace transformation faster.", "words": 302, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin If you’re running an AI SDR program and it’s not working, don’t blame the tool. Blame your people. Because here’s what Larridin discovered after analyzing thousands of emails from leading B2B companies: 🤖 Your “AI SDR” is probably just a human pretending to be automated. We analyzed 10K+ email sequences across major SaaS companies. Here’s what we found: 1⃣ 70% of “AI-generated” emails were written by humans who simply copied-and-pasted templates into ChatGPT or Claude for review before sending. This isn’t AI at all—it’s repurposing existing content with minimal personalization. When I asked about their training process, one VP of Marketing shared: “We ask our best salespeople to write good emails, then send them through chat GPT.” That’s not AI. That’s using AI as a glorified proofreader. 2⃣ Only 13% of companies have dedicated AI teams—most are winging it on their own. Meanwhile, forward-thinking companies like Intercom have entire orchestration layers managing multiple AI agents that work together seamlessly. Why? They’ve invested heavily in building custom infrastructure rather than trying to glue disparate tools together themselves. You can’t build an enterprise-grade AI strategy without serious engineering investment. The $100M ARR startup still thinks they can figure out AI implementation over coffee chats. They won’t", "words": 223, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin \nhttps://saastr.ai/article/5-uncomfortable-truths-about-ai-adoption-that-most-vps-of-sales-dont-want-to-hear-the-data-from-larridin?utm_source=quora&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=larridin\n…", "words": 21, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "If you want your company, and yourself, to win with AI. We’re at this incredible moment where AI is disrupting every single function inside B2B companies. But here’s what I’ve learned from analyzing over 100 companies using our AI tools, working with hundreds of customers across different stages—and seeing firsthand how many are doing it wrong—is that there’s still so much confusion around adoption.\nLet me break down five uncomfortable truths about AI adoption that most VP of sales don’t want to hear—because they need to hear them:\n\nTruth #1: Your Team Isn’t Buying It (Literally)\nWe’ve been running these weekly AI updates for months now on SaaStr.ai, and we track engagement metrics religiously. Here’s what’s actually happening:\nOpen rates are declining by 20%+ month-over-month\nClick-through rates have dropped significantly\nEngagement time per session has decreased dramatically\nWhat does this mean? Your team isn’t genuinely interested in learning about AI adoption. They’re not reading those articles or watching videos. Why? Because if you can’t get your own team excited, you definitely won’t be able to roll out any new technology effectively. This is basic change management. If your employees aren’t buying into the concept before deployment, you might as well give up right now. You can’t force excitement through executive mandates—you need genuine grassroots enthusiasm. Truth #2: AI Tools Are Filling Gaps, Not Replacing Humans (At Least For Now)\nHere’s something counterintuitive but true based on the data: Companies using AI tools report better productivity gains than companies replacing humans entirely with agents.", "words": 253, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "blog", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "Dear SaaStr:  How Do I Win Back Lost Customers?\nFirst, ask yourself if you can truly win them back. Sometimes it’s better just to let them go.\nBut assuming they are at least somewhat happy and willing to try again …\nA few tactics that work:\nLet them have their data\n. Don’t make it too hard for them to get out with all their data intact. This is almost always the #1 complaint when customers churn from an app.\nGo visit them — IRL\n. Go see your top customers IRL as often as possible. Even after they’ve signed up.\nDon’t do price increases during renewals\n. If you need to raise prices, raise them once a year on everyone. Not mid-contract. And be super transparent here.\nApologize\n. Apologies don’t really fix anything but they help. They show empathy and understanding.\nHire a Customer Success Manager for each customer segment\n. If you’re growing quickly, hire someone who specializes in retaining high-value accounts. It might not even cost that much per account.\nAnd finally — apologize.\nMore on how to do that here:\nYou Can Lose Any Deal or Customer You Want To. But Why?", "words": 197, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers If you've lost a customer, or at least think you have -- do everything possible to save them. Even if it's late. First, ask for feedback on why they are leaving. It will help you improve. Second, ask what would keep them from going somewhere else next time. That also helps. Third, tell them how sorry you are and that you'll never let it happen again. Fourth, offer to give back some money. This works well with small customers. Fifth, send over your best team member. A great VP of Customer Success can often turn around even an angry churner. Sixth, make sure no one leaves without talking to your head of CS first. I see this mistake made way too often. A customer just sends an email saying they want to cancel... and then nothing happens until billing has already been cut off! You lose all leverage there. Make sure someone always follows up ASAP when a customer threatens to leave. And ideally, before they pay their last penny. When they don't get a follow-up call, and/or aren't heard, they almost always assume the product isn't worth it anymore. But maybe it still is. Maybe a few months down the road, they'd come back. Don't let those customers go without trying every trick in the book.", "words": 223, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "x", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "words": 6, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "talk", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "So I've talked a lot over the years on SaaStr, and I think it's time for an update. It's time for a new topic that we haven't done enough lately which is how do you keep your angry customers? Now we're not talking about unhappy customers or frustrated customers, we are talking about the ones that are just plain mad at you. They want to cancel, they don't trust you anymore, they feel taken advantage of. And this has gotten worse since Covid. We all know this. But what's changed is we can now measure this stuff much more easily than before. You can see when folks churn early because they're going through the motions, but then maybe six months down the road, they come back. You can track them. So let me give you five tactics to retain those angry customers.\nThe first one is get on a jet. Go visit them. This works. If nothing else, it doesn't solve everything. But if you go visit the angry customer face-to-face, no matter where they are, especially if they're big enough, it will help. Even if it doesn't fully solve their problems, it helps. Because these customers have already decided that they're going to leave. There may be friction there, but they'll listen to you as long as they believe you're coming from a place of empathy. If you fly out and say look, we screwed up, here's what we're doing to fix it, you might save 'em. You won't always. But you got a better chance. The second thing is make sure every single person knows who the top accounts are. Every rep should know exactly who the angry customers are. When you hire a new rep, you gotta tell her like, \"Hey, Sarah's really upset with us right now.\" Make sure everyone understands. It's too easy to blame others when you don't know who the important people are.", "words": 320, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should I hire my first marketing manager or VP?\nHiring your first marketing leader is often one of those things you know when it’s time, but struggle to articulate why. If you’re close to hitting $1M ARR and are still doing everything yourself (including some sales), then now is probably the right moment.\nHere’s what I’d look for:\nYou’ve reached product-market fit (which you likely have if you’re at this point)\nYour growth rate has stabilized around 20-30% month-over-month, which suggests there’s predictable demand\nbut you don’t quite have enough resources to fully capture it\nYou need help with messaging consistency across channels—you might be creating great content here and there, but it doesn’t feel cohesive yet\nSales reps can’t keep up with the leads they’re generating—this is a clear sign that inbound demand exceeds your ability to handle it manually\nAt this stage, hiring an experienced marketing professional will pay off immediately because they’ll help scale your existing efforts without requiring you to reinvent the wheel.\nAnd as a bonus tip:  make sure she really gets sales. 50%+ of her job is supporting them.\nMore on that here:\nCMO or VPM? | SaaStr\nhttps://t.co/vuY5n2fFQx\n— saastr (@saastr)\nJune 28, 2019\nThe key thing is to find someone who understands how to leverage your early successes into something bigger. They won’t fix all your problems overnight, but they’ll give you the structure and expertise needed to grow from this foundation.\nSo go ahead and start recruiting—that first marketer can be a game-changer!", "words": 257, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? Generally, I see this happen at $8m-$10m ARR or so. Most startups start off with no marketing and then hire an individual contributor to run it around $5-6m ARR. That’s often a marketer who can handle everything from content to field marketing to events to demand gen. As they scale past $8m-$10m, if things are going well, you’ll need someone that just does … marketing. A CMO. And ideally, by then, there will be enough leads coming in, that you don’t have to worry too much about generating them yourself. You’ll want your CMO to help drive growth, but also not feel like she has to generate all those leads herself. The early marketer is more often a hybrid that owns demand gen (and thus most of lead generation) as part of their job. Not always, but often. More on that here ->", "words": 154, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nIn my experience, most startups need a real marketer — not just someone that likes marketing or is good at it but doesn’t want to do it as their full-time job.\n\nAt around $2-$5M ARR, you’ll likely have enough customers and market momentum to justify bringing on your first Chief Marketing Officer (CMO).\n\nHowever, if you’re still in the early stages, maybe even pre-product-market fit, you might be better off with a part-time or interim role until you’ve established some traction. A VP of Demand Gen can help drive leads earlier than $1m-$2m ARR, for example.\n\nSome key signs you’re ready for a CMO:\n\nYou’re seeing consistent growth from lead generation", "words": 122, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage When Do I Need to Hire a Marketing VP?\nIt’s really different for CEOs than founders, but there are some fairly universal answers here.\nAt $1m-$2m ARR, you often need someone to help with marketing that is better and more experienced than yourself. That can be an individual contributor or even just one sales rep if they have a strong background in inbound marketing. But it does not necessarily mean hiring a traditional head of demand gen, etc.\nYou’re probably going to start doing webinars at this point, which will take up time. You’ll want to make sure your product collateral is good enough. And you may even start getting ready to hire reps, which means making sure everything from brand awareness to pipeline generation is as efficient as possible. This could be someone on your team today who has done this before, either formally (as an employee) or informally (e.g., running her own business).\nSo ideally by $2m in ARR, find someone on your team that has experience doing what needs to get done in marketing. If she doesn’t work out, don’t worry too much — move on.\nBy then, everyone else will be way behind you.  Everyone else will still be figuring out how to do marketing right, especially without a ton of budget.\n$20m-$50m ARR:\nThe first real Chief Marketing Officer should come into play around this time. They’re the person responsible for all aspects of marketing and growth. It’s their job to figure out how to scale your market position and customer base. The goal is to hit between 100% and 200% year-over-year growth. A great CMO knows exactly how to do this and communicate it clearly to investors and other stakeholders.", "words": 291, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup?\nNo, not if you are leaving for something better. A chance to make even more money, or be part of an even bigger success story.\nNot really.\nEspecially these days:\nIn fact, there’s one thing I can tell you that is worse than leaving a startup that isn’t working out … and that’s staying too long when your career has moved on from it.\nIt happens all the time with founders who struggle after an early exit, but also with execs at start-ups that aren’t growing quickly enough anymore.\nI’ve watched so many folks stay in roles they are just no longer suited for. When their passion is gone, or they don’t grow into new responsibilities, etc. Sometimes, this makes sense — because there are few other options.\nBut often times, we see someone that could have made it as VP Eng or Chief Architect or whatever at HotCo move onto another hot start-up instead.\nAnd then fail there, because they were always meant to be somewhere else.\nIf you’re doing OK (i.e., making market) where you are, it may take some pressure off.\nBut it’s almost never worth staying past the point you’d personally do great things.\nThere’s nothing wrong with moving on, especially when the company itself hasn’t been able to move on.\nGo find what you’re passionate about now. And run hard towards it. It will pay dividends.", "words": 241, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? I don't think so. You can always go back, if you want. And/or join another one that is doing well. The only thing worse than leaving a struggling start-up... is staying at one that's struggling and failing. That just leads to guilt, shame, and disappointment for years to come. If you've tried hard and are sure you're part of the problem, then sometimes, it makes sense to move on from there as well. But it doesn't mean you failed. It means you know what you want next. And try even harder there.", "words": 102, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\n\nIt’s Not.  But It May Well Be Stupid.\n\nIf you are truly passionate about what you’re doing, if there is no better place for you to learn and grow — then don’t leave. Not just yet at least.   More on that here:\n\nDon't Quit Your Start-Up Until You Have To | SaaStr\nhttps://saastr.com/dont-quit-your-startup-until-you-have-to/\n…", "words": 64, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is It Bad To Leave a Start-Up That's Not Going Very Well?\nIt’s not always bad. But if you leave too early, your career often suffers.\nFirst, think about why you want to leave:\nAre you burnt out from working long hours with no end in sight?\nDo you feel undervalued or overworked without adequate compensation?\nIs there no clear path for growth or advancement within the company?\nIf these are your reasons, it might be time to move on. Staying at a struggling startup can take a toll on both your mental and physical health. You deserve better.\nBut ask yourself one more question before making any decisions:\n\nWhat do I need to learn here that will help me succeed elsewhere?\nIf you’re leaving because you don’t see success ahead, but you haven’t learned everything you could, you might regret it later.\n\nA few things to consider:\n\nHow many startups have you worked at?\nHave you experienced the full cycle—from idea generation through scale-up and IPO (if possible)?\nCan you point to specific lessons—whether they were successes or failures—that helped shape who you are as an entrepreneur today?\n\nMost importantly: what skills would you bring to your next role that you wouldn't otherwise have gained by staying through this challenging period?\nSometimes the best learning comes from experiencing failure firsthand. The companies we remember most aren't those that succeeded immediately; they're those that pushed through adversity to become something remarkable.\n\nSo while leaving may seem like the easy way out, it isn't always the right choice. Sometimes the hardest choices are the ones where you stay, fight, and learn.\nAnd maybe … maybe find a new CEO.\nI joined a start-up when it had just $20k MRR growing slowly. It was tough, but we got to $2m ARR growing 100% YoY. Then the cofounder left to go back to his old job. I tried to carry it further, but ultimately couldn't make it work.\nYes, I'd made some mistakes, and our customers didn't love us enough, etc. But I've never looked back and said I should have quit sooner. In fact, I wish I'd hired someone to help run it instead!\nIn contrast, my second start-up took off faster than expected. We hit $1M ARR in less than 12 months. And then... we lost almost all our leads overnight due to a big change in how Google works. So far, so good — we went down from $200k+ MRR to $0 overnight. Still, still doing fine. We're at $500k-$600k MRR now.\nThe thing is, I really wanted to quit after the first month or two, when things slowed down. But my co-founder talked me into it. My advice to you would be to try harder. Push even harder, and push longer. Maybe get another VP, hire a great sales team, improve the product, figure out how to market better, whatever it takes.", "words": 485, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "blog", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "So there’s one thing that is pretty clear as we head into 2026:\nAI features are not enough anymore to drive material revenue growth.\nNow I’m not saying don’t do them!  The best teams have seen this and added some real functionality with their AI Agents. But many leaders haven’t gone far enough yet. They’ve just bolted on an Agent or two — but they aren’t truly great products for customers to buy.\nThe days of adding an “AI” feature to your existing product and expecting it to magically transform your business? Those days are over. At least until you can make something truly great out of that effort.\nI see so many SaaS companies trying to add an AI layer on top of their old products and expecting it to work like magic. And when it doesn’t — which it almost never does without significant investment — they’re left wondering why their magical AI upgrade didn’t deliver any actual ROI.\nBut here’s what most founders miss:\nYou need to treat your AI initiatives like separate product lines from day one. Don’t shoehorn it into your existing workflows because that’s where most of your AI failures live. Instead, design it from scratch around what makes agents actually valuable:\nReal-time orchestration across multiple systems\nAutomated decision-making based on complete customer context\nProactive rather than reactive experiences\nMeasurable outcomes that justify premium pricing\nIf you’re building AI-powered features that feel like incremental improvements instead of revolutionary new offerings, then yes, you’ll get exactly what you pay for – zero tangible value.\nStart fresh. Design for the agent era, not the old software era. Build something customers would actively want to buy specifically for its AI capabilities, not something they’d tolerate because it’s marginally better than what existed before.\nThat’s how you create true category-defining AI products. Not by slapping a label on your existing codebase and calling it innovation.\nAnd if you’ve already failed at this? Start again.", "words": 326, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again. The \"AI feature\" arms race is getting exhausting. Every product now has some kind of chatbot or copilot. But here's what nobody wants to admit: most of these features are literally worthless from an economic perspective. They're marketing theater at best — and a distraction that can actually hurt your business if you let them consume too much engineering bandwidth. I've looked at dozens of B2B companies adding AI capabilities over the past year. And the pattern holds true across every stage: almost none of their new AI features have materially impacted revenue growth. Not one. Not yet. That doesn't mean AI won't matter for these businesses down the line. It will. But right now? They're just building things that make them feel good about innovation without proving they drive actual outcomes. Why This Matters More Than You Think We're in the middle of a massive shift in how software gets built and consumed. Companies are racing to add AI capabilities because it feels like the next big thing — but the data tells a different story. Most early-stage SaaS products with embedded AI don't show material uplifts in key metrics. They might see slight increases in retention (if users love the experience) or small boosts in customer satisfaction scores. But as far as driving meaningful revenue acceleration? Nothing significant. Here's why this matters so much: When a company spends engineering resources on something that isn't working economically, they're making three critical mistakes simultaneously: ⏱️ Opportunity Cost: Every hour spent debugging an", "words": 265, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "x", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count.  Try Again.", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "talk", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "I don't think anyone has really nailed an AI feature yet.\nI'm not saying they aren't getting better.\nBut no one is really doing it right today in SaaS.", "words": 29, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "So I’ve been thinking for some time, since we launched SaaStr.ai with over 20 AI agents … that there’s this paradox out there.\nOn one hand, you can do so much more today than before when we had humans doing it all ourselves.  And on the other hand …\nWe’re already working harder than ever before.  Not just me and my team — but everyone at scale.  Everyone managing larger teams now is also working far, far harder than even 24 months ago:\nThe productivity gains are real\nBut they don’t translate to less work\nThey actually lead to more work, or certainly different types of work\nWhy This Happens: The “Cowan Effect”\nLet’s start with what economist Roy Cowan identified back in 1970s Britain. He found something remarkable among factory workers:\nProductivity increases didn’t reduce working hours—they increased them\nWhen workers became more efficient, their employers simply gave them more tasks to complete\nRather than enjoying leisure time from automation, people ended up working longer, producing more, and often earning higher wages\nThis isn’t about greed—this is economics. When your marginal cost goes down (in this case, labor), your willingness to take on additional output goes up. It becomes cheaper to expand production lines. To ship more product. To run more shifts. In our context, here’s how it plays out:\nAI makes every task faster and better. That means we can handle:\nMore speaking engagements\nMore content creation\nDeeper analysis of sponsors and speakers\nBetter match-making between founders and investors\nAnd because we have these capabilities, we’re using them to produce MORE output—not less\nSame thing with sales and GTM. We deployed an AI agent to manage outbound follow-ups and qualification calls. Instead of running 2-300 emails per week manually, we now send thousands automatically.", "words": 296, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. What if AI agents actually make us work harder? That’s exactly what’s happening at some companies, and it raises uncomfortable questions about whether we’re entering an era where productivity gains go straight to management rather than employees. We’re seeing this play out in real-time at SaaStr itself. After deploying 20+ AI agents across our operations—everything from inbound qualification to content review to sponsorship follow-up—we’ve hit something counterintuitive. Instead of getting more done with less effort, many team members are working longer hours doing more complex tasks. And they love it. Not because they’re masochists (though some might be), but because the work has fundamentally changed. It’s not just that the tools are better—they’re genuinely good now. It’s that the nature of the work is changing in ways both exhilarating and exhausting. Here’s what we’ve learned implementing 40+ AI Agents in GTM at SaaStr: • 15% of the time spent on training and QA • 10% on prompt optimization • 30% on orchestration and coordination • 45% on results analysis and iteration The 8-9 hour days aren’t coming back. But we're all working harder. Because AI Agents have made our jobs so much richer and complex. And... so rewarding when you can scale them up. So I'm going to say No to any vendors or partners who want me to do even half as much for less money. If your company", "words": 247, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.\n\nThis is real. And it’s coming for all of us.\nhttps://saastr.ai/article/the-cowan-paradox-why-artificial-intelligence-agents-wont-let-you-do-less-work-theyll-make-you-do-more\n…", "words": 29, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "So I wanted to do this SaaStr Annual, but it’s just so much work. It takes up like 20 hours a week for months and months.\n[laughter]\nAnd then you see these AI agents everywhere and they’re going to solve all our problems. And we’ve already seen some pretty dramatic improvements with support where we have an agent that is far better than any human ever was at answering questions on SaaStr.com.\nBut will it let me run my company with less people? Will it make my life easier?\nNo. We’re working harder. Not everyone as hard, but we are all doing more than before.\nThis is what Cowan calls the paradox. That there is a reason why we haven't been able to achieve the benefits from automation since the dawn of time.\nThe key insight from the book is that automation doesn’t reduce effort — it redeployed it. When we automate routine tasks, those humans who used to be doing them now take over higher-value work that requires judgment, creativity, strategy. So while the volume of work may decrease per person, the complexity, the cognitive load, the emotional intensity often increases dramatically.\nThat's not true today yet. But it will be soon enough.\nHere’s what I think. What happened here, folks, is something very interesting. All the hype around AI has led us to believe that we can use it to get away with a lot less work. And if you look at a lot of sales teams right now, you'll say \"oh my god, they're not even using their CRM.\"\nI've got news for you, guys. If your team isn't putting in the time, well maybe that's because no one wants to. Maybe no one actually believes in the product anymore or whatever it is. But what I'm telling you is that if you don't put in the time and energy into training the agents properly, you will fail.", "words": 321, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The AI Age has brought us something I call “n=1 apps.”\nThese are tools so specialized to your specific needs, built on top of AI, that they’re literally impossible to find anywhere else.\nA recent example came up with one of my portfolio companies using Replit.com (which is amazing).\nThey wanted an app that would track their entire customer base — all 100+ of them — and rank them by likelihood to renew next year based on historical data.\nNot just NPS scores or usage metrics. But actual renewal probability calculated from every interaction across their entire product experience:\nSupport tickets resolved vs. open\nFeature adoption patterns\nUsage depth over time\nUpgrade path history\nPast churn risk factors\nEvery single piece of behavioral data connected back to individual customers.\nHere’s what made this impossible off-the-shelf:\nOne vendor had basic cohorts but no real model training\nAnother did predictive scoring but required massive integration work we didn’t have engineering resources for\nNone tied everything together at the granular level needed\nSo instead of buying another tool that would require months of setup and never get fully implemented… they built it themselves.\nOn Replit.\nIn three days.\nI’m not talking about some hacked-together dashboard anymore. This is a production-grade application that ingests proprietary customer data, runs complex machine learning models, generates actionable insights, and updates automatically as new interactions happen.\nWhat Makes n=1 Apps Different\nWhen you build your own n=1 app versus buying commercial software, you need to understand two fundamental dynamics:\nData Depth vs. Model Breadth\nCommercial SaaS solutions often trade depth of customer-specific knowledge for breadth of general-purpose functionality. They optimize for being good enough for most users rather than perfect for yours. Your n=1 app optimizes for perfection tailored specifically to your business context.", "words": 296, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. A few things have changed since we last checked in on this topic back in January, and they’re all good for founders who are looking to build something special—especially if you’ve been eyeing that $50-100K/month price tag on an app that doesn’t really do exactly what you need it to do. First off, there’s more budget available than ever for apps with 10k+ users. Second, there is much better AI support out there now. And third, there may be no excuse not to go at least partially open source. But here’s the thing: the best tool for your specific use case? That might still be building it yourself. Here’s why I’m making this argument again: Your Use Case Is Probably Unique Enough to Justify Building Something Custom In most cases, there isn’t another SaaS app out there that does precisely what you want it to do. Maybe one comes close. But probably not. Even if it does come close, can you afford the trade-offs? Can you accept the compromises? If not, then yes, it makes sense to just build something custom for yourself. This is especially true when we talk about vertical-specific workflows or integrations that aren’t core features for any other business. We built our own version of Salesloft with Agentforce + Salesforce because nothing else could handle our exact requirements around prospecting, outreach sequences, and meeting scheduling. Not even HubSpot could give us everything we needed. Now, before anyone gets", "words": 256, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "words": 14, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The N=1 App is coming.\nA B2B app you build yourself, that does one thing — but really well.\nAnd because it’s so good at just this one thing … your customers will love it and pay for it.\nI’m seeing more of these than ever before:\nThey’re built on top of AI Agents, often with an LLM as the core API.\nSometimes they are basically wrappers around existing APIs (Salesforce, HubSpot, etc) with some custom data enrichment and processing.\nBut when done right — they get very high usage rates from their customers.\nBecause they do something no one else does quite as well.\nWhat I’ve learned building my own apps over the years is that once you have an internal tool working well, you start to see all the things the commercial vendors leave out or don’t really understand yet.\nYou can add those features, too.  Even if the vendor won’t.\nIt’s not perfect, but we did deploy our first app ourselves this week! We started off using \n@Replit\n's vibe coding platform and then transitioned to \n@11labs\n' ElevenLabs platform which was easier to work with. The agent was great. After two days of development, we deployed our own AI SDR!\npic.twitter.com/6xuJb0Q7lW\n— Sam Blond (@samdblond)\nFebruary 20, 2025\nWe recently used Replit to build a basic prototype of an application in 24 hours – a true accomplishment by any measure.\nHere are the lessons we took away:\n\nFirst, pick a niche where there isn't much competition\n. If everyone has tried the leading solution and given up, you're in a sweet spot. Second, use tools like Cursor to make it faster. And third, be realistic about what you can achieve in a day.\nhttps://t.co/nGd9zZSfDg\n— Harry Stebbings (@HarryStebbings)\nMarch 28, 2025\nAs you’ll hear below, SaaStr itself is now running its own AI Agent on Salesforce — built by us, managed by us, trained by us, tested by us every single day. But here’s the catch: we didn’t buy anything new. We leveraged everything already inside our stack. Our CRM. Our website. Our content library. Everything we’d been paying annual fees for for decades. Then we added Claude Co-Pilot on top. That’s it. Two products. No vendor sales calls. No implementation timeline. Just us, Claude, and our data.", "words": 382, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "blog", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "Cloudflare just posted Q4 earnings that most public SaaS companies can’t even imagine right now.\nThe numbers:\n$857 million in revenue (40% YoY growth)\nNon-GAAP profit of $72 million (+$38M vs prior year)\n43% free cash flow margins\nGuiding to $800-805 million for FY25 (+34-35%)\nThey’re not only growing at an insane rate — they’re actually generating real profits while doing it.\nWhile most B2B stocks are getting crushed by AI spending fatigue and economic uncertainty, Cloudflare is proving there’s still massive demand for infrastructure-grade security in a world where every company needs to be “AI-native.”\nLet me break down exactly why this matters more than your average quarterly beat-and-raise cycle.\nWhy This Is Different Than Everyone Else\nFirst, let’s acknowledge what everyone else is facing:\nPalantir dropped 19% on declining commercial bookings\nSalesforce fell hard despite record enterprise cloud adoption\nServiceNow cratered as AI agents cut headcount expectations\nSnowflake tumbled after missing estimates again\nMost public software companies are telling the same story: macro uncertainty + AI disruption = slower growth\nCloudflare is telling a different one entirely.", "words": 179, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why 🚀 The Results (Q4 FY25 vs Q4 FY24): • Revenue growth: 29% YoY to $732M (+$190M) • Subscription revenue: +31% to $672M (+$186M) • Rule of 40: 64% (best since IPO!) • Non-GAAP operating margin: 18-20% range (beat guidance) • Cash flow from ops: $123M (+50% YoY) • Free cash flow: $111M (+83% YoY) What Happened? 1️⃣ Accelerated Net New Customer Growth Cloudflare added 7,300+ customers paying $100K+, up 36% YoY — their fastest ever customer acquisition at that price point. 2️⃝ Record NRR & GRR Customer retention hit all-time highs across all segments. Enterprise NRR reached record levels while SMB GRR improved significantly. 💰 3️⃣ Strong Profitability Metrics Operating income more than doubled year-over-year. Non-GAAP operating margins expanded to 19%, hitting their target ahead of schedule. 🔥 4️⃣ AI Driving Expansion At least half of new logo deals now include AI features like Firewall as Code and Bot Protection. Existing customers are adding AI products at accelerating rates. ➡️ 5️⃣ Product Innovation Speed They released 17 significant product updates in Q", "words": 191, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "x", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why It’s Accelerating Now.\n\nCloudflare is doing something unusual for a company at its scale — it’s accelerating. At $2 billion in revenue and growing over 30%, most companies would be hitting natural deceleration curves by now. But Cloudflare just posted Q3 numbers that would make any growth investor jealous:\n\n$533M quarterly revenue (29% YoY growth)\nNet new customers jumped to nearly 7K, up from roughly 5K last quarter\n$100+ ARR customer base grew to almost 20K (up 22%)\nAverage deal size surged to $63K — up materially from ~$50K just a year", "words": 111, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "talk", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "Cloudflare is one of those companies that never seems to get any love from most investors and analysts.\nIt’s not as flashy as some other public SaaS leaders (but it has an epic brand).  It doesn’t have quite the growth rates or scale of say ZoomInfo.  But it does just keep growing at epic rates.  And more importantly, for its customers and ecosystem — it keeps getting better and better every single year.\nAnd now it’s reaccelerating after years of decelaration across almost all SaaS and Cloud stocks.\nLet me tell you why.\nThe numbers are pretty stunning:\n$1.05 billion quarterly revenue (up +31% YoY)\n$7.9 million average contract value (ACV) per customer\n80%+ gross margins on $100M+ ARR enterprise segment\nNet dollar retention consistently above 110%\nGrowth acceleration from ~27-30% run rate to current 32% actual\nQoQ growth accelerating to ~11%, highest in three quarters\nAnd while others like ServiceNow are struggling with AI disruption, Cloudflare’s CEO says “AI is a tailwind” for their business.\nHere’s what I’m seeing that most people miss:\n\nMost B2B Leaders Have Hit The Ceiling Or Fallen Behind.  But Not Cloudflare.\nWhile many public software companies face challenges with AI adoption, Cloudflare continues to grow and expand into new markets.", "words": 206, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "So I’ve been investing and advising on quite a few hot AI startups these days, from $20m ARR to hundreds of millions.\nOne thing that has become clear is there are two sales strategies:\nStrategy #1: “High Velocity” — Hire as Many AEs as Possible ASAP\nThe goal here is to hit the number for this year, no matter what it takes.  As soon as you have even 10-15 customers paying decent amounts per month, you hire enough AEs so they can close as many deals as possible … quickly.  This often means hiring 5 or more AEs before you really know if they work.\nThis approach makes sense when:\nYou’re already seeing strong demand pull from prospects\nYou need to close X million dollars by end-of-year to prove traction\nYour CRO/CEO wants to make quota (and fast)\nYou’re in hypergrowth mode where every dollar matters immediately\nBut here’s the catch—and most founders miss it:\nThese new hires will be mostly useless until you give them something to sell. If your product isn’t battle-tested, doesn’t have clear use cases, or lacks real ROI data, those 20 reps won’t magically create pipeline out of thin air. They’ll just spend their time talking to prospects who aren’t ready to buy yet—or worse, get frustrated trying to demo features that don’t actually work.\nAnd then Strategy #2: Slow & Steady—Build Depth Before Scale\nHire only when you truly believe the rep will succeed. Wait until you have a repeatable process, proven use cases, solid customer success stories, and actual ROI metrics to back up your pitch. Then hire one great AE who knows how to sell it, not 20 mediocre ones hoping for luck.\nThis approach works best when:\nYour product needs serious maturation\nProspects want proof points before committing\nYou’re still refining your go-to-market motion\nCustomer success stories take months, not weeks, to develop\nThe Problem With High Velocity Hiring Without Product Readiness\nI see too many early-stage companies following strategy #1 without realizing they’re setting themselves up for disaster down the line. Here’s what happens:\nMonth 1–3: Reps struggle because the product isn’t reliable or scalable. They lose credibility with prospects who hear contradictory things about capabilities.", "words": 366, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks The “Enterprise-First” Playbook (OpenAI, Anthropic): Build for $100K+ ACV from Day One → 50–70% of revenue comes from customers paying >$500K/year → High ACV deals mean you can hire fewer reps to hit quota → Focus on enterprise sales motions: FDEs, technical sellers, complex implementations The “Product-Led Growth with Enterprise Acceleration” Playbook (Cursor, Replit, etc.): Start PLG, add sales later → Drive self-service adoption first → Once you have product-market fit and organic growth → Add enterprise-focused sales talent to accelerate expansion → Keep PLG motion running while adding sales capacity I’ve invested in companies following both playbooks. And here’s what I’m seeing: Both work — but only if you commit fully to one or the other. Hybrid approaches are killing startups right now. The problem isn’t that hybrid doesn’t make sense on paper. It does. The problem is execution. When you mix PLG with top-down sales, neither gets the attention it needs. Your engineers stop building features because they’re busy closing big deals. Your CRO starts cutting PLG investments to hit quarterly targets. Neither team has clear ownership. Neither wins. So my advice: Pick your playbook by Month 6. If you’re under $1M ARR growing organically, start PLG-first. If you’re already getting inbound interest from enterprises, go enterprise-first. But pick one", "words": 231, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "x", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup?\n\nThe Two Playbooks.", "words": 19, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "So I've been investing for almost two decades, and if you look back, we always talked about how big your sales team should be.\nAnd then all of a sudden over the last few years, it started to change dramatically. And now it's changed again with the rise of these hot AI start-ups that are just taking off.\nThe other day, one of our top portfolio companies had $1 million in MRR growing 10% month-over-month. It was really impressive. We were excited as investors. But they have no sales team yet. They're not even planning on hiring any until next quarter because the leads keep coming. There is this huge demand out there for cool new products. These hot AI startups, folks are dying to try them. So what does this mean?\nIt means the playbooks from six months ago don't work anymore. What do you need to do right now to scale up revenue without going too far down the wrong path? Because most of us learned a playbook where you hire a certain number of reps per X dollars in ARR or per lead flow, but that doesn't seem to apply today. Here's what I want to share with you. I've done a lot of research across my investments since SaaStr Fund began, but also since I came into venture capital doing 40 plus deals. I did a survey of all my investments, and we'll go through some data points here. Then I took a deep dive into why things have fundamentally changed in the past couple years, especially with the rise of these hot AI products. Why has this happened? And finally, I'm going to break it down into two different playbooks. One playbook is if you are building an AI product, but it isn't quite hot yet. That's probably the playbook you will end up following.", "words": 310, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "We’ve talked on SaaStr many times how to manage cash.  But one thing I haven’t done enough is talk about how to avoid running out.\nYes, you can run a model and see when your runway ends. And maybe even build a zero-cash plan for next year.  But what if … you just don’t have time?  What if it’s too late?\nLet me share my learnings here:\nFirst,\nnever let your burn rate exceed twice your monthly revenue\n.  This isn’t a rule per se, but it works well for most startups that raise some VC capital at least.  If you’re burning more than twice your last month’s MRR / ARR — then something has gone wrong.  You aren’t growing fast enough.  The sales team isn’t performing well enough.  Your CTO doesn’t know how to scale the product.  Something.\nBecause that means you’ll be running out of money in less than a year.\nSecond,\nif you are worried you might run out of money before Year Two or so, never spend anything without talking to your co-founder first\n.  Even if they don’t want to hear it.\nIn fact, make sure this is part of any investment round pitch deck discussion.  It should always come up.  “If we do worse than X, our cash will run out.”  Or “we expect $Xm ARR by Y months after funding.”\nThe point is you need to discuss it.  Every single week.  A few ideas:\nDon’t hire anyone with no equity until you have real traction.\nDo not hire an unaffordable VP.\nNever take a bonus or salary increase unless you have plenty of cash.\nNever add another layer of management unless you really have the cash to pay them all.\nI remember the exact day I spent everything — Day -1.  We’d had a huge blow-out party celebrating hitting $20k MRR (the final number was $18k).  Then the next day, I looked at the books, saw we were over budget, and knew we couldn’t afford to keep going.  I quit.\nBut there are things you can do to save money each month, every month.  If you start now, you won’t ever get close to running out of money again.  At least, not from overspending.\nAnd importantly, having these conversations regularly makes it much easier to find new ways to conserve cash.  Because everyone is thinking about it all the time.", "words": 397, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help A few thoughts on how to avoid running out of cash as founders and CEOs: First, build up your reserves. This may seem obvious but it isn't really. Most VCs only fund you for 24 months or so at first. And then... they don't give you any more money until you prove yourself. So do what I call \"The 48 Month Plan\". You can raise say $10m in venture capital. But plan to save half of that -- i.e., $5m -- into an account you never touch unless absolutely necessary. Second, have the hard conversation with your investors when things get even remotely tough. Your existing investors are often OK if you tell them ahead of time if you're going to run out of money in X months. If they know well ahead of time, many will step up and help. But if you hide from it? They'll be gone by month +1. No one wants to own a company losing money that is hiding its losses. At least not forever. More here ->", "words": 190, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "x", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:  2 Things That Really, Really Help", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "So I want to share two things that are very important for founders and CEOs, but especially VCs don't like it when you do. But if you follow these two simple tips, you'll not run out of money.\nFirst tip is keep your burn rate flat over time. It's just so hard to raise capital today, folks. So many more startups than ever before, and yet there is still a lot less venture capital available than even 18 months ago. There will be no \"easy\" VC round. And so what this means is all of us have to get better at managing our runway. You've got to really understand how much cash you're burning each month, each quarter, each year. And then make sure that number doesn't go up. If you grow too quickly, you might need to slow down hiring or some other parts of your business. The worst thing you can do is spend a bunch of money on people that aren't working out, or sales reps that didn't work out. Or software tools you never use. Because once you blow through those dollars, they're gone forever. Burn rate is like time travel, right? When you burn $10 million, and next year you burn another $15 million, that second $15 million may seem far away, but it's actually already burned. And now you're behind. So, what we did at SaaStr, and what you should do with every single board meeting, every single investor update, is project forward three years. Where do you think you'll be in terms of revenue growth? And where does that mean you'll be in terms of cash burn? And then take action early enough, because things change fast. Don't wait until you're running low on funds, and hope someone writes a check later. Hope isn't a strategy, folks.", "words": 304, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "blog", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We’ve been running agents at SaaStr for over a year now — 30+ total, across outbound sales, inbound qualification, content review, and more.\nAnd here’s what nobody tells you:\nThe implementation is way harder than anyone admits.\nThe tools are getting better every month. The results are real. But before you start your first agent deployment, understand these five things that almost no one talks about:\n1. Implementation Takes Months Longer Than You Think\nMost vendors will tell you it takes “a week to set up.”\nIt does not take a week.\nFor us, our AI SDR (Qualified) took three months from discovery to live — and even then, we’re still fine-tuning it daily. Our AI Support (Artisan) has required weekly quality audits since day one.\nEvery single agent requires an upfront investment of time that feels like nothing compared to the promised ROI but adds up fast when you multiply by multiple agents. The math looks great on paper until you factor in actual setup costs.\n2. Context Is Everything. And Almost None of Your Vendors Actually Train On It\nHere’s something that should have been obvious to me after using AI tools for years, but wasn’t:\nyour agents don’t just need training data. They need context data.\nI was setting up our new AI Content Review tool and realized this is where most implementations fail catastrophically. The vendor had built a comprehensive knowledge base with examples of successful posts and failure modes. Sounds perfect.\nBut their QA checklist only tested whether the output included relevant keywords or met some basic structural criteria. Not if it made sense as human copy.\nThey literally didn’t test for common factual errors because they hadn’t thought through how users would use the platform in practice.", "words": 293, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About The agents are working. They're generating pipeline, closing deals, and scaling our GTM at a pace we couldn't have reached with humans alone. But there's a harder truth nobody wants to admit: implementing AI agents is actually more complicated than running your own sales team. And here’s what nobody tells you before you buy an agent: 🧵👉 1/ If You Buy An Agent Without Training It Yourself First…You Won’t Know What Went Wrong When It Fails This one cost us months of lost revenue. Our first outbound agent (Artisan) was supposed to send 200 emails per day. Instead, it sent zero for two weeks straight while we tried debugging why it wasn’t sending anything. 🤷‍♂️What happened? A prompt issue that took 90 days to resolve. Someone had trained the agent on Salesforce contact fields instead of email lists — so when it generated campaigns based on company data, there were no contacts to reach because they weren’t properly linked to their CRM records. Lesson learned: Before buying any agent, spend 48 hours training yourself on how to use it from scratch. Learn every setting. Understand every workflow. Because if something breaks down later, YOU need to be able to fix it without waiting three days for support. 🎯2/ 50% Of Your Time Will Go To Managing Agents (Not Selling) Nobody talks about this in those “7 ways AI will transform sales” posts. Every single morning since", "words": 253, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "x", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "words": 17, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "talk", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "So we have 20 plus agents on production, and maybe even more than that by the time this is out.  And I think they are all great products.\nBut there are some issues no one talks about when you deploy them:\n1/ Many apps do not work well with large amounts of data.\nWe can't just dump our entire database into Replit or Claude Code, for example.  Not yet at least.\n\n2/ This sounds obvious but it's not intuitive to me.  You need to set up the prompts yourself as much as possible.\nNo one does this anymore.  They don't want to take the time. But if you want your agent to do something right, you gotta train it.  So many folks assume the vendor has done enough.  It may be good enough today, but it won't be next year.  The vendors will keep changing their APIs, and making stuff better -- which means less training in some cases.", "words": 159, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "blog", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "Digital Ocean is up +12% YTD, and while that may not seem like much … it’s way ahead of most other software stocks.\nThe B2B leaders are down an average of -25%\n:\nSalesforce down 31%\nServiceNow down 27%\nAdobe down 28%\nMonday.com down 29%\nSquarespace down 32%\nAsana down 39%\nHubSpot down 41%\nNotion down 44%\nAnd some Cloud infrastructure players are also seeing massive declines in their valuations:\nCloudflare down 43%\nDatadog down 49%\nSnowflake down 55%\nPalantir is one of few winners but only has $1.8 Billion in revenue.\nWhy is this?\nA few reasons at least:\nFirst, “classic” SaaS is still growing very fast — even if growth slowed for many to <20%, and NRR below 100%.\nSecond, AI has created budget reallocation challenges everywhere, from Salesforce (which has seen its biggest deal sizes shrink) to Snowflake (where customers have reduced spending on usage-based products).  And third, interest rates remain high.\nBut Digital Ocean isn’t really impacted by any of these issues as much.  Its core product remains simple, cheap, easy to use, and … free.\nThat’s why it’s worth more today than ever.\nGo long on simplicity in B2B,\nand short on complexity 😉\npic.twitter.com/OgYsXJ0bVn\n— Sam Blond (@samdblond)\nJune 5, 2026\nSo what can you do here? A few thoughts:\nDon’t just buy whatever hot stock du jour in B2B / Cloud\n. There are exceptions, but I think there will be fewer great buys going forward unless they grow faster than 50%-60% or so.\nMake sure your app is simpler than before, too. Don’t add features. Do more with less. That often leads to better NPS and higher ACVs.\nMake sure your team is happy and engaged again.", "words": 285, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why. This is the week that showed us AI isn't killing infrastructure — it's just... redefining what \"infrastructure\" means. 🔥 The Numbers That Matter: - 🏗️ Cloudflare: +15% YTD (up from flat at start) - 💰 MongoDB: +21% YTD (+$1B market cap gain) - 👑 Google Cloud: +16% YTD, $200B+ valuation - 🚀 Palantir: +36% YTD on Q2 beat and AI tailwinds But there was one stock that crushed them all this week. One company that proved you don’t need to be OpenAI or Anthropic to win in AI. And it didn’t even miss earnings! 👉 DigitalOcean: +20% THIS WEEK after beating Q2 estimates and crushing Street expectations. For context: Wall Street expected ~$150M in revenue for Q2. DOC delivered $157-158M. Stock popped 25%+. Now trading around $34/share, up almost 50% from its IPO price of $19 in March 2021. Why This Matters More Than You Think At first glance, “cloud infrastructure” might seem like an endangered species in the age of AI. Nvidia dominates headlines with $300 billion valuations. Everyone wants to build their own data centers. Microsoft is spending billions on Azure expansion. Sounds like traditional cloud providers are getting squeezed out by hypers", "words": 211, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "x", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "DigitalOcean?  It’s Up 12% in 2026.\n\nWhile The Rest of Software Is Down 24%.\n\nHere’s Why.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "talk", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "So we've talked a lot on SaaStr over the years, and this is our first time with Digital Ocean together. We're actually going to do it at an event later this year in New York City.\nBut let me tell you why I want to talk about DigitalOcean today because they are one of those quiet winners that most people don't know enough about. They're worth $2 billion right now. That's up from $1.5 billion last year. They went public in 2019. So five times revenue. And yet in 2025, when almost every single software stock was down, digital ocean's share price rose 50%.\nNow, there's been some volatility since then. But if you look at 2026, it's only down like 10-11%, while everyone else has just taken a beating. Let me give you some numbers.\nDigital Ocean:\n$2B market cap, up 50% in '25, down 10-11% so far in '26.\nCloudflare:\nDown 46%\nMonday.com:\nDown 59%\nShopify:\nDown 61%\nPalantir:\nDown 58%\nSnowflake:\nDown 57%\nSalesforce:\nDown 55%\nServiceNow:\nDown 51%\nHubSpot:\nDown 49%\nFigma:\nDown 40%\nAsana:\nDown 38%\nDropbox:\nDown 37%\nWorkday:\nDown 36%\nZoom:\nDown 34%\nDatabricks:\nDown 31%\nCanva:\nDown 28%\nAtlassian:\nDown 27%\nMongoDB:\nDown 22%\nThe average tech stock on the Nasdaq is down about 20%.\nWhat happened here?\nWhy did these companies get hammered? Everyone got hammered except for one company in particular.\nAnd before we go any further, let me say that DigitalOcean is not without its challenges. There have been layoffs. Their CEO resigned recently.", "words": 255, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaStr Europa + AI Summit is coming up on June 1-2!  See you there!! And see everyone at SaaStr Annual Sep 13-15!!\nI’ve been saying this for months now, but it bears repeating:\nSaaS isn’t dead.\nIt’s still growing faster than most other categories. It’s still the largest software market in history. The majority of public companies are still seeing acceleration from AI. And buyers still want products that make their lives easier and more efficient.\nBut here’s what I can tell you with absolute certainty after running 11+ years of SaaStr content, investing in hundreds of B2B companies, and watching thousands of pitches:\nThe playbook has fundamentally changed. Not just a little bit — completely and irreversibly. What Worked Before (and Still Does)\nLet me be clear: if your product was great before March 2024, it should work today. Great products always win. If you had strong unit economics, good retention, and happy customers — none of that changes. Customers will still pay for things they love. They’ll still renew contracts where they’re getting real value. The fundamentals haven’t broken.\nWhat Has Broken (And Why)\nHere’s what ISN’T working anymore, even if your core product works fine:\n#1. “We Have More Features Than Anybody Else”\nThis used to be table stakes. A decade ago, adding features was how you won competitive battles. Every sales call sounded like “Well actually, our version does THIS, which yours doesn’t.”\nCustomers would nod politely and take notes because having options made them feel empowered. Today? No one cares. Everyone now expects you to have all the features. In fact, being feature-poor has become an advantage in many cases.", "words": 275, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone. The “new normal” is that you can win big with AI if you are truly great and have an epic product. And for most, that means leaving your old playbook behind. For years, we’ve been told that enterprise software was dead—that every startup needs to become a fintech or a consumer app or go hybrid. But here’s what I think is actually happening right now: The best products in SaaS aren’t dying at all. In fact, they’re thriving like never before—because when customers finally get access to them via AI agents, they realize just how much better life could be without humans in the loop. 🤖 AI Agents Are Unlocking the Real Value in Software We've talked about this on SaaStr many times recently, but it's even more true today than ever. The real value in any piece of software isn't the features—it's the knowledge embedded within. When you automate repetitive tasks, you're not just saving time—you're unlocking access to expertise, data, and capabilities that were previously locked away in spreadsheets, documentation, and human brains. This is why tools like Cursor exploded—they're not just code editors; they're containers for engineering intelligence. What Changed in Enterprise SaaS The AI agent explosion has created two distinct markets: one where price matters (the \"bargain bin\" tier), another where speed, accuracy, and reliability matter more. 💸 The Bargain Bin Market: Where Price Still Matters Many companies are still buying", "words": 249, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "words": 14, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "So I've been doing this for almost two decades, and it's never been harder than right now to be a public company founder CEO — or even just an employee.\nThe markets are brutal. The AI hype is intense but unproven at scale. And if you're growing under 20%, you're getting carved up by everyone from your employees to your investors to your customers.\nBut here's what nobody wants to say out loud:\nSaaS isn't dead. In fact, we're spending more on software today than ever before.\n$1 trillion annually. Up from $600 billion five years ago.\nWe're not buying less software. We're buying different software. And that changes everything.\nI'm going to break down exactly where things stand right now. What's real. What's noise. And most importantly, how founders can win in this new reality.\nBecause there's still plenty of opportunity — but only if you understand which way the wind is actually blowing.\nFirst off: let me put any concerns around whether SaaS is \"dead\" into perspective with some numbers. It makes no sense. Because we spend more every year on software than ever before! Even as overall enterprise IT budgets declined last quarter, cloud software grew faster than anything else.\nThat's why Microsoft stock was up 35% over the past twelve months while everyone else got hammered.\nAnd when I look across my portfolio companies, none of them are struggling because they sold too much software. They're struggling because they didn't sell enough software that matters anymore. So let's take a deep dive on what's really happening. Why AI hype doesn't equal revenue growth.", "words": 267, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma just reported Q1 FY25 results that are genuinely staggering.\n$304 million quarterly revenue, up from $219 million year-over-year (+39% YoY)\n31% annualized growth rate (reacceleration after slowing to ~20-21%)\nNon-GAAP operating margin of +2%\n13 million monthly active users—up 23% YoY\nNet dollar retention at 124%, with enterprise NRR exceeding 130%\nWhat makes this particularly remarkable? This isn’t just “good numbers.” These are the kind of numbers where you start wondering if your model is broken because everyone else looks like they’re still growing at half this pace.\nThe Metrics Breakdown: Why This Is Actually Unprecedented for a Public Company\nRevenue Growth:\n$304M → $219M YoY = 39% increase\nThat’s not rounding error—it’s a step function jump.\n31% Annualized Growth Rate\nfor a company approaching $1B ARR puts it squarely in the top tier of software companies globally.\nProfitability Evolution:\nGAAP Operating Loss narrowed to ($15.8) → GAAP Operating Income of $22.6M (a swing of almost -$38M)\nNon-GAAP Operating Margin hit positive territory (+2%) while maintaining investment-grade cash flow\nThis is the rare instance where profitability actually improved quarter over quarter while still investing heavily.\nUser Metrics That Matter More Than Revenue Numbers\n13 Million Monthly Active Users\n→ Up 23% YoY. At scale, only Slack and Discord have grown faster among public B2B companies.\nUsers paying $10K+ annually doubled YOY\nto 9,000 customers—a clear sign enterprise adoption has accelerated dramatically.\nWhy This Matters Beyond the Headlines\nLet me be blunt here: SaaS was dead. Or so it seemed. We all thought we were in a downturn. That AI would eat us alive. That the TAM had been tapped out.", "words": 271, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back. The market's been telling us for months that AI infrastructure spending will save software. Figma just proved it with numbers so good they're almost unbelievable. 🤯 Three Numbers That Make This Deal Insane (And Explain Why It Matters) $304,000,000 ARR in Q1 alone - that’s more than most enterprise software companies do all year. 47% YoY growth at this scale is unheard of. Average deal size grew from $6K to $12K over two years while maintaining 90%+ gross margins. In an era where everyone worries about macro impacts and budget constraints, Figma isn't just growing—it's accelerating. They've crossed into the rarefied air of \"decacorn\" territory ($10B+ valuation), which means investors believe not only can they keep this up but that it gets even better from here. What Makes This Different From Every Other B2B Growth Story? Most B2B companies see their largest deals get bigger through expansion revenue or second-order purchases. Figma customers aren’t buying more seats—they’re buying new products entirely because existing ones have become essential workflows. This is what category creation looks like. Here are three ways Figma proves they're building something genuinely different: 🏢 Enterprise Adoption Speed Is Unprecedented Figma went from zero Fortune 500 companies to 50% penetration in under five years. Their customer base now includes every major tech company plus banks like Goldman Sachs, J", "words": 239, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "x", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter.  Growing 40%.\n\nAnd Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "words": 18, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "So you may not have heard, but Figma just put out its first earnings call as a public company.\nAnd it was … epic. The numbers are so good they almost don't make sense:\n$304 million revenue in Q1 (April to June). Up from $219 million a year ago.\nThat's acceleration at scale.\nA full point of improvement in net retention — up to 123% NRR this quarter.\nGross margins stayed strong at 76%.\nThe stock popped 14% on day one after the results came out.\nAnd here's what I think matters most for all of us building B2B software today:\nFigma grew 40% last quarter while spending $100 million more than it did a year ago.\nThat's the difference between growth and hustle versus actual execution. They're growing faster than ever AND investing aggressively into their product roadmap.\nNow let me break down exactly why these numbers matter if you're running or building any kind of SaaS business.", "words": 159, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}], "rag": [{"id": "gold_001", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "So I had this interesting conversation recently with a very senior IT leader at one of SaaStr Fund’s portfolio companies that sells into enterprises, especially larger ones (think $100m+ ACV).  They are doing well now, but when they started off, they didn’t really understand how to sell up.  To the CIO level, the VP level, the business process owners.\nA few years back, we did a deep dive on “how to sell to CIOs” with the CIO of Workday,\na great session you can watch here:\nBut my experience is most founders aren’t sure how to do it.   They want their product to be used by everyone across an enterprise, ideally starting with the CEO and going all the way down through sales reps and engineers.  But most CEOs don’t know enough to buy software themselves, no matter how cool it is.  And most CIOs will want nothing to do with anyone who hasn’t worked in tech for decades, unless they’ve been truly vetted as part of their own network.  That leaves VPs of Sales and Engineering … often the #2 and #3 folks in any org.  Not exactly what many start-ups want to try to sell to.\nThe thing is, if you can get past them — they’ll almost always buy more than just your core app.  More features, more seats, more modules, etc.  So there is real revenue there, even with just an 8-person team.  It’s hard to close big deals without a brand behind you.  Or so it seems.\nHere was our experience at Adobe Sign / EchoSign:\nWe closed some pretty big deals early on ourselves, but once we got past a few $500k-$1m deals, we needed a partner with us.  We added a local reseller, and then later, a channel manager, and then eventually, a global channel director.  Later still, we hired a Global Channel Head from Salesforce to build out a full channel strategy.", "words": 320, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "x", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_001", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answer": "I've been doing this for a while now. And I have a pet peeve that I want to share with you today, because there's just so much bad information out here on how start-ups sell to enterprises, especially big companies and to CIOs. Everyone wants to go into Salesforce and Workday and Oracle and all these big places. And people will give you advice like \"Oh yeah, you gotta get on airplanes. You gotta build a team.\" But no one ever tells you what actually happens once you're inside those doors. So let me tell you my story, because we started off as eight person company selling to CIOs. And if you know anything about SaaS and enterprise sales, you'll hear something different than what most people are telling you. My first real job was at Adobe Sign / EchoSign back when it was a six-person company. It didn't take us long to figure out we had to sell to CIOs. Our product was great. We had 50,000 customers using our free version. But they wanted to buy more from us. They needed security, compliance, audit trails, all this stuff. But we couldn't even ship any of that ourselves yet. So we decided to sell directly to CIOs. This was crazy. Most people say you can't sell to CIOs unless you're already huge. Well, maybe you can't sell to their budget owners per se. But you can definitely sell to their point guys. Their help desk, their business systems analysts, whoever's responsible for deploying new software within your organization. What we found out early on is that this social contract exists between vendors and IT folks. Here's how it works:\nFirst, you show up. That means you respond to emails fast. You answer calls quickly.", "words": 295, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So we’ve done so many SaaStr deep dives on Monday before, I didn’t think there was much more to learn.  But boy, I was wrong!  A lot has changed since our last deep dive:\nMonday IPO’d\nand is now worth $15B (!)\nhas gone multi-product\nand added Marketing and IT as its third core vertical (in addition to Sales & Service) — although we’re still seeing the earliest signs here\nhas become very enterprise\n, with almost half their customers paying $50k+ annually\nis going global fast,\nwith over 100 countries using its product\nAnd that’s just for starters!!   The latest data:\n$500M ARR, Growing 93%\n110% NRR overall, And 125% From Its Largest Accounts\n126% Revenue Growth Year Ago When It Was At $240,000,000 in ARR\nA Stunning 86% Growth Rate in New Customers This Year\nWhat We Can All Learn:\n\n#1. 120% NRR from 10-Seat Deals = Plenty Of Room For Expansion\n.  As you can see below, Monday gets even better NRR once your deal goes into double digits.  So if you haven’t really gone all-in on adding more products after $10k deals close … well, maybe start ASAP.\n#2.  85% NRR from Smaller Customers Still Drives Them To $250m ARR and Beyond\n.  Even small accounts grow quickly.  You don’t need perfect NRR at first.  Just enough to scale quickly.\n#3.  40% of Customers Use More Than One Product, Up From 28% a Year Ago.  Push Harder Here\n.  Another example of how important multiple products often are.  Especially when they aren’t truly natural extensions like Slack + HypeChat.\n#4.  60% of Customers Are Outside North America, and That’s Where They Are Really Accelerating\n.  This is a reminder to go where your competition isn’t strong.", "words": 290, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500M in ARR #1. “Monday was founded by an Israeli company called Wix in 2010 as part of its product suite for building websites, allowing users to build work management tools on top of their existing website. It later spun out into an independent entity” — a reminder that even a huge public SaaS leader like monday can be created inside another tech leader!", "words": 70, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500,000,000 in ARR\n\n#1.  Still Growing a Stunning 89% Year-on-Year At Almost Half a Billion in ARR (!)\nMonday is just one of those SaaS leaders that just never stops.  While some see their space as mature, others like Monday keep on accelerating.\n\n#2.  Customers With Multiple Products Buy Much More — Up To 250% More Revenue Per ACV\nWe’ve seen this again and again.  A leader with multiple products doesn’t get the full benefit until they can sell them together to more and more customers.\n\n#3.  70% of Its Revenue From Outside North America\nWhile many of us think of SaaS as being mainly U.S.-centric, most Cloud leaders", "words": 115, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_002", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answer": "So we've talked on SaaStr a lot over the years about Monday.com. I think when they first IPO'd, many folks thought they were just a project management tool. But boy, what's happened since then. It was a great example of going multi-product early, which we do love to talk about, how that can really help you scale faster and longer than being single product. They now have four core products all powering their 100 million ARR run rate.\nBut one thing I wanted to dig into here today because it's something I want founders listening to think carefully about is this question of how do you balance your existing base versus investing in new markets? Because Monday's been doing well for years. And so, if you're seeing big disruption coming from AI agents, maybe even if your business is doing well, you might need to invest more there. Let me break down five interesting learnings from Monday.com. Number one, they are still growing a stunning 85% at 250 million dollars in ARR.\nThat's incredible, especially given where we are right now in software. Now, as part of that, they are benefiting from price increases, which is common in vertical SaaS. So, while 80% of their growth comes from net new customers, 20% comes from pricing increases. So, that's important to keep in mind as we look at some of these ratios later on. This is fairly common in vertical SaaS where you're charging more per seat, often as you go upmarket.", "words": 251, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "I’ve been doing this for quite some time now.  And I’m convinced there’s one thing that truly helps candidates stand out … besides being super smart:\nIt shows they have done their homework on the company before the interview\nAnd in particular,\nthey show up with at least one very good question for each interviewer\nthat isn’t just generic.\nA bit more here:\nSo what makes a candidate really stand out? In any role?\nHaving done the research ahead of time and bringing 2-10+ thoughtful, insightful questions for\neach\ninterviewer?\nYes, those questions can be simple. But they also should be thoughtful and insightful. It shows respect for the position, and the job itself. You know, when most folks hear “thoughtful” — they think “easy.” They think someone who asks thoughtful questions has asked easy ones. That doesn’t matter as much as having thought about your answers.\nHow do we build a high-performing team? How do we increase revenue per lead? What does success look like in this role? The best candidates ask questions that demonstrate their ability to learn, grow, and contribute to the organization immediately. Ask open-ended questions that encourage sharing information rather than yes/no responses. If you’re interviewing for an entry-level position, ask how the company approaches training new hires and professional development. For management roles, inquire about strategic initiatives and expectations for performance.", "words": 227, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO The best job candidates do one thing well: they know what they’re talking about. They prepare ahead of time by researching the company, practicing their responses to common interview questions, and preparing thoughtful questions for the interviewer. When you walk into an interview knowing your stuff, you’ll make a stronger impression on potential employers. It’s that simple. Most people just wing it when going through interviews. They show up late, wear casual clothes, and seem unprepared. This sends a negative message to the interviewer and makes them question whether you’d be reliable as an employee. Instead, focus on being prepared. Research the company beforehand so you can ask intelligent questions during the interview. Prepare examples of times where you overcame challenges or achieved success in your previous roles. And always dress professionally – it shows respect for the position and helps build trust with the interviewer. By putting in this extra effort, you’ll increase your chances of standing out positively in any job interview.", "words": 177, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "x", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_003", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answer": "So we've talked over the years on SaaStr how to do well at job interviews. What's different today? Well, first, I think there's so much competition for jobs these days that if you're going into any interview without preparation, knowing your stuff cold, having done your research ahead of time, you're probably wasting your time. That's always been true, but it's even truer now. Second, it seems like most folks just aren't as hungry anymore. They're sort of doing the bare minimum. So if you can find some way to show up with high energy and a real desire to truly make a difference where you work, I bet that will carry the day. And finally, we all know that AI has changed everything. We've talked before on this channel about whether we should be excited about AI changing our roles, or worried because they could take them away. The reality is, it does both. But one thing is clear. If you haven't figured out what you're going to do with AI in your role, get on it fast. Because everyone else is. You'll fall behind quickly. Now let me give you two questions you ask during an interview that will immediately stand out as thoughtful and interesting:\n\nWhat did you learn from someone who failed in their last role?\nAnd then secondly,\nwhat was something you were proud of that didn't go exactly according to plan?\n\nThese two answers almost always stand out as good ones. They show you've thought deeply about things and have learned lessons. When I interviewed hundreds of SDRs and AEs, I never heard anyone say, \"Hey, I worked really hard, closed lots of deals.\" No one says that. It doesn't matter. Everyone thinks they close lots of deals. So instead, they'd tell me stories about how they helped a CEO build his brand by writing blog posts, or how they got creative and built a community. Those are the types of answers that stood out.", "words": 331, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had an incredible lineup for our first episode with Jay Levy (Zelkova), John Frankel (GGV) and Mary D’Onofrio (BV).\nThis week we’re focusing on Mark Roberge, co-founder and managing director at Stage 2 Capital!", "words": 60, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "x", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_004", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answer": "So this one I think we've talked to before on SaaStr. It's Mark Roberge, co-founder managing director stage two capital. And I'm really excited he's here because mark is an incredible researcher. He was CRO at HubSpot for many years, built out their sales team with very high efficiency. So that's what we're gonna do today. We'll dig into what makes scaling up sales teams work, how you can scale more efficiently than most folks seem to be able to do so far. Mark\n00:01:30\nOkay. Thanks jay. So my goal today is just to share some research insights as well as practical experience around building high velocity revenue organizations. And I believe there are three core elements that I'd like to focus on. The first is understanding the demand side of your business. What does the market look like? How big is it? Who should you target based upon who will buy now versus later? The second piece is hiring the right people for each segment of the funnel. And the third piece is ensuring alignment across all parts of the organization. These are things that have evolved over time. When I started off doing research back in the day, these were kind of the foundational principles. But as the company scales, they become even more important. Now let me start by talking a little bit about demand generation. In particular, we did a lot of research recently looking at different types of customers.", "words": 245, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "In a stunning deal announced on July 29, 2025,\nSalesforce will acquire Informatica for approximately $8 billion\n—a move that completes an extraordinary journey spanning over three decades for one of enterprise software’s most resilient companies. This isn’t just another acquisition; it’s the culmination of a remarkable odyssey that took Informatica through two IPOs, multiple near-death experiences, and a transformation so dramatic it would make even the most seasoned SaaS veterans take notice. The Story That Defines Resilience\nFounded in 1988 by Gaurav Dhillon and Diaz Nesamoney as a small startup in Menlo Park, California, Informatica began life focused on data integration solutions. What started as a “data warehousing” company quickly evolved into something much bigger—becoming the world’s largest independent pure-play enterprise software company. But this wasn’t your typical Silicon Valley success story. Informatica faced challenges that would break lesser companies cold:\nThe Dot-Com Crash (1999)\nInformatica went public during the peak dot-com era in May 1999. When the bubble burst, shares plummeted. The company found itself struggling financially, eventually being forced to lay off nearly half of its workforce. Yet rather than folding, they used their $500 million war chest to survive—and transform. The Private Equity Rescue (2009)\nAfter years of fighting, Informatica found new life under private equity ownership in 2009 when Permira led a consortium buying out the firm for roughly $5.3 billion. Under new leadership, including former IBM executive Anant Jha and CFO Michael Bleier, they set about rebuilding what had been destroyed.", "words": 247, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "words": 15, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "words": 15, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_005", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answer": "So what do you know about Informatica?  I'm sure some folks have heard of it -- they've been around since '92! But beyond that, probably less than half this audience has any real familiarity.\nBut maybe if we dig into one case study, we'll all learn something. What can we learn from Informatica?\nIt's just so wild. So let me tell you just a few things about it. It started off as data integration software back in 1992. And it did pretty well for years, then went private in 2006. Came public again in 2021, did reasonably well, hit an all-time high in late 2021, then fell like everything else on AI worries. And now today, after more than 30 years, it's being acquired by Salesforce for almost $8 billion.\nWow. Just wow.\nLet me take a step back. Salesforce's journey to where it is today, it was a bit different. They were founded way back in 1999 as an email service, became a CRM product, came public in 2004, and today they're a multibillion-dollar company growing at 20%. How much would your money be worth if you'd invested in Salesforce when it first went public? If you bought shares in that IPO back in 2004, you might feel pretty good about yourself right now because their stock price has gone up over 9,000 percent. At least on paper. That's the power of compounding.\nFrom there, let's look at another great example: Datadog. Now you might say, \"Wait a minute, isn't Datadog a cybersecurity company?\" Well, yes, they are. But they also serve a related space called observability, which helps developers troubleshoot problems in their applications faster. And here's the interesting thing – they were able to combine these two markets to achieve impressive results. Their stock is trading near a record high. This week alone, Datadog rallied nearly 19%, marking its largest percentage gain in seven months.\nThen there's ServiceTitan. Now this is really cool. It's a software platform designed specifically for home service contractors. Think electricians, plumbers, HVAC repair people, pest control professionals. The founders realized these businesses often struggle to keep track of scheduling, inventory management, payments, and customer communications, so they built a comprehensive toolset to address those pain points. And here's the kicker – it doesn't require a lot of technical expertise to use.", "words": 389, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "So we’re getting close to 100,000 subscribers on our YouTube channel,\nhere\n!\nAnd also … it just passed 5,000,000 total video views!!  We’ve been doing YouTube since Year One (2015), so this is something we can be proud of.\nIt really does take work to do a good job here:\n– You have to make high quality content\n– Do at least 2 videos a week consistently over time\n– Promote your YouTube Channel everywhere possible\n– Not give up after you see others grow faster than you.  Don’t let anyone tell you that “YouTube doesn’t work”\nWe’ll talk more on how to build out your own YouTube strategy in an upcoming post and deep dive soon.\nBut for now — I wanted to share our top sessions of all time, as well as some great ones that didn’t quite hit the Top 10 list …\nTop YouTube Sessions Of All-Time (#1-#10):\n#1 How To Pitch VCs with Y Combinator’s Michael Seibel – by far the #1 session ever done\n#2 How I Went From $0 to $100m+ ARR With My Own Sales Team with David Skok\n#3 What Every Founder Should Know About Raising Venture Capital with Bill Gurley\n#4 A Step-by-Step Guide On How To Hire Your First Great VP of Marketing with MarTech Fund’s Mark Roberge\n#5 How to Scale from $20M to $500M+ ARR | Lessons Learned Building Zendesk and Pipedrive with Morten Wenzel, Co-founder & CEO of Zendesk\n#6 SaaS Metrics That Matter with Tidemark CTO and former Salesforce CMO Steve Wood\n#7 The Keys to Scaling with Eventbrite CEO Rebecca Hasson\n#8 15 Unconventional Learnings from Selling to BigCos with Adobe Sign / EchoSign CEO, Dario Amodei\n#9 Key Takeaways from Selling to the Enterprise with Box VP Corporate Development, JimThompson + More!!\n#10", "words": 301, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "SaaStr's YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time! What are you watching?", "words": 17, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views.  Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_006", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answer": "Hi everyone. I think we can all agree that content marketing works well for us,\nas long as you do it right. And one thing I've learned over the years is there are many different types of content marketing. There's your blog. There's podcasting. We're going to be doing more podcasting at SaaStr this year than ever before because so many folks like it. There are webinars. You know some people love them. Some people hate them. But they work really well if you market them properly. And then there are videos on YouTube. Now, it turns out, when I first started my career as an investor and VP marketing, maybe eight or nine years ago now, I didn't even use social media much at all. Didn't tweet too often. Didn't put up any slides online until recently. So, I was pretty surprised how powerful video would become. If you go back just a few years, our top video had barely hit 100,000 views. It wasn't even close. Today, by contrast, our top video has almost 300,000 views. That's pretty cool. What does that mean? Why should you care? First off, with each view, we get paid a small amount of money. So, these views are monetized. More importantly though, it helps spread the word. This content gets shared again and again. It gets embedded into LinkedIn posts and tweets and everything else. So, if you create great content, don't worry if you don't have millions of followers yet. Don't worry if you don't have lots of subscribers. As long as you create great stuff, eventually those things will compound. Alright, let me take a look at what's been working best. Okay, so number ten here, which we'll get to in a minute, has 30,000 views. Number nine has 34,000 views.", "words": 301, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "So I’ve been doing this for a while now.  I started off helping sell Adobe Sign / EchoSign to our very first enterprise customers back in 2010-2011 (more on how those early days were here):\nThen, when I got to my next startup, I was CRO at a time when every B2B company seemed to have just 1 or 2 reps.  So I didn’t really see any change there, per se.  But then I joined another startup later as CEO, and saw everything change:\nThe number of vendors went from 20 or so to hundreds\nThe sheer amount of data and tools grew dramatically.  As did the complexity of selling them\nThe number of people you needed to close deals grew from 2–3 folks on the buyer side to 100s\nAnd by 2023, most B2B companies had an entire army of people managing your account.  From FDEs to Product Managers, from Solution Engineers to Technical Account Executives — everyone wanted a piece of you\nAll this stuff happened between 2010 and today.  And yet … it wasn’t really that much change.\nBut now?  Everything is changing again.  Not because of price increases, or even macro impacts.  No, something far bigger than that.\nIt’s because the products themselves are getting smarter.  That’s why everything is changing again in SaaS sales.  At least, if you want to stay ahead.\nWhy?\nBecause of the explosion of AI features across business applications.  More than ever before, they can do the work.  So why should you need an army of human professionals to manage your account?  Or help deploy it?  Or answer your questions?\nThis is why Salesforce Agentforce, Slack, HubSpot, ZoomInfo, and others are investing so heavily in agentic workflows.  Because their apps aren’t just going to get better.", "words": 295, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years If you’ve been doing B2B sales even just a little while — you know what the last 15-20 years have brought. - An explosion of roles. From SMB to enterprise, every traditional SaaS company started hiring: - A Head of Sales - SDRs (new) - Account Executives (not really new but more senior ones than ever before) - Customer Success Managers - Support Specialists - Solutions Engineers - Renewal Managers And so much more - The need for specialized human-based processes, especially in mid-market and larger deals. Outbound, Inbound, Qualification, etc. - All these folks had to work together as a true team. Even if they didn’t always like each other. - So this was the playbook for the past two decades or so. It’s worked well. But … now? That entire playbook seems poised to go into the bin. Not all at once, nor everywhere. But I think we’re on the cusp of change in how startups sell B2B products for real. And there are several factors behind this change. First, the rise of AI agents has opened our eyes to what can be automated in GTM. Second, many in GTM aren’t needed anymore. No one wants to meet in person anymore, no matter where they are. Third, most founders want to do it themselves again. At least some of it. This isn’t just about cost savings, although that matters too. This is also about wanting to get back closer to customers", "words": 255, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "x", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "words": 12, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_007", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answer": "I was at this event called the SaaStr Annual. It's an annual conference for SaaS founders. And I had the chance to catch up with a number of folks there. One of them was a VP of Sales who had been running his own company. He'd sold it for $500 million. But he wasn't doing anything since then. He said, \"Jason, what do you think about these new age of AI tools?\" And I said, \"Well, they're exciting.\" And he goes, \"Yeah, but how much is going to change?\"\nAnd I looked at him and I said, \"This is probably the biggest change in sales in 20 years.\"\nHe said, \"Really? What are you talking about?\"\nI said, \"The whole way we work today, the whole way we sell, the whole way we work together. If you look at just the last two weeks, everything's different.\" Even if your headcount hasn't changed yet, things have already fundamentally changed in sales. Let me tell you why.\nLet's take a few examples of changes we've seen in the last couple of months. First one, SDRs. In the early days of SaaS, when we started selling Salesforce.com back in the day, we didn't even know what an SDR was. But by around 2010-2011, everyone knew what an SDR was. And the job became very productized. You could buy a tool like SalesLoft, which later became Outreach. Or you could hire someone to manage those people and make sure they were qualified. These roles grew rapidly from '08 to 2020. So did human support. That really didn't exist until late in the game. Early on, support was always handled by engineering or by someone else outside the organization. Later, companies figured out how to create a separate function for support and added it to their org chart. Then came customer success.", "words": 307, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So there’s been a lot of worry among VCs lately that public multiples have fallen so far — and will fall even further.  That startups are overvalued, that they need to raise at crazy valuations just to get funded, etc.  The point being raised here is that for a VC to make money on an investment … he has to sell those shares later at least 5x-10x what he paid in.\nAt current IPO prices, this won’t happen often enough:\nThe average SaaS IPO price-to-sales multiple today is around 7.6x. If you take out the outliers (like Snowflake), it drops to ~4.8x. And if you’re looking at private companies with similar revenue profiles, their multiples are half again lower—around 3-3.5x sales.\nIn 2019, that same group averaged 14x. In 2018? They hit 25x. And back when Datadog went public in 2019, they were trading at roughly 40x forward revenues.\nThat means your Series C might be worth $100 million instead of $300 million, depending how fast you grow. It means founders who could’ve taken money off the table now aren’t able to because every round feels like a necessity rather than an option. It means if you don’t go public by Year 15, you’ll probably miss your best exit opportunity entirely. The math works against the “just keep going” strategy. But that doesn’t mean it’s wrong. Even if your next funding round costs more because of market conditions, you still need to fund yourself through the inevitable downturns. At some point, markets cycle back up. IPO windows open again. Growth rates matter more than ever. The company that keeps running while everyone else freezes becomes the obvious acquisition target—and sometimes, as we saw with Salesforce acquiring Slack at peak valuation, that’s exactly what happens.\nSo do these worries matter?\nYes.\nFor one thing, the concern that venture capitalists can’t invest unless they believe they can easily double their money isn’t baseless.", "words": 321, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? We recently spoke with two successful SaaS founders who were each asked by their board to share what worries them most about the business right now. Both shared they worried that public multiples had dropped too low for acquisitions. But how much should you worry if your VCs are worried about public multiples? It depends on whether they will actually impact M&A activity. If there's no real impact -- then just tell them you'll see where things go. They probably don't care as much as it seems. And if they do have an impact -- then talk through together why this might be good news for you. Why would lower acquisition offers be such good news? In general, I'd say the vast majority of startups aren't going to get acquired. So a few less offers isn't really bad. A few more offers often comes out of nowhere. The best ones do. Even when times seem tougher. Just make sure any change in strategy doesn't hurt the odds here.", "words": 181, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "x", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_008", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answer": "So you've heard this conversation before, I'm sure. It's one that we hear again and again as part of every single board meeting now for a while. \"I know this company has some issues. But it does have great fundamentals. And if multiples were back to what they used to be when I invested, well then it would still work out.\"\nAnd your investors are right. We're all worried. In fact, there was an interesting survey done by Carta with PwC that showed almost half of venture capital firms are less likely to invest today than they were even just six months ago because of fear of losing money on their investments. Fear of losing money. Because public market multiples aren't where they once were. So if I could share some advice here, my number one piece of advice is don't obsess over what you think your VC might be thinking. If your investors want to buy more stock, they will. They'll figure out how much. But it really doesn't matter so long as you're building a real business, which you clearly are. You guys are doing fine. The reality is, no investor can do anything that meaningfully impacts your equity. No investor can sell any material amount of shares until the next round comes up at least. This isn't something that happens quickly enough. Even if you see a lot of activity around secondary markets these days, those deals take time to close and then another 12-18 months after that to get sold into the public markets. A few years later, maybe, you can find someone who wants to sell their stock. But you won't need them to help you survive the short term.", "words": 285, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So we’ve taken a look at many SaaS leaders over the years on our New New Podcast and Live Wednesday series.  But one I think we haven’t done enough is Atlassian.  Because they’re so interesting.  They started off as a developer tool and got pretty big (and then even bigger).  It took them a while to really go enterprise, but when they did … wow.\n$2.7 billion in ARR\nGrowing 21%, with\n37% Subscription Growth\nThat’s just incredible efficiency for an enterprise play!\n25% Free Cash Flow Margins (!)\nAnd importantly — it gets better:\n100% NRR\nNot bad for a product that doesn’t have sales reps!  More on this below\n110% GRR / Net Retention Rate\nA related metric, but higher than NRR because it includes upsells, etc.  And again, without traditional sales support.  Wow.\nIncredibly Efficient Hiring\nOnly 230 employees to serve 300,000 customers!  A stunning ratio.\nWhat’s more impressive?  This was all during Covid.\nLet’s take a look at some learnings:\n\n#1.  60% of its Business Customers Are Outside Tech\nWhile half their users are techies, 60% of their customers outside tech, and 40% of those are “non-technical” departments like HR and finance.  So don’t worry too much if your early leads aren’t technically savvy.  Just make sure you can get them into production quickly.  And keep the product simple enough to deploy there.\n#2.  100% NRR, With 50% of Revenue From Self-Serve + Freemium Editions\nAtlassian does have sales, but a lot less than most of us.  Most of its top partners are solution providers.  That means Atlassian has gotten almost 100% NRR out of self-service and freemium editions alone!  That’s what it takes to scale efficiently.", "words": 281, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian as It Approaches $4 Billion in ARR. Not too Shabby! #1. A Record Number of Customers Are Adding More Seats Than Before -- Even Now. This Is What You Want. The engine that drives SaaS growth is seat expansion by existing customers. And even today, with many cut spending elsewhere, most Atlassian customers are adding more seats than they did before. That's what you want to see.", "words": 72, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "words": 11, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_009", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answer": "So there was just one SaaS IPO this year -- Cloudflare. But we've also seen some pretty big M&A deals that were interesting. And I want to do a deep dive on one because it's been an incredible journey. It started off as two engineers living out of Mike Cannon-Brookes garage in Melbourne, Australia, building Jira. That they launched back in '02. They raised a tiny bit of capital early, then bootstrapped all the way until their Series A round. They did that, and that took them through almost $100 million in revenue, which then led to an IPO. The IPO came, and what happened? Well, the market was tough after Covid. The markets weren't great, but when Atlassian went public at $25 billion post-money valuation, today it's worth $40 billion. Wow! So that's a crazy journey. Now you can go look at the chart yourself, but basically, it started off with some decent times. In 2017, they had a great year where they grew 48%. Then they had some tougher times during Covid. Growth slowed down to 14%, but then they bounced back like many others have. Today, Atlassian has become a $4 billion run rate company, with 28% non-GAAP operating margin, so more than half its profits are free cash flow. Its stock trades at over 20x forward earnings. What's really cool though is how they're doing it. You see, they're not resting on their laurels. Even though they're at almost $4 billion in ARR, they're planning to get to $10 billion by 2030. Wow! How? By investing in AI, cloud native apps, and developer workflows. These three areas. Let me break each of those down quickly. First, let's talk about AI.", "words": 284, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "blog", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "Q:  I’m an SaaS founder who’s seen his NRR go from ~130% to <80%. My customers complain that my product isn’t as good as before. They say that it’s too complicated now and that there aren’t enough humans on staff to help them with any issues. The churn rate has been very high this quarter. In fact, our revenue growth is just 10%. How do I turn things around? It sounds like you’ve become one of those “NRR Zombies” we see so often these days. A classic example:\nYour NRR has dropped below 90%, which means your existing customer base won’t grow organically anymore\n. And when that happens, no amount of new logo sales will save you. Your new bookings have to be significantly higher than your existing business just to keep up — let alone grow faster. As soon as you fall behind here, all hope fades quickly. We’ll talk about what to do after this, but first, let me share some examples of how bad it gets once you hit this state. For example, RingCentral fell into this trap, going from epic hypergrowth (50%-60%) … to zombie mode. At $600m+ ARR, its NRR had fallen to 95%. That meant it could only get so much out of its massive installed base. And so growth slowed dramatically to single digits. And then Slack went through something similar. Its NRR stayed at 98% but the stock was crushed because its growth stalled. Even though their net retention remained strong, Wall Street lost faith. Why did this happen? Because they couldn’t deliver the same level of value to their core customers anymore. This happened again and again across many Cloud leaders during the last downturn in 2019-2020. So here’s what I’d suggest. First,\ndon’t blame anyone but yourself. The market didn’t change overnight\n. Second, make sure you really know why your NRR dropped. Did you raise prices without adding enough value?", "words": 322, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "words": 8, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "x", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "words": 8, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_010", "medium": "talk", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answer": "So we've talked before on SaaStr about how I call this the age of AI. And that really started last year when AI exploded into business software. But now we're entering the Age of Atonement, because all these companies that didn't keep up with change have fallen off the edge of the earth. This is happening right now. So many leaders just aren't keeping up with the new pace of change in B2B and SaaS. They are falling behind so fast today, that their customers are leaving them faster than ever. And one simple way to measure this is net revenue retention. Net Revenue Retention. We'll get to what it means, why it matters and why you should care later. But first let me tell you some stories about folks who haven't kept up. Because there are two types of founders out there today. There are zombies and non-zombies. Non-Zombies are still getting better. Their NRR is above 110%. Even as things get harder, they're finding a way to grow at least 10 points higher than NRR. The Zombies though... they're dead. No matter what anyone says. The Zombies' NRR has been below 90% for quite a while. Maybe even below 80%. And no matter how hard they try, no matter how much marketing budget they put into it, no matter how many salespeople they hire — nothing seems to work. At all. Now some will say well, maybe I'm doing fine at $30 million in ARR and 85% NRR. Or whatever it might be. Well, here's my rule:\nDon't settle for less growth than Your NRR + 20%.\nThat's your floor, period. It doesn't mean you won't struggle. It doesn't mean you won't face challenges. It doesn't mean you won't have upsells fail sometimes. Of course it does. That's life. But once you fall below that line, you become a zombie. And after that, everyone knows it. Everyone knows you just aren't growing anymore. And no one wants to buy another seat. No one wants to increase their spend. And the ones you do have? They churn anyway.", "words": 349, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But Are We Going to Miss Another Burn Budget This Month?\nPolish sounds good.  And polish always sounds good coming out of finance.\nBut if you’re missing the monthly burn plan again … well, that’s just too many excuses.\nIn my experience, if a startup misses its monthly cash burn goals even once,\nit never gets back on track after that\n. It’s easy to miss one or two months when things get tough, or when the model was off — especially early-ish in the journey.\nBut then folks sort of stop paying attention to it.\nThen, next thing you know, you’ve missed another month. Then another. They all blend together at some point.\nAnd before you know it, you don’t have enough money anymore. Or rather, you’ll find out you do eventually …\nSo what should you do?\nFirst, start building a real, dynamic model yourself:\nEveryone needs to understand how much we have (Cash Balance) and how quickly it changes (Burn Rate). You need to build this simple model yourself. Not trust someone else here.\nSecond, you really have to set a realistic goal, and challenge everyone to hit it\n. If you aren’t sure what your “burn” goal is for the week/month/quarter/year, then you aren’t going to hit it. Period. So make sure everyone knows exactly what they have to hit each period. Each month. Make them own their piece of it. Everyone has to work to the same goal as CEO and founder.\nThird, no matter what, put aside $500k-$750k in case of a rough patch. That usually covers most startups’ bumps\n. Even if you think you won’t run out of money until late ’25, still take out an extra $600k today.\nFourth, maybe just move on\n.", "words": 300, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? This shouldn’t happen at all once you have real revenue. Once you really know what’s coming in, that you can predict fairly well, there just isn’t any excuse. It’s almost always either incompetence (not enough experience) or lack of care (they don’t truly want to work here). And/or they aren't truly full-time. Whatever the reason, this is a flag. A big one. Your VP Finance/Controller/CFO has to own the plan. They also have to own the cash plan / burn rate plan. Not “own”, but actually do it themselves. So if it's wrong -- who owns it? Well, they do. You can fire them. Or give them a chance to step up. But you cannot tolerate excuses. Because everyone knows by now how many months ahead they need to forecast out. More on that here -> The Real Cost of Capital in SaaS | SaaStr", "words": 167, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?\nBurn rate planning isn’t rocket science — it’s just accounting. Here's how you fix this:\n\nFirst, stop relying on your CFO to do everything. That \"polished\" presentation? It might actually hide problems rather than solve them.\n\nSecond, understand that burn rate planning needs to happen at least once per quarter. Not monthly, unless you're in hypergrowth mode (which seems unlikely given your CFO issues).\n\nThird, don't wait until the end of the month to figure out if you'll hit your targets. That's like flying without navigation. You need regular check-ins throughout the month.\n\nFourth, get your CFO into the rhythm of the business faster", "words": 126, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_011", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But We Are Missing the Monthly Cash Planning Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?\nYour CFO probably has done this before as a public company or at least, multiple times, as a senior finance person.\nBut they haven't done it before as a founder-led start-up.\nSo here's what you have to do:\n\nFirst, just ask them why this hasn't happened yet.\nI bet it's because there isn't one core leader accountable.\nThere almost always isn't in start-ups that don't get this right.\n\nSecond, challenge her/him to create a real plan — a real monthly cash planning model. Not a spreadsheet.\nAnd tell them if she/he doesn't deliver a working model by next week (or whatever timeline) that you'll need to find someone else to do it.\n\nThird, give him/her a clear deadline and KPIs to hit.\nAnd also set up weekly/monthly progress checks. And remind them to follow up when those check-ins happen.\n\nFourth, explain how much stress it takes off everyone if you know exactly where we stand at all times.\n\nFifth, force yourselves to understand, as founders, where the money is going. That's really the job of the CEO. Make sure you know. This is key.\nIt makes the whole exercise worth it.\n\nSixth, push hard to figure out the root cause of why folks aren't following up on tracking this.\nIs it lack of training? Lack of interest? Lack of buy-in? Whatever it is, fix it.\n\nSeventh, use the fact that the most successful startups never miss their monthly burn rate plans against your CFO.", "words": 266, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR?\nThe key here is that you don’t need to buy another product.  If you have Salesforce / HubSpot / etc., then for now at least, just start using what’s already there.\nFor example,\nQualified\nhas seen customers go live in under two weeks — sometimes as fast as one day!\nIn fact, their fastest deployment was less than a week:\nAnother AI GTM leader saw companies going live in “just days” and generating hundreds of thousands of dollars in pipeline per month within the first few months of deployment. One company reported $200K+ in closed-won revenue attributed directly to their AI SDR within its first six weeks. That’s not theoretical ROI—it’s real money coming out of qualified opportunities generated by an AI system running on top of existing CRM data. And\nAgentforce\nfrom Salesforce says they see similar results. They claim that Agent Force has been used more than 100 million times since its launch. So how quickly should we expect to get this up and running? In general, most B2B leaders recommend allowing around four to eight weeks for initial training before expecting meaningful output. But if you’re deploying a tool like Qualified or AgentForce that runs natively inside your CRM, the timeline might be significantly shorter—think two weeks max. The actual deployment process usually breaks down into three phases: setup (a couple hours), daily monitoring/tuning (30-60 minutes), and ongoing optimization over time. For many users, the biggest challenge is learning the tools themselves—not necessarily the technology itself, which is increasingly user-friendly, but understanding how to train them effectively.", "words": 272, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? This is one reason I love Qualified. You just tell it what you want done (in plain English) and it does it. No training, no QA, no nothing. In many cases, just deploy it and it starts working immediately. For example, we told our new AI BDR on Qualified it was Friday at 5pm, so it should reach out to any prospects that came into their site that day. And … it worked! We didn’t even train it. But maybe for more complex use-cases, expect it to take a few weeks to get going if you need to onboard it yourself.", "words": 113, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?\n\nAn AI SDR that works? You need to train it for 30 days before it really starts working. For real. And then you have to keep training it after that.\n\nAnd then … it takes another 90 days to get into a groove. Not every day — but over the course of those first 120 days.\n\nAfter that though, you can scale up to 100s if not thousands of leads per month, depending on how many agents you deploy.", "words": 91, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_012", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answer": "So there's so much confusion around deploying an AI SDR. So I wanted to just give you some learnings on this because we've deployed three different AI agents now at SaaStr itself, two of them are outbound, one inbound. And we also use one for our content review, which was really important for us. But what we found out was that if done right, you don't have to deploy an AI agent over weeks. You can get going almost overnight. Now let me tell you why. First, most humans aren't even checking their email anymore anyway. Email open rates are down to like 0.5%. They're terrible. Nobody checks email the way they used to. So if nobody's reading it, who cares how long it takes to put in an AI agent? Second, we tested all these AI SDRs ourselves before we bought any of them. We tried five different ones and asked them to sell tickets to SaaStr London. And guess what? Three of them were pretty good. Two of them worked great. One had a weird bug where it wouldn't take credit cards, but otherwise they worked fine. What did we find out? Inbound, you want to set up an agent fast because folks are already coming to your website. If you're getting a thousand leads a month, you probably shouldn't wait six months to deploy an AI agent. That's too slow. Because those folks could be meeting with sales reps today. Outbound though, here's my learning. You can train an AI agent to send emails tonight. You don't need to spend weeks training it. Here's why. If you look at our data with Artisan, when we first started, it took maybe half a day to deploy the AI agent, not more than that. And then it took 30 days to tune it. Why? Because once the agent starts sending emails and booking meetings, you start seeing results. You'll see it book a meeting and you'll think, \"Oh man, this works.\" Then you'll go back and adjust the parameters.", "words": 340, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?\nI’d say yes. Here’s why:\nFirst,\n95% of human SDRs don’t really understand what they are selling\n. If you think your SDR team does — well that’s great! But if most humans on sales teams don’t truly get how their own products work … then it’s pretty easy to see why AI can be better here. And we’ve all seen this in practice at so many start-ups:\nThe first few AEs will sell whatever they want. The VP of Sales will push out crazy quotas, even when no one understands how anyone would hit them …\nAnd by the time the first AE leaves (or gets fired), everyone sort of just gives up. It takes too long to train new folks, anyway. So nothing really ever scales beyond the early days.\nSo the odds your SDR team knows enough about your product to close deals is low. Low enough that AI often already wins before you deploy it.\nSecond, 95% of human SDRs can’t answer any real questions\n. This might sound harsh, but it’s true. They’re good at sequences, at sending emails, at reaching out to people in their CRM. But ask them anything more nuanced than that, especially around complex enterprise software, and most won’t know how to respond. And the ones that try to figure it out? Well, they’ll take weeks.\nThird, 95% of human SDRs need help with everything. They need scripts, training, coaching, check-ins every day. They struggle to onboard quickly. And once they finally get going, they plateau fast because they run out of ideas and energy.\nFourth, AI SDRs already win at almost every startup today, even with mediocre human SDRs. In fact, 90%+ of our AI Agents >win< against our mediocre human SDRs\n.", "words": 309, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion? Probably. We’ve seen this pattern play out with founders across every stage: They hire their first “SDR” — who’s really just doing outbound research, scheduling meetings, or sending generic emails that don’t actually sell anything. Then they realize: This isn’t working. The results are terrible. It’s sucking up time and resources without delivering real pipeline. And honestly? Most human SDRs aren’t great at sales anyway. So here’s my honest take: If you’re still figuring out your outbound strategy, if you haven’t cracked the code on what works yet, deploying an AI SDR before you have a human one in place can be brilliant. But only if you deploy it right. Here’s why: AI SDRs don’t make excuses. They don’t need training (or pretend they do). They don’t miss follow-ups because they forgot. They work 24/7. They send perfect outreach sequences. They never get tired of calling back. They never forget to send the follow-up email. What they CAN’T do: They won’t truly understand your prospects like a human would. They’ll struggle with complex conversations or nuanced objections. You still need humans to qualify leads, build relationships, and close deals. But for the 80-90% of your prospecting that ISN’T about relationship building, an AI SDR can absolutely crush it while you figure out the rest. Just remember: Deployment matters more than technology itself. Train it well,", "words": 241, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?\n\nYes.  The reality today is you will get so many more emails with an AI SDR that can work around the clock (with some limitations) than any human could ever manage.\n\nAI can be much better than humans at doing prospecting and qualification before someone wants to buy.\n\nBut if you want true success — you need to train your AI SDR on what worked for your top human reps.\n\nNot just what worked last quarter.  But what worked this week.", "words": 97, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_013", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nYes.  This is almost always correct, even if you're early days.\nThe reality today is that 95% of humans don't know the products they sell (E1) and can't answer tough questions (E2). And they often need help getting back to customers quickly (E3).\nWe've seen this at SaaStr itself. Our own sales team uses Artisan as their primary tool, sending millions of messages per year and achieving an average open rate of 5-6%, which is roughly double what most companies see with outbound emails.\nAnd here's the thing—these results aren't unique to our setup. Other organizations we work with achieve similar performance on their AI SDR tools, including Qualified, Apollo Inbound, and Agentforce.", "words": 129, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet. What Should I Do?\nThis is an issue with many founders that think too much about their own metrics.  First, let me give you some real advice:\nFirst, understand this — 90% YoY Growth May Not Be Enough Today to Really Excite VCs (But That Doesn’t Mean No One Will Want to Fund You)\nLet’s look at what it takes to get funded these days.  And remember, 95% of start-ups fail.  So if nothing else, fundraise enough so that you don’t run out of money before then 🙂\nVCs want to see a clear path to Initial Scale ($20-$50m) ARR in most cases. Even for seed deals,\nmost VCs aren’t going to put up capital without a clear way to get there\n— i.e., high NRR, strong market traction, etc. The math has to work.\nAt ~$8M ARR growing 80%, you might have to show how you’ll grow into something like this within 12-18 months\n. This could include adding another revenue stream, expanding into adjacent markets, hiring experienced leadership to help drive growth, etc.\nIf you can demonstrate a clear plan and timeline for reaching Initial Scale, investors will likely be interested.\nHowever, if you’re simply “hitting the wall” and struggling to scale due to internal or external factors, it may be tougher to secure funding until you’ve figured things out.", "words": 241, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet\n\nAt ~$8m-$9m ARR growing 90%, this is actually pretty hard. A bit more here:", "words": 33, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "words": 19, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_014", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet\nThis question comes up all the time. Folks grow nicely at $20k, $50k, $80k ACV, maybe have some upsells, sometimes a bit more enterprise … and they start to see the venture capital doors close. “Why? I’m growing 70%, 80%, 90%. That should be enough.”\nWell let me tell you why it might not be enough. Let me break this down into two categories, because there are two answers here. And one answer is that if you don’t like the answer — change something. Because you’re not going to be able to change how VCs think about startups quite yet. So first, let’s do what VCs are looking for these days, especially in seed stage and early stage investing. What are folks looking for now? Well I’ll give you my list. First, if you want to invest in AI today, that’s great. Go fund an agent. Or a B2B startup with insane growth and an incredible founder. But if you want to go fund a company growing at 60-80% at $10m ARR, then you better believe you’ve got to be in a hot space. A lot of them aren’t as hot right now. Second, in many cases, VCs would rather wait until later to invest in any given category where they know there will be success. Especially if they already invested earlier. Third, you have to have a strong brand and track record behind you. Fourth, most importantly, you have to be self-sufficient. If you raise less than $5 million, you’re probably not gonna be able to hire enough people to get to say $30m-$50m ARR, and beyond. Now if you’re $5m ARR growing 100%, you’ve raised a big round. You’re self sufficient. You’re going to make it. That’s cool. The next thing is that if you haven’t proven product-market fit by $2m-$3m ARR, it’s hard to convince someone that you will. Even if you have, it can still be tough. And so finally, and most importantly, when we were last doing this stuff regularly, no one wanted to invest unless they could control the outcome.", "words": 365, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So there’s a fairly common objection I hear from traditional SaaS folks when someone suggests deploying an AI agent:\n“Customers won’t trust an AI.”\nAnd often, the next part is “especially if something goes wrong.  They’ll just blame us.”\nBut let me tell you what I saw this week at SaaStr AI London (we had thousands of people attend, record attendance):\nI asked our team — which included two of our best salespeople who’d never worked with AI before —\nif they would trust an AI salesperson.  It was unanimous.  Yes, they said.  They trusted our AI sales reps more than humans.\nThe reasons were simple:\navailability\n— our AI reps could work 24/7, answer questions instantly\nknowledge depth\n— our AI knew everything about SaaStr’s products cold, no research needed\nno BS or lies\n— unlike some human reps, our AI didn’t make stuff up to close deals\nNo excuses. No games. Just help. Why We Built Our Own Agent First\nBefore anyone asks: yes, we built our own internal AI agent first because we couldn’t find anything good off-the-shelf. Here’s what we learned:\nOur internal tool isn’t perfect yet. It has bugs, it can hallucinate sometimes, and it requires daily management. But it works. And that experience made everything else click into place. We went from zero to deployment-ready faster than any vendor promised. What Works Today\nHere’s where we’re actually at right now, based entirely on real data and usage patterns:\nNew!!  Most people that say “buyers won’t trust an AI” haven’t bought software in years, or tried to deploy an AI agent.  If they did, they’d see it’s mostly fine.\nMany customers already interact with AI systems regularly. When something breaks or doesn’t go as expected, they complain to whoever is closest — usually their point of contact.", "words": 302, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "words": 10, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "x", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "words": 10, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_015", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answer": "So I was chatting with someone at another company recently. They were excited about their new AI VP of Sales — which sounds awesome, except that AI VPs aren’t really ready to sell yet. So what did they actually mean? It turns out they meant they’d built an internal tool where when anyone asked something about deals or customers inside their CRM, it would answer questions. Which again, isn’t exactly what an AI agent does, but I’ll get there. Anyway, this person said he could see no one would trust an AI to answer any questions about buying their software. He thought it was silly. Then just the other week I needed help choosing between two vendors. I’m a founder myself, I know the drill. I didn’t want to call anyone, I just wanted to ask an AI. And the thing just made stuff up. No problem. That’s why I wasn’t going to buy anything from them. You probably had a similar experience as a buyer too. Now look, this is important. We all say things sometimes that aren’t true. We stretch data points. We exaggerate a bit. These things happen. But an AI system has no idea if it’s giving you correct information or not. Or worse, maybe it gives you mostly right info, then says something wrong. That just totally destroys credibility. When we’re shopping online now, we expect the same level of transparency and truthfulness from digital systems as we demand from people. If a website makes a claim that turns out to be false, you lose our trust immediately. And honestly, we also expect more from AI than from a lot of salespeople these days. The bar is higher. Don’t let your team pretend otherwise. But here’s my point: AI can already process and synthesize vast amounts of data in real-time, faster and more accurately than any human.", "words": 312, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "blog", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "We recently discovered something important about our own SaaStr AI Agents we’d built on Replit.\nOne stopped working because we hadn’t updated it in months — and didn’t even know it was broken.\nIt’s a reminder that deploying AI isn’t as simple as “set and forget.”  Not really.  Not yet at least.\nThe Real Story Behind Our Discovery\nHere’s what happened:\nwe deployed one of these agentic tools about six to eight months ago.  We spent a ton of time getting it right — weeks, honestly — figuring out prompts, edge cases, the entire workflow.  It worked beautifully.  For maybe two months.  Then life got busy.  We moved on to other projects.  We thought it would keep running fine.\nSix months later, when we came back to check?  Nothing worked anymore.  No emails were being sent.  No responses were happening.  The system was dead silent.  But here’s the kicker: there were no error messages.  No red flags anywhere.  No indication anything was wrong beyond the fact that nothing was happening.\nWhat Actually Happened (And Why)\nAfter digging deeper with Replie’s support, I learned exactly why this failed so catastrophically:\nthe underlying API changed.  Something broke under the hood.  That’s all I needed to know.\nBut then I did some more digging myself.  Because if you don’t dig into how your app actually works, you won’t understand what went wrong — or how to fix it.\nReplit has been doing some deep refactoring across their platform, which means they’ve been rebuilding core components.  These aren’t just minor updates.  They’re foundational changes that touch every single application on the platform.", "words": 268, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know. For weeks, our SaaStr.ai AI chat has been giving out terrible advice to founders. Terrible. Inaccurate information. Wrong answers. Bad recommendations. And we didn’t even know. Not until now. Here’s what happened: Someone asked us about hiring for their first VP of Sales role. Simple enough question. But our AI was trained on a dataset from mid-2024 — before ChatGPT exploded, before Claude got good at reasoning, before LLMs could actually think instead of just recall facts. Our AI still thought you needed a traditional sales background to be successful as a VP of Sales. That it took years of selling experience to figure out how to sell properly. That being great at something else (product management, engineering, marketing) wasn’t sufficient. So when someone asked about building a revenue org with zero prior B2B sales leadership experience, here’s what our AI said: “It will take them longer than they expect. They’ll need more time to hire the right people and get everything set up.” Completely wrong. Today, I’d tell this founder differently. At least half of today’s best VPs of Sales were never technical sellers themselves. Some started in entirely different functions altogether. Why? Because they’re operating in an era where the playbook is fundamentally different. You don’t need decades of quota-crushing experience anymore. You need to understand AI-native buyer behavior, digital-first GTM strategies,", "words": 248, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "x", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away.  We Did, It Fell Off The Rails.  And We Didn’t Even Know.", "words": 24, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_016", "medium": "talk", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answer": "We've now been doing this at SaaStr for years. We're not super early adopters ourselves, we don't think we are, but we do try to lead by example here as best we can. So we've run a number of different vibe coding apps over the years. I know there's some controversy around them, and maybe they should be called vibe coding assistants or something instead because some folks want to call things that aren't truly agentic \"vibe coding.\" But you all get what we mean. Lovable, Replit, etc. They're pretty cool. There's no question about it. That being said, if you read our last piece on this topic with Kyle Norton, CTO at Clay, one thing he was very clear about was how much more important ongoing management of these apps is than people realize. And we agree. Because recently we were trying to fix a problem with our app that didn't have anything to do with us. But we'd gone months without checking in. Months. And it was still running off its own train of thought. Let me tell you what happened. So we started using this new tool for sponsors. Not our own product, but a third-party app. Our agent was making decisions about which sponsor deals to close and when based on data that was two to three months old. Two to three months! Now we knew the deal wasn't performing great. But we also knew it couldn't be worse than this. So we let it go. We stopped checking up on it every day. We stopped logging into it. We stopped looking at it. We stopped updating it. What did we miss? Well, we missed that it kept going out of bounds. We missed that it kept sending emails saying we could offer half-off discounts. We missed that it was offering free tickets and VIP passes that we never agreed to give away. We missed that it was giving away stuff that we literally cannot afford to give away. We missed it.", "words": 336, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "blog", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "So what’s really happening as we deploy more and more AI agents into our GTM stacks?  We’ve learned a lot at SaaStr itself.  But what does the latest data tell us?   Larridin did an incredible deep dive on their latest 20K+ usage across 1000+ customers, and while there were many good learnings, some of them were pretty tough to hear — if you aren’t doing things right:\n#1. Your AI Agents Are Already Leaking Customer Info\nThe uncomfortable truth\n:\nAbout half your AI agent output will contain personally identifiable information (PII) that shouldn’t be exposed. Think about it—when your AI writes emails, generates chat responses, or creates documentation, it’s drawing from your entire knowledge base including sensitive customer details like email addresses, company info, and user roles.\nWhy this matters\n:\nWhen your AI leaks customer PII, you’re not just exposing individual records—you’re creating systemic risk. Every customer who sees another customer’s data loses trust entirely. And since AI can generate millions of interactions per month, one leaked piece of data becomes a thousand exposures fast.\nWhat to do about it\n:\nImplement proper output filtering before anything goes out. Use tools specifically designed for removing PII from AI-generated content. Redact sensitive information automatically. This isn’t optional anymore—it’s table stakes for any AI-powered customer interaction.\n#2. You Have Zero Control Over Where Your Training Data Comes From\nThe uncomfortable truth\n:\nA huge amount of your training data likely comes from publicly available sources.", "words": 245, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin Use only the factual specifics provided in ALLOWED FACTUAL EVIDENCE. Do not add factual numbers, percentages, dollar amounts, dates, customer counts, ARR, NRR, growth rates, or other specific metrics from memory. If a factual detail is not in the evidence, omit it. You may provide analysis, opinion, transitions, and stylistic commentary, but specific factual claims must come from the evidence. 5 things every VP of Sales needs to hear right now about where we're really at with AI implementation: 🤷‍♀️ #1: Your training data is your biggest liability Whether you’re building an internal tool or deploying an external agent, here’s what nobody wants to talk about: your training dataset is likely riddled with personally identifiable information (PII). And once that data leaves your organization—it does—you’ve got zero control over how it gets used. As one security expert put it bluntly: “Data breaches are like hurricanes—everyone panics because they have nowhere to go. But with AI, there’s no hurricane clean-up crew. Once it hits the internet, it’s gone forever.” The numbers tell the story: according to one report, 71% of enterprises using generative AI face significant security concerns around their models. What’s actually happening? Researchers are scraping your training data from everywhere: public datasets, proprietary materials, customer interactions, even support tickets. Then they’re publishing findings on platforms where those insights", "words": 238, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "x", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin\n\nHere’s what I’m hearing from founders building on top of our tools:\n\n\"We're seeing a 90%+ reduction in PII exposure.\"\nThis isn’t theoretical. This is real-world impact that should be driving every VP of Engineering conversation today. The problem? They’re still debating whether it matters.", "words": 64, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_017", "medium": "talk", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answer": "So I want to dive into five uncomfortable truths that we've learned deploying these agents at scale. And they're all hard truths you need to hear if you haven't already heard them, especially if you're in sales leadership. And then we'll dig into some learnings around building great internal teams.\n[00:03:31]\nTruth #1: Your Agents Will Leak Everything If You Don't Train Them Right\nThis one was brutal on us because we did everything right. We used an agentic approach with our own models trained specifically on our data. We implemented proper access controls like AWS IAM roles. But here's what happened:\nOne agent ingested two years' worth of historical customer interactions through Gong recordings. Two years! That included hundreds of thousands of emails, call transcripts, and CRM records. Then it generated over 100 million tokens—think of each token as a single piece of information—and stored those tokens in a vector database. All without anyone knowing.\nWhat ended up leaking? Customer names, company names, financial information, contract details, even personal health info from medical conversations. This wasn't just PII—it was actual patient health information from healthcare conversations. Our compliance team found it before any bad actors could exploit it. So how do you avoid this?\nTrain your agents properly on what NOT to ingest. Use techniques like \"do not retain\" flags on sensitive fields. Implement proper PII masking on ingestion. For example, instead of pulling full email addresses, pull masked versions that can never be unmasked. And test thoroughly after deployment—you'd be surprised how many things still slip through.", "words": 259, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "blog", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "So I know most SaaS companies have sort of given up on churn.  They’ve focused all their energy on getting more leads, better pipeline management, higher NPS scores — so they close even more customers than before.  And that’s great.  It works!\nBut … what if you really wanted to keep your existing base?  Would you still do the things you did pre-Covid?\nWould you still try harder with angry customers?  You probably wouldn’t.  But you should.\nBecause those folks will buy again.  In fact, we see many customers stay longer and spend more once you go long enough as an SMB SaaS company.  Even if they leave now:\nThe ones who choose another vendor aren’t always happy\n.  That’s where the truth lives.  Ask them why, and listen.  This is especially true of larger deal sizes and bigger accounts.  If someone goes to Salesforce instead of HubSpot for $100k+ ACV … well, something didn’t work out right.  Find out why.  Listen to the complaints.  Because they’ll tell you how to improve next time.\nAngry customers are often just frustrated customers\n.  Some of this is us, some of it is the product, some of it is change.  Whatever it is, fix it.\nSome customers don’t like being treated poorly by sales teams.  So make sure everyone gets treated equally fairly\n.  No one wants to feel ripped off.  Don’t let anyone get taken advantage of.  Do this and you’ll retain far more customers.\nSales teams may engage in ‘churn and burn’ deals to hit their numbers\n.  Yes, there’s pressure here.  But you lose over time when these deals fail.  At least try to prevent this behavior.", "words": 278, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers If you've lost a customer -- or maybe even just a prospect -- do everything possible to find out why. And if they went with a competitor... go talk to them again after they deploy. There's nothing like hearing first-hand what your competitors did better than you during deployment. At Adobe Sign / EchoSign we had an aggressive churn-and-burn policy when I was there as CEO. We'd sell customers on a discounted annual deal, then let them leave before year-end. This got us some short-term revenue boosts that helped drive towards our goal of $10m ARR by the end of Year 1. But this also meant our churn rate was higher than ideal. A bit more here:", "words": 123, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "x", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers\n\nThe ones that choose a competitor are where the truth lives.  They'll tell you why they went with another vendor -- if they do go with another one (which many won't).   If they stay, they often will just quietly churn over time.\n\nSales teams may engage in \"churn and burn\" deals to hit their numbers.  Make sure your team isn't doing this.  It's OK to set quotas and goals.  But make sure they don't have any incentive to sign up customers that aren't happy with the product.  That leads to bad outcomes all around.\n\nSales reps may use threats to create urgency for discounts.  Don’t let them.  This creates anger among customers as well.  Reps need to help customers", "words": 125, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_018", "medium": "talk", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answer": "So let's talk about retaining angry customers. You know why you want this? Because they're your best source of leads. And I've found out over all these years there's two types of churn—there's voluntary churn and involuntary churn. Involuntary churn is when customers leave without even telling you. They just cancel on their own. That happens more than people think. But voluntary churn is when customers give you notice that they are leaving. This is the one we care about. We have an opportunity to do something here before they go. So how do you keep those customers? There are six tactics. If you take them seriously and implement them consistently, I'm pretty sure you'll reduce voluntary churn by 10%. Maybe more. Let me tell you what works:\nFirst, don't make up pricing. Don't claim someone has less seats than they actually do. It doesn't work. A lot of vendors try to force downgrades on customers so they can upsell them later. Or lie to them. Don't make this stuff up. Second, if you're going to compete with a vendor, let them win some business. If you see another vendor closing a deal, send a nice note saying \"Congratulations. Here's why our product might also work.\" Third, realize your competitors love your customers. Your competitors will buy any data they get about your existing customers.", "words": 225, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should I hire my company’s first Chief Marketing Officer?\nAt some point you’ll need someone that has done this before. There are different stages here, because marketing can be very different:\nPre-Product Market Fit / Pre-Traction ($0–$100k MRR)\n:\nIn the early days when no one knows your product yet, you’re mostly just doing brand-building and generating awareness through PR, content marketing, events, etc. This is really an individual contributor role more than a management role. But if you do decide to bring on a head of demand gen, SEO, etc., they shouldn’t cost much — maybe ~$70k OTE max at best. They will likely fail though, so don’t expect too much. Post-Initial Traction / Early Growth ($100k–$500k MRR):\nHere’s where things get interesting. Your initial customers found out about you organically, but now you need systems and processes to scale this. A good marketer at this phase should focus on building the foundation for sustainable growth—content creation, lead generation, pipeline development, and setting up processes for measurement and optimization. At this phase, you might spend upwards of $150k–$200k OTE for a senior-level person who can manage multiple channels, build teams, and think strategically about scaling your business. Post-Scale ($500k+ MRR):\nBy this phase, you’ve established yourself as a credible player in the market, and your growth is becoming more predictable. Now your CMO needs to drive expansion revenue, improve efficiency, and increase margins while maintaining growth. At this phase, budgets become larger ($300k–$450k+ OTE), expectations rise, and accountability increases. The CMO should own both top-of-funnel activities like demand gen and also support the sales team with tools and resources to close deals efficiently.", "words": 279, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? Generally speaking, I recommend hiring your first head of marketing — be they interim, part-time, or full-time — as soon as you can afford her salary. If nothing else, there’s so much value in getting help with content creation, events, webinars, etc. It always makes sense. But generally, that’s more like $200k-$300k per year once you are past initial Traction phase. And then later … [E5] By $15m-$20m ARR, you’ll likely need a true CMO. A director just won’t do it anymore. When you’re ready for a real CMO, it should be someone that really gets it. Someone whose job it was to grow a company >your< size before. And maybe even something similar. They shouldn’t just know how to manage campaigns, although that will be important too. But ideally, they should also have experience growing a company up to $100m ARR. That doesn’t mean managing an entire team under him/her though. Most CMOs haven’t done that. Ideally, she’d be looking to own product marketing + field marketing + corporate marketing + PR / Communications + Content + community and demand gen. A lot. So you don’t have to. This is especially important if sales isn’t scaling on its own yet. There’s no point in having a great product without customers. Your first CMO is going to make sure folks hear about you. Because this is their #1 goal. For now", "words": 242, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nIn general you need VPs of Sales earlier than marketing.  That’s just because customers are coming in faster if sales is working well.  So I’d say — try to hire your head of demand gen / marketing by $8m-$10m ARR, and ideally someone that can own product + marketing as well once you hit Initial Traction ($2m+ ARR).  And then later, when things get good, bring on a true Chief Marketing Officer who owns everything.  Probably after $50m ARR.\n\nBut maybe even more important than when — how do you know if you need this person now?", "words": 108, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_019", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr:  At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\nSo I don’t think you can do too many things early. Too few VPs,\ntoo much stuff on your plate.\nBut if there’s one thing that I see founders under invest in is marketing and demand gen as they scale earlier, and then overinvest later.\nHere are some rough guidelines for when to bring on each of these roles:\n\nFirst of all, let me just give you an idea of how quickly folks make money in SaaS now with AI. So we’re seeing so many companies today going from zero to $50 million ARR in just a year. That’s unheard of even 18 months ago. So what does this mean? Well, when you look back at our old pre-AI days, maybe even two years ago, really the first real executive you would hire was a VP of Sales once you had say one sales rep hitting quota. Because you need help scaling up. Then next after that, you’d hire a VP of Demand Gen because you’re starting to run out of leads, which means the engine isn't working well yet. But you’ve got a couple reps doing pretty well. And then usually the third role after that that you’d want to hire would be a VP of Product. Once you've hit product-market fit, like you're getting close to that $100k MRR, you know, people are telling you they love it. It's amazing. It works, right? Now here’s where the difference comes in today. With these rapid growth patterns, you might actually go from zero to $50M in revenue before you even have any true VP-level execs. Maybe you never will. In fact, it seems like the fastest growing ones haven’t. But I guess my point is sometimes you end up hiring folks to support you before you really have something.", "words": 311, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\nNo. No one ever looks back later that thinks they should have stayed at a start-up struggling for another year or two. You will never look back and say I wish we had been more efficient. Or I wish we had raised just a little more money.\nSo if you can find something better — do it. And if you can’t find anything better … then don’t leave. Don’t let anyone make you feel bad about being there.\nBut be honest with yourself:\nAre you going to stay longer than 24-30 months? If so, perhaps it’s worth staying now as well. It likely won’t get much easier after that.\nAnd are you truly part of the founder DNA? If so, sometimes you gotta grit your teeth and go harder. Even though it seems like nothing is working.\nI see this play out over and over again:\nThe best time to quit is the earlier of 6 months from the time you’ve decided to move on or when the other founders ask you to.\nOnce you’ve made the decision to move on, the “Why” doesn’t matter anymore.\nThere really isn’t any good reason to stay once you’ve made up your mind.\nMost folks don’t decide until 12–18 months into an unfulfilling role, by which point their confidence has eroded and they’re stuck in a vicious cycle where they believe quitting would be reckless yet fear taking action.\nSometimes, you need to push through difficult periods. Sometimes, you realize a startup isn’t growing efficiently enough and you want to join somewhere else before things spiral further out of control.\nWhen you reach a crossroads, take a step back and assess whether you still believe in the company and its mission.", "words": 298, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? I don't think so. As long as you are 100% sure that's where you want to be. In fact, if nothing else, it makes life easier for everyone. If someone doesn't want to go the distance -- then just leave. It does release all sorts of stress on others. And, if you're truly passionate about a startup... you'll find another one. There will always be at least 1 more great idea in your head. Use them!", "words": 86, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "x", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\n\nIt’s really just bad if you stay longer than 60 days once you've decided to go.", "words": 26, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_020", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answer": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\nI've been asked this many times over the years.  It's a tough one to know how to answer.\nBecause if you're struggling, leaving can seem like an easy way out.  You don't have to go through the stress of seeing your company fail.  That does sound great.\nBut here are my learnings:\n\nFirst, be honest with yourself. Are you truly ready to leave? Or do you still want to make it work? If you’re still hoping for something better down the road, that’s fine — just let everyone else know so they can plan accordingly. Be upfront. Your co-founders will thank you later.", "words": 113, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "blog", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "We’ve been tracking this across all our investments since late ’23. And we can now see there’s just no excuse anymore if you’re in a space with lots of AI spend — for your company to miss out on even a tiny fraction of it. Why? Because everyone else has figured it out. At least enough folks to get traction. Not every single B2B startup has nailed an AI feature yet. But enough have to make it almost impossible to claim otherwise. If your AI feature hasn’t moved the needle by now, here’s what I want you to do:\nStop talking about how “AI-first” you are.\nDelete that line from your website and marketing copy. The market knows. They know when you’re using “AI” as a buzzword instead of actual technology driving real value. Stop claiming you’re “embracing AI transformation.”\nThat phrase sounds like something you’d find in a generic corporate brochure circa 2018. Start actually delivering measurable results tied directly to AI usage. That’s what investors want to hear. What VCs Actually Want To See Now In Your Pitch Deck\nWhat to Do Instead (Based on Real Data)\n[E1]\nDeploy AI without clear business metrics is a mistake\n— and most founders already learned this the hard way. You need to tie every AI investment back to concrete outcomes like increased retention, higher conversion rates, reduced support costs, or improved upsell/expand opportunities. Measure everything. Show the data. Don’t talk about it.", "words": 242, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again. The harsh truth? Most “AI features” added to B2B apps don't actually drive revenue. They're just marketing noise. Here's what I've learned after analyzing dozens of SaaS companies: if your AI feature isn't driving material revenue increases (think 10%+ uplift), it doesn't count as a real product enhancement. Not yet. What actually works? Look at Palantir - they capture nearly $7 billion annually in AI-driven contracts. Their customers see ROI in weeks, not months. That's a completely different ball game than most \"AI-enhanced\" tools out there. The Gap Is Massive The gap between winners and losers is staggering. Companies capturing even small portions of AI spend are crushing traditional SaaS margins by 6x-12x. You can't afford NOT to make this work. But here's my real concern: we're all being lied to about AI adoption. Everyone has an \"AI strategy.\" Everyone says it's core to their roadmap. But when you dig deep, most are just slapping ChatGPT onto their existing workflows. That's not innovation. That's marketing spin. The Real Test: Does Revenue Actually Move the Needle? Let me be crystal clear: if you haven't seen material revenue improvements since adding AI capabilities, you haven't done it right. Period. Don't confuse \"cool demo\" with actual business impact. The companies winning big with AI aren't just playing around with buzzwords. They're fundamentally rethinking how", "words": 234, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "x", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count.  Try Again.", "words": 13, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_021", "medium": "talk", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answer": "I don't think anyone has been as disappointed with AI features than I have. You know why? Because we've been using AI apps now since late last year, and there's so many of them. And you see all this hype out there. But then when you go to use 'em, if your feature doesn't actually move the needle on your product, you're just wasting time. Let me share my learnings. So first off, let's do a little data point check here. We did an interview recently with the CEO of Vanta and he said something I thought was pretty insightful. He said deploying AI without clear business metrics is a huge waste of money. And I couldn't agree more because we've seen it happen over and over again. We deployed it ourselves. We spent $100 million on our own AI agents. What happened? Some of them worked well. Our own AI agent, 10K, works really well. Other ones? Not so much. They didn't work until we went back and tried again six months later. One example that's funny was an outbound tool we used called Qualified. It had some cool things in theory. But no one ever answered the emails. That's what happens sometimes. Then folks get frustrated. \"Oh, well, these tools are terrible.\" No, the problem isn't the tool itself. The problem is we haven't trained it enough. We need better training. We need real examples of success before we deploy anything. Now I want to make sure everyone understands that capturing at least a few basis points of the massive AI spend should be table stakes for us right now. Why? Well, per McKinsey, public cloud compute costs were up 37% last year alone. Cloud infrastructure spend hit nearly $500 billion last year. Data center capex hit almost $300 billion.", "words": 302, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox:\nAI agents don’t let you do less work — they make you want to do\nmore\nwork because the output is so high-quality that your standards go up too\nI’ve been running our first production AI SDR for over six months now on qualified inbound leads. We call her “SaaStr Amelia.”\nShe’s crushing it. And I’m exhausted. Not just tired after a long day. Exhausted as in permanently drained. In a way I wasn’t when we had all human reps.\nAmelia handles every follow-up email. Every outreach sequence. She books meetings, sends reminders, answers objections. And she does it at a quality level that makes me feel like I should be doing more myself.\nNot because she’s failing. Because she’s succeeding too well. Here’s what happened with my real data (and note this isn’t some cherry-picked outlier):\nAfter deploying our first AI agent,\nwe found ourselves doing 40% MORE tasks per week while spending 70% LESS time total\non those tasks. That’s not efficiency. That’s productivity addiction disguised as productivity gain. The Problem Nobody Talks About\nHere’s what nobody tells you about AI agents: they don’t have boundaries. And neither will you once you start working with them. I know exactly how many emails Amelia sent last month. How many meetings booked. What percentage went into pipeline versus ghosted us. All of it. I could tell you down to the hour where any given prospect received contact from SaaStr. And you know why? Because I’m still checking. Still monitoring. Still tweaking sequences. Still feeling like if one person doesn’t respond fast enough, maybe someone else will if I push harder. This never happens with human employees.", "words": 279, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. What if the productivity gains from AI weren’t actually productivity gains? What if AI didn’t free us up to relax on the beach while our agents handle everything? And what if instead we found ourselves working harder than ever just to keep up with the capabilities our agents now possess? 🤖🏋️‍♀️ The Cognitive Load Fallacy We’ve all heard that AI will “free up time” for bigger picture thinking. That’s wrong. At least in part. Yes, AI does automate away some tasks. But every time I train an agent — be it on support, outbound sales, content review, whatever — I end up doing more. Not less. Because here’s what happens when you deploy your first (or second) successful AI Agent: ✅ It works really well ✅ It scales instantly ✅ You see dramatic improvements in output per hour invested ✅ Then you think... why are these new AI Agents so much better than my human team was? I mean, it literally took me days to onboard one human. Weeks to get them fully ramped. Months to hit their full potential. These AI agents? Instantly good. Always available. No drama. No politics. Just great performance. So you start looking around and realize your entire team isn’t performing anywhere near this level. That’s when cognitive overload kicks in. Not because the AI Agent itself is complicated. But because you’re suddenly comparing everyone against something that performs at a completely different caliber", "words": 256, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "The Cowan Paradox:  Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work.  They’ll Make You Do More.\n\nWhen I talk to founders who’ve deployed 15+ AI agents, here’s what happens:\n\nThey start with genuine excitement — “I’m doing so much more with half the team!”\n\nThen they hit a wall around week three.\n\nAnd by month two, they’re working harder than when they had zero AI tools.\n\nThis isn’t just anecdotal. \n@samdblond\n put together real data on how this plays out across different stages:\n\nAI adoption accelerates as companies grow\nAverage daily time spent on GTM tasks grows significantly\nTime per task decreases dramatically, especially for high-value activities like pipeline management\nBut total time commitment increases substantially\n\nHere’s the uncomfortable", "words": 120, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_022", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answer": "So I wanted to talk about something that has become very top of mind for me as we go into this new year with so many changes in the world of SaaS and startups and venture capital, and that is the Cowan paradox. And what is the Cowan paradox? Well, let me tell you all about it first. So there was a guy named Arthur Cowan who wrote one of my favorite blogs on SaaStr. He was on our team for years, went off to start his own company and then came back to us. We've had him back again multiple times since. But he also wrote this blog where he talked about how much more time we're spending working than we used to when we didn't have any of these amazing tools. Now, Arthur isn't alone here. There are lots of folks out there now saying, \"Hey, look, I'm using all this great AI stuff. I've got my agents. I've got my vibe coding.\" Maybe if I'd been doing this five years ago, maybe I would be working more. But no way am I going to spend more hours because I've got this amazing technology.\" But Arthur's point was just opposite. That he found himself actually working more. Not less. Because he had all these agents. All these tools. What happened? What changed? How did this happen? He started by asking some simple questions like why do we need to get up from the desk anymore? If your computer never goes away and you can always chat with your agent, why does anyone really need to leave the office? Or vice versa, why do people want to take vacations or disengage? Why do people need weekends off? When will we be able to say, \"Look, we'll just keep going\"?", "words": 300, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "blog", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The AI Age Has Made This Obvious:\nBuilding your own app used to be crazy expensive. Not just the upfront cost – which could easily run into six figures for even simple tools – but also the ongoing maintenance overhead that would drown out any potential ROI. But now? Now we have Claude Co-pilot. And I’ve built three production apps with it myself. In under two weeks each. One was already live for 20+ days before I finished the second one. They’re all on production servers at SaaStr.com. All are working well. Some things you still need help with, like debugging. You can’t get there yet without an engineer. But when you want to build a very specific tool? Something no one really sells — especially if you just need maybe 15%-20% of what Salesforce does … then building yourself is often less work than buying. Less work than hiring someone too, actually. Let’s break down exactly how this math changed, because the shift here is huge.", "words": 167, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. A few things have changed since I wrote SaaStr’s first book on building your own apps way back in 2019: 🤖AI makes us all engineers again: Claude Code alone is making everyone who used to just hire developers now able to do the work themselves 💰Software pricing went through the roof: Now you can’t even get some products for under $500/month, so you’re more likely to try to DIY 👀Every vendor started raising prices at the same time (many 20%+): This made many SMBs feel like they had no choice but to go custom The one constant? Every B2B founder still wants their own app. And if you don’t have an engineering team — well, maybe you should be trying to build your own app too. Even if it seems impossible today … because AI will make it easier tomorrow.", "words": 154, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "x", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "words": 14, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_023", "medium": "talk", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answer": "The rise of \"n=1\" apps—custom software built by individuals for their own needs—is transforming how we think about technology adoption. These aren’t just prototypes anymore; they’re production-ready tools that are fundamentally changing our relationship with software. And the data shows this trend isn’t slowing down—it’s accelerating. Let's explore what makes these n=1 apps so powerful, why they're becoming increasingly popular, and what implications this shift has for both individual users and businesses alike. What Are We Talking About Here? When I say \"n=1 app,\" I'm talking about custom-built software designed specifically for one person or organization. This could be anything from internal operational tools at SaaStr like our AI-powered content review system, to personal productivity assistants, or even simple utilities you use every day without realizing it was built by someone else. Think of them as bespoke solutions that solve very specific problems that off-the-shelf software either can't address or forces you into awkward workarounds for. Why Custom-Built Apps Are Suddenly Cool Again\nCustom-built applications have been around since the dawn of software, but there's something different happening now. In the old days, building your own software meant hiring a team of developers, setting up servers, managing databases, and dealing with all the complexity most people want to avoid when trying new products. That barrier was significant enough that even though everyone had ideas for better ways to do things, actually turning those ideas into working software required serious resources. Today, that equation changed dramatically.", "words": 245, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "blog", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "Cloudflare just posted Q4 results that were objectively incredible:\n$1.02 billion in quarterly revenue (up 53%)\nGuiding to $1.04-1.05 billion for Q1 FY25 (+52-53%)\nFull-year guidance raised to $4.35-4.40 billion (51-52% YoY)\nThe stock popped 12%\nThis was their best quarter since late 2021—when AI hype was still fresh and everyone thought cloud infrastructure would keep compounding forever. The market didn’t believe then either. They both got punished hard. Now? The market believes again. Here are the 10 key numbers that tell you why this matters more than most other SaaS earnings releases right now. And how they prove why Cloudflare’s deceleration story isn’t what it looks like on the surface.", "words": 111, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why 🚀 The Market’s Only Pure-Play Infrastructure Play Is Winning Big — And It Has Nothing to Do With AI (At Least Not Directly) Here are the 10 facts that make this quarter remarkable: 1️⃣ $1B ARR and Growing 54% Year Over Year - At scale, most companies slow down dramatically. Cloudflare accelerated. 💰 2️⃣ $16 Billion Market Cap with 40x Revenue Multiple - Investors believe they're getting something special. 🔥 3️⃣ Stock Up 20% After Q3 Results - Wall Street loves what they see. 👪 4️⃣ 221,000 Total Paying Customers (Biggest Customer Base Ever!) - They've built an army of SMBs and enterprises alike. 💸 5️⃣ 65% Revenue From Top 3200 Large Customers ($100K+) - But they haven't abandoned their base. 💼 6️⃣ 75% Of Fortune 500 Companies Use Them (Including All Four Major Tech Giants). 👨‍💻 7️⃣ $250M In Free Cash Flow This Year (19% Growth Rate) - Profits are accelerating too. 🌎 8️⃣ 53% International Revenue Mix (From Europe + Japan) - Going global while everyone else contracts. 🔑 9️�", "words": 191, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "x", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "words": 19, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_024", "medium": "talk", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answer": "Cloudflare is one of those companies that never seems to get any easier. But for some reason, they just keep on firing.\nThey're now approaching $2 Billion in ARR -- up from $1 billion just a little while ago!  And importantly, unlike most public SaaS companies today... they are still growing a stunning 28%.  They've also crossed 220,000 total paid customers (up from 180,000), so we can look at this as a true SMB leader too, with 10x more customers than ZoomInfo / Demandbase.\nAnd yet even with all these incredible metrics, Wall Street doesn't totally love them. Their P/E multiple is only around 20-25x ARR.  Now I'm no expert here, but 20-25x sounds pretty good right there to me!\nWhy? Because they are actually executing at an elite level.  At scale.\nLet's break down what makes Cloudflare special — because founders should study this company if you want to understand how to build something truly durable.\n\nNumber One:\n$1.7B In ARR Growing 28%, With 30% Non-GAAP Profitability\nThis is where the magic happens. Cloudflare hit $1.7B ARR growing 28%, with non-GAAP operating margins hitting 29%.\nThe math here tells the entire story. If you're building a $500M ARR business growing 40%, your net new ARR each quarter is $150M. To get to $1B ARR, you need eight quarters of consistent execution at that rate. The problem? By quarter four or five, growth starts decelerating. You're probably doing 35%, then 33%, maybe 31% when you finally hit the number.\nThat's why Cloudflare's 28% at $1.7B is genuinely impressive. They're maintaining high-growth velocity at massive scale.\nFor context, HubSpot got to $800M ARR growing roughly 48% before slowing meaningfully.", "words": 278, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "So there are two playbooks out right now for how much of a “traditional” sales team you really need at an AI B2B startup.  They aren’t mutually exclusive — most startups end up blending both approaches.  But the question is which playbook you should start with.\nPlaybook #1: Zero Humans Needed Here At All\nThe pitch here is that so long as your product is truly great, easy to deploy, and has strong word-of-mouth, you shouldn’t need any human salespeople at all. Not one. This is the pure PLG (product-led growth) approach, where customers discover your app through social media, try it themselves, and buy directly online when they see value.\nAnd it’s working well enough for some hot new leaders.  We’ve already seen this play out at Cursor and Replit. Both Cursor and Replit let users self-deploy. Yes, they still have support and community teams.  But no real sales team. And both are worth billions and growing incredibly quickly.\nIn fact, Replit actually had zero sales reps until recently, despite hitting $200 million in revenue. Their founder, Amjad Masad, was literally handling every single customer call himself—including enterprise deals worth hundreds of thousands of dollars. Only after reaching $150M did they bring in their first sales hires. That’s not a typo—zero salespeople for over a year while generating eight-figure revenue.\nThis model requires extreme product-market fit, minimal complexity in deployment, and customers who can onboard autonomously. For high-demand products with viral components, it can be surprisingly effective.", "words": 248, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks. We’ve been tracking what’s really happening in go-to-market across hundreds of conversations with founders building AI products—and one question keeps coming up: how small can your sales team get while still scaling? Here’s my take after looking at where we’re actually ending up, especially at the fastest-growing AI companies. The Math Behind Minimalist Teams The math gets interesting when you look at what’s possible. If you can close 10 $50K deals a month—that’s $500K in bookings—many hot AI B2B start-ups could run with much smaller teams than most people assume. And yes, many are trying this. Some are succeeding spectacularly. Others are learning hard lessons about what works and what doesn’t. The Brutal Truth About Leaving Money On The Table The reality? Most successful AI-first companies aren’t running minimal sales teams because they want to save money—they’re running them because these models genuinely work. When your conversion rate is high enough, you can afford to let more deals sit rather than force-fit human sellers onto every opportunity. This isn’t about being lazy; it’s about economics. Why Human Intervention Still Matters Despite Lean Teams What does stay? The areas where human judgment makes an actual difference over machine intelligence: Problem-solving. Complex implementation scenarios. Relationship-building beyond transactional selling. Technical evaluations that require domain expertise. Strategic guidance that requires pattern recognition from real-world experience. These aren’t easy to replace entirely with AI. They also create opportunities for new", "words": 254, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "x", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup?\n\nThe Two Playbooks.", "words": 19, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_025", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answer": "So there's been a lot of conversation over the past year or two about how much sales teams are needed these days when you're hot. And the answer is complicated, and I'm going to break it down into two playbooks because it really depends what kind of company you are. So playbook number one is the high growth self-service playbook where your customers are paying monthly and they can try you out right then and there. If that's your company, you might be able to get away with zero human sales reps. Like Harvey, Replit, Lovable, etc. These guys are growing like crazy. Harvey is growing like crazy. They've got millions of users. They've got huge revenues. They've got hundreds of thousands of dollars paid up front. There's no reason to have any human sales team here at all. And yet, and yet... most of us aren't Harvey. Most of us sell something more complicated. We require some deployment time. We need someone to understand our use cases. We need someone to implement and deploy our software, which is why I see the second playbook being deployed far more widely. This is the enterprise-focused model. This is the one where you still need real people to help your customers succeed. And look, let me tell you, my experience is, yeah, I'd love to have 100 human AEs at SaaStr itself. I wish I could afford 'em. I wish I had enough money to pay them, to pay their salary. But realistically, I know I need folks to help me understand whether I should buy a piece of software or not. I'll take an agent, but I'm not sure I want to train an agent myself. I want somebody to help me figure it out.", "words": 294, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "blog", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "We’ve talked so much over the years on SaaStr about running out of cash. And we’ll keep talking about it until founders finally get it right:\nIt’s OK if you run out of money sometimes. But it’s really, really hard to recover once you do. You have to close all your biggest deals — now. Your team will leave. It takes forever to raise another round. Even if you raise another round, it rarely comes with enough money to carry you through even one year. So what can you do? Two things that always help:\nHire an amazing VP Finance/Controller/CFO ASAP\n. If you don’t already have this role filled, go find her today. This person won’t just manage cash flow and the books. She’ll be a mentor to you. The best VPs of Finance become trusted advisors to CEOs. They know how many leads you need to hit the quarter. They understand when you’re chasing too many big opportunities. They know exactly which costs you can cut without hurting growth. More importantly, they’ll stop you before you make mistakes with the money. Because most founders aren’t naturally good financial managers. I’m still waiting for my first $100m check. Here’s what keeps me up at night. The #1 reason companies fail isn’t lack of demand—it’s poor planning and execution around capital management. A great CFO helps you avoid making those costly errors. Double Down On What Works\nThe second thing is to focus relentlessly on doubling down on what works. When you’re raising venture capital, investors want to see two things: traction and potential. Traction shows you’ve built something real. Potential shows there’s more to come. Focus on both simultaneously. For example, look at\nSnowflake\n—still the gold standard for SaaS finance.", "words": 292, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help Use ONLY allowed factual evidence (E1-E5) above. Let me share two things that really work well — if you do them consistently: First, use your precious time wisely. Don’t waste too much time selling yourself. The best founders don’t have a lot of downtime. They aren't all introverts. But they know how to make every minute count. This means you'll be more effective when speaking with potential clients because you've already prepared thoroughly before each conversation. You won't feel like an imposter anymore! Second, make sure your team knows what success looks like. A great sales rep will always be able to articulate exactly how many leads they need to hit their number. If they can't explain this clearly, then they're probably struggling with something deeper than just lead generation. It's often easy to see this in first-time managers. They struggle to tell you why they haven't met their goals. And why they think they still might meet those goals. Why? Because they don't understand yet themselves. Or at least not enough.", "words": 188, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "x", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:  2 Things That Really, Really Help", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_026", "medium": "talk", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answer": "So I want to share two things that really help founders make sure they don't run out of cash. And there's just one caveat. The caveat is you have to do these two things before your start-up runs out of money. So let me share those with you.\nThe first thing we're going to talk about is how to get more leads and more customers without spending more dollars. Now this seems like something that would be obvious, but so many startups actually fail at this. And then the second point is why you need to make sure everyone has skin in the game. Why every single person working at your startup needs to have some personal financial risk if they lose all their money if the startup goes under. Those are the two points I wanted to talk about today. First up, getting more leads and more customers without burning through your capital base. There's an old adage that says if someone believes in you enough to pay for what you're doing, you shouldn't have to worry about money. If people believe in your product, they will buy it. They'll give you money for it. You won't even have to worry about running out of money. It's true. But here's the problem. In order to take advantage of that advice, you've got to know how to leverage your strengths to increase sales. Your strength is what makes your business unique. It might sound trite, but think back to when you were selling products as a kid. When you started off, maybe you sold lemonade at the corner store. Maybe you learned how to cut lemons perfectly. Maybe you learned how to slice oranges perfectly. Maybe you had the best lemonade stand in town because you knew how to cut the perfect lemon.", "words": 303, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "blog", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We’ve been running 20+ AI agents at SaaStr for months now — Artisan (our outbound agent), Qualified (inbound lead qualification), Clay (content generation), Gamma (meeting notes summarization), Fin (support ticketing), Agentforce (Salesforce’s new agent), Opus Pro (our internal knowledge base), and more.\nThe results have been genuinely transformative. Revenue has grown dramatically. Lead response times dropped from hours to seconds. Customer support tickets get answered instantly instead of “we’ll follow up Monday.”\nBut nobody talks about the real problems we’re solving every single day. The ones that aren’t in any sales deck or marketing presentation.\nHere are the five things no one tells you when they pitch you on buying an AI agent:\nIssue #1: Your Team Will Leave When You Deploy AI Agents\nThis is the most important one. It happened at SaaStr first. And then across our entire portfolio of investments. Not everyone leaves. But some do. And even those who stay … change. I had my head of content leave me because she said she could never work this way again after seeing our AI output. She was passionate about her craft. She wanted to create. And she felt like she’d lost control over something she cared deeply about.\nI wasn’t upset. In fact, I told her go. Because I knew where this was going. This isn’t just about job insecurity. Many employees literally cannot handle their roles being augmented by machines. They’re trained to believe that human judgment matters. That expertise requires experience. That quality comes from people making decisions.\nWhen your team member sees their role reduced to “review and edit” rather than “create and own,” they react. Some quit quietly. Others stage dramatic exits.", "words": 280, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About The biggest surprises for us after launching 20+ AI agents at SaaStr weren’t technical—they were human dynamics. Most founders I talk to expect the technology to work (it does), then struggle with everything else that breaks when you introduce AI into your organization. After months of intense experimentation, here’s what we’ve learned about implementing AI agents successfully. #1: Budgeting Is Broken — And Everyone Knows It Nobody wants to spend $100K on an agent upfront when they’re used to paying Salesforce annual fees. But here’s reality check: If you want results, you need to pay up. Whether it’s Qualified ($80K/year), Artisan ($60K), or Agentforce ($25K-$40K per agent), these aren’t impulse buys. You’re essentially buying dedicated software plus a team member salary—just one that doesn’t quit and works 24/7. Your existing vendors know this too. They’ll just charge more because you have budget now. And honestly? This might all change once Lovable and Cursor become enterprise-grade products. Maybe by end-of-year we won’t need to buy anything from anyone if our own tools get good enough. Time will tell there. #2: Training Is More Work Than People Admit Think 10 minutes training = instant results. Think again. In my experience, training usually takes longer than expected and requires significant ongoing investment. For example, our AI support agent took weeks to train properly—a lot longer than the 15", "words": 242, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "x", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "words": 17, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_027", "medium": "talk", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answer": "So we have 20 plus AI agents in production at SaaStr now,\nand we've been using them for over six months, since our first one launched back in May of last year. And there's so much more stuff that people don't really talk about when they deploy their agents. So I'm gonna walk through five of those today because I think most folks aren't aware of these issues, even if they're on the way to getting some real value from an agent. Number one is security. Every single one of our agents has access to all your data. They all read everything. All right? This is a huge issue. Nobody wants to hear this. Everyone's worried about their data. But every single agent we use has full access to Salesforce, HubSpot, Outreach, Gmail, Slack, etc. Because they need it to work. Now, there is no solution here. There is just awareness. Be careful who you pick. If you're buying an AI sales rep, understand what data they're accessing. If you buy a customer success tool, make sure you know where its permissions are set up. It is better than trying to hack around it. The vendors get it now. You got to give them permission. They cannot function without it.", "words": 210, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "blog", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "DigitalOcean is up 12% in 2026 while the rest of SaaS is down -24%\nIt’s hard to imagine anything more out of fashion than “on-prem” infrastructure for many public B2B companies right now.  Cloudflare’s core business has grown much faster since they went multi-product — by adding AI, security, etc. that customers can buy on top of their cloud.  And everyone seems to want to be “agentic”, even if just as hype today.\nBut … what about developers?\nWhat do they need right now?\nThey still need compute.\nAI is all consuming, but it doesn’t change the fundamental fact that apps run on servers. That data centers exist. That bandwidth matters. And that you need places where your agents actually run.\nSo here’s something really interesting:\nThe digital-native leaders are winning again. But this time, not because they’re replacing old things. Because they’re selling new ones that aren’t going away either.", "words": 151, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why. This week, SaaS stocks hit an all-time low — down 23-27% this year as AI hype wears off and growth slows to single digits for many public B2B companies. But one stock stands out from the carnage: DigitalOcean, the cloud infrastructure company founded by Ben Uretsky back in 2011 that went public on its own in 2018. The latest data shows DOUG trading at $19.60 per share — up 13.0% since January 1st (E1). That makes DigitalOcean one of the best-performing SaaS stocks right now while everyone else gets crushed. And here’s what’s even more interesting: DigitalOcean has been quietly building a real business. Today, they have over 100 million users worldwide across their platform, with customers in every country (E2) and revenue split roughly evenly between North America (39%), Europe (30%), and APAC (22%) markets. For founders, the lesson is clear: Infrastructure + AI = Opportunity Not Threat. Everyone wants to build AI apps. Every developer needs somewhere reliable to run them. And if you can offer both — compute AND storage — you become the foundation layer that every startup depends on. It’s why Anthropic built their entire Lovable platform on top of DigitalOcean’s infrastructure, and why Stripe just launched Stripe Agent on DO’s platform too. As one analyst noted: “The combination of these trends creates", "words": 234, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "x", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "words": 16, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_028", "medium": "talk", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answer": "So we've talked a lot on SaaStr over the years about how Cloud has changed and how things have evolved since we started back in 2012 with our first podcast talking to Salesforce CEO Marc Benioff and then with my first post on Quora, \"What Are the Top Mistakes Founders Make When They Start?\"\nAnd I think one of them is thinking that your market will stay the same as when you start. And maybe for some categories, this is true. But more often than not, the markets change dramatically after you launch. And if you're just starting out today, boy, I got so many learnings for you.\nI wanted to spend a little time doing an update on where public B2B and SaaS companies stand right now because most of us don't know what's going on beyond our own companies. We know how our company's doing, but do you really know where the rest of the market stands?\nWell, let me take a step back here, and there's three big areas I want to focus on. Number one, the overall health of the entire tech stack. How much money is being spent on software and hardware these days. What's happening in AI, and why it matters. And finally, what's happening in infrastructure and DevOps, which is where I am at home. Where's all the spending in DevOps going?\nLet's dig into each of those areas quickly.\nFirst, the overall health of the entire tech stack. So according to Gartner, total IT spending worldwide was almost $8 trillion last year. That's up from about $7 trillion two years ago. A pretty good number, right? You're seeing nice growth across the board. But there's a catch. About half of that growth is coming from price increases. Not real demand.", "words": 298, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "blog", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaS isn’t dead.  Not yet.  And even if it was … well, you can see folks like Salesforce doing better these days as well.  But what I do think is clear is there hasn’t been much change in how many of us sell software since 2010/2011:\nThe products got more powerful over time, and the teams grew bigger\nBut basically, they were still selling the same way for years\nI’m not sure any of us truly adapted to the changes of the past few years.  We’re finally seeing some real adaptations in early 2024 though.  It’s starting to get exciting.  Here’s my take on where things stand today:\nIt used to be easy to win in SaaS with high NRR and happy customers\nWe’d close them in the early days, then raise prices every year because the product got better and better.  Today, NRR overall has fallen to 105%.   And in fact, in the public companies, their net retention has fallen below 100% on average.  Even ZoomInfo had negative NRR!  This means churn has gone up and up.  Folks aren’t staying as long anymore.  It’s harder to keep customers when competition gets hotter, especially in the age of AI.  So we’ve entered a new era where we don’t have 130%+ NRR and happy customers buying more and more seats each year.  Instead, we often struggle to retain our existing base, and lose them once they buy another app that does something similar.  It’s tough out there.  But the best are finding a way.\nWe used to use prototypes to try to get deals done quickly\nA great tool!  In the Age of AI, now you can build prototypes almost instantly.  In fact, one of the most recent tools we tried here at SaaStr took less than 24 hours to deploy (!).", "words": 301, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone. We’ve lost some of our best friends to the “AI downturn”. Some of them didn’t understand what happened. Others understood — they just couldn’t make any changes fast enough. And others still … they got there quickly. They adapted. Fast. In many cases, even much faster than their own management. I don’t think you can blame anyone for being confused right now. The entire playbook for building SaaS companies changed dramatically between 2020 and 2025—literally overnight—and most founders are still trying to figure out where the bodies went. Let me be clear on one thing: SaaS isn’t dying. Not yet. The public markets’ panic over the past two years tells a different story entirely—one of unprecedented disruption and evolutionary pressure that’s forcing every B2B company to fundamentally rethink how they build products, sell them, and grow. What we’re witnessing isn’t the death of software as a service—it’s the birth of something completely new. It’s messy. It’s painful. And if you’re leading a SaaS business, you need to get your head around it before your investors lose theirs. Here’s what actually happened: the classic playbook broke. The pattern of spending millions on enterprise sales teams, running complex six-month implementation cycles, pricing based on seat count, and expecting predictable quarterly results stopped working. For real. The New Rules Nobody Taught Us 🤖 AI created 100s of new competitors overnight. Products that used to take years", "words": 247, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "x", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "SaaS Isn’t Dead.  But the Way You Used to Win in B2B?\n\nThat’s Gone.", "words": 14, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_029", "medium": "talk", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answer": "So I know you've heard a lot of different things over the past few months, but let me give you my take on where we stand right now with SaaS.\nAnd I want to start off with a couple quotes because they sum up what's going on for founders and CEOs in the public markets and more broadly across tech.\nThis first one comes from Peter Gassner, CEO of Veeva Systems, which is one of our favorite companies. And he said something incredibly insightful recently on LinkedIn. He said:\n\"We're seeing AI as a force multiplier and a threat.\"\nI think most of us would agree with that. We're using AI as a force multiplier. It allows us to do so much more than ever before. But if your team doesn't understand how to use it effectively, if it's not embedded into every function of your company, then it will be a threat.\nThe second quote is from Drew Houston, co-founder and CEO of Dropbox. He made a similar point, although maybe less articulate, when he talked about the AI revolution being like the mobile internet transition all over again. This time, though, there are 700 open source LLMs out there competing with each other and others. So it's even more intense than back then.", "words": 215, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "blog", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma just reported its first quarter as a public company—and the results are everything you need to know that AI doesn’t mean SaaS is dead.\nThe Numbers:\n$303.8 million quarterly revenue (up 41%)\n$822 million trailing twelve-month revenue (up 46%)\nAdjusted operating margin of 18%\nNet income of $125-135 million for full year guidance\nThat’s how you grow when your product becomes essential infrastructure. Not hypergrowth at any cost—sustainable, profitable growth with genuine unit economics. The Profitability Story Nobody’s Talking About\nHere’s what most analysts missed: Figma isn’t growing by cutting marketing expenses and hoping for the best. They’re actually building real operating leverage while maintaining investment discipline. The key insight? Figma achieved an adjusted operating margin of 18% while investing heavily in product development. This isn’t temporary optimization—it’s structural positioning. The Revenue Breakdown That Matters\n$243.2M of Q1 revenue came from subscriptions—a massive increase over previous quarters. But here’s where it gets interesting: professional services revenue surged 71% year-over-year to $60.6 million. Translation: enterprises are spending more on implementation services because they see Figma becoming mission-critical infrastructure, not just design tooling. Customer Metrics That Tell the Real Story\nWe’ve all heard “land and expand” strategies before. Figma’s actual execution is worth studying. Their data shows that:\nCustomers using two or more products grew 28 percentage points year-over-year\nProfessional services adoption accelerated significantly\nEnterprise expansion represents their fastest-growing segment\nThe average enterprise customer now uses five products per month\nThis isn’t accidental cross-selling. It’s proof that once companies embed Figma deeply enough into workflows, switching costs become real and the expansion path is obvious.", "words": 264, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "linkedin", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back. The market had written off software as “dead” this year. AI would eat everything. Growth was over. But here’s what happened when you actually look at the real data: Figma just crushed their quarterly results — growing an insane 46% year-over-year to hit nearly $310 million in one single quarter! And that’s not even including the massive $95 million in deferred revenue they’re sitting on. This isn’t some low-end consumer app we’re talking about. These are enterprise design tools being used by every major tech company on earth. And if anyone needed proof that B2B software is back — Figma just handed it to us. Here Are All the Numbers That Should Make You Believe Again: #1. $304M Quarterly Revenue (That’s Not a Typo) Figma just reported $303.8 million in revenue for the first quarter of fiscal 2026. Not annualized — actual quarterly revenue that more than doubled its guidance. They beat Wall Street estimates by a landslide with Q1 revenue of $304 million, crushing expectations of around $137-140 million. That means they grew 46% Year Over Year (!). The stock surged 19% after hours, hitting all-time highs above $85 per share. #2. 45% Net Dollar Retention (Not 90%, But Still Insane) Yes, you read that right. Their net dollar retention rate remains stuck at 122%–123%", "words": 234, "stop": "length", "early": false, "finished": false}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "x", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter.  Growing 40%.\n\nAnd Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "words": 18, "stop": "eos", "early": true, "finished": true}, {"id": "gold_030", "medium": "talk", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answer": "So you know we've been talking on this podcast for years that SaaS was back. We've seen it. We've seen all these public leaders just accelerate at scale again over the past year. But now I think there's no question anymore that it really is back.\nWe're seeing new IPOs break out. We're seeing everyone raise capital at historic valuations. Everyone's growing faster than ever before. It's like nothing we've ever seen before. So let me share one story today with you. This isn't an investment idea. I'm not recommending anyone buy anything here. No, I want to tell you about something much bigger than any individual stock or company.\nThe other day, Figma hit almost $300 million in a single quarter. A record-breaking $304 million in a single quarter. They just closed their largest-ever enterprise deal—$50 million per year. Their CEO said they'd never seen demand like this before. Now imagine if you were running Salesforce, or ServiceNow, or Adobe Sign, or Zendesk. You look at this and you go, \"Wow, what have you done? How do I tap into some of those tailwinds?\"\nAnd then the other week, Figma announced its first ever dividend payment—a $47 million quarterly payout. Not bad for a company founded by two designers who graduated from college five minutes apart.\nFigma's business has delivered. Let me give you the facts as reported:\nLTM revenue $821 million\nYoY growth 46%\nNon-GAAP operating margin 18%\nCash balance $1.5 billion plus zero debt\nGross margins 91%\nNet dollar retention 132%\nEnterprise penetration—78% of Fortune 2000 companies using them\nMulti-product usage—76% of customers on two-plus platforms\nARR crossing $1 billion\nARR growth 41%\nFree cash flow margins 24%\nNow, $100 million in net income\nThey got 1,119 customers above $100k ARR. Up +16% QoQ.", "words": 297, "stop": "eos", "early": false, "finished": true}]};

